      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>ni 4,9 e Backache nil e J Kidney lILLS</head>
        <p>ni 4,9 e Backache nil e J Kidney lILLS</p>
        <p>NEW POULTRY.</p>
        <p>Fresh Varieties that have Proved Very Disappointing. HOUSING FOWLS IN WINTER.</p>
        <p>It must be confessed, says a writer in "Farm and Home," that the crop of new breeds within the last few years has been singularly disap- pointing. Original and distinctive types have been conspicuous by their absence;and in their place we have been given a large number of plagiarisms and variations which suggest that the modern fancier is in too much of a hurry to prod.uce something really good. True, there have been attempts to secure sonic original combinations in colour and marking; and in some cases these are still being prosecuted; but the fact that so many new breeds have been put before the public long before' they are ready for distribution not only detracts from their value, but damages their prospects of popularity. ,</p>
        <p>• It is an unfortunate state of affairs when we find new varieties being boomed for the benefit of their producers without regard to the inter- ests of the general public, and in such circum- stances it is a pity that no authority possesses the power to classify and value such introduc- lions, as new plants are classified and valued by horticultural societies. It remains for the public to be on their guard against new produc- tions that have not had time to receive a fair and exhaustive trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WINTER HOUSING.</head>
        <p>WINTER HOUSING.</p>
        <p>' The hot weather that has been. enjoyed during the past summer, has led tOSyst6ins of housing that left little to be desired, but the time is draw- ing near when the houses should,pe overlooked with a view to alterations or repair that may be necessary to secure- adequate warmth 'and shelter against the cold nights and mornings that may be expected any time from now on- wards. Chickens reared during a dry and hot season are less able to resist cold than are those that have been reared under less favourable conditions. , The open-fronted house has now become very favoured, and when they are pro- perly constructed on a sound common-sense plan, one can quite 'understand their popularity since they possess many distinct advantages over the old closed up, stuffy, and badly ventil- ated habitations. There are many open-fronted houses that have rendered excellent service in the summer, but are quite unsuitable for the cold weather. On the other hand there are 11 Open-fronted houses which are , adaptable for winter, and can be made equally as good as they are during hot weather:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>VALUE OF STRAIN:</head>
        <p>THE VALUE OF STRAIN:</p>
        <p>To those commencing poultry-keeping I can- not too strongly emphasise, says "E. 'l'. "ii " Farm and Home," the necessity of securil, stuck from a good strain, whether exhibition ~r utility. People are often advised to keep a cer- tain breed, and many jump to the conclusion that they will be perfectly sltfe so long as they keep representatives of that breed, no matter Where they come from or what they cost. 'Now- adays, however, there is a very great difference between certain strains of the same breed, or rather between strains that 'deserve the' name, and those that do not. The efforts of specialist breeders in raising the standard of productive- 'miss among many pure breeds of /ioultry should be more extensively recognised, and beginners will make a. safe start by securing stock from -s,iene of these strains. The initial expense will lte greater, but them can be no doubt that in a year or two's time the well-bred froductive strains pay for their extra cost many times over.</p>
        <p>NOT A DESIRABLE FOOD, I think that we arc quite correct in saying</p>
        <p>that practically every authority . agrees- that Indian corn, Or maize, is not the most desirable food, and its excessive Use is generally con- demned. At the same time, it is quite as gener- ally agreed that, given in-the right proportion, and at the right time of the year, it is excellent. In accepting this statement, which applies to the general run of circumstancese there are, however, places where much latitude may be allowed, and it is here where discretion should play a very prominent part. - If, for. example, the fowls are kept at a high altitude and in an unsheltered place, an amount of Indian corn may be given that would be quite dangerous under more favourable conditions, since maize is of a very'heating nature—an ordinary sample contains 62 per cent. of carbohydrates—and, consequently-fowls under the former conditions are considerably assisted by this large percen- tage of heat-giving matter, whereas under the latter conditions the same amount would be quite unnecessary. .There is yet another matter to -be regarded in determining the proportion of maize that may be given advantageously, and that is the nature of the soil. 'Birds that exist on a very damp, heavy, clay soil can eliminate a much larger percentage of fat than can those. that live on a soil of a light, sandy nature, since from a sandy or gravelly. soil there arises a certain amount of heat, and this,, in Connection with the heat contained in the Indian corn, makes it,- much more injurious. Discretion in this direction counts a good deal, and one's own judgment should be exercised to know just how far such advice meets one's own case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FREEDOM EOR FOWLS.</head>
        <p>FREEDOM EOR FOWLS.</p>
        <p>There are many advantages to be derived by fowls which are intended for breeders in allow- ing them absolute freedom until the near ap- proach of the time when theyore to be mated for breeding. It is not, of curse, imperative that they should be so kept, since many exist under conditions that seem far from ideal, and yet ..results, so far as progeny are concerned, appear to be quite satisfactory. I remember once being shown a number of pullets that had been set apart for breeding from which were kept in a very small house with an open -run about nine feet square. This run had once' been laid down in grass, but at the time Of our visit it was a wet, slimy mass of impurity, and the pullets living on this .unsavoury mess were ulti- mately to breed chickens. 'A YeAr afterwards I was shown another generation Of pullets, and they, in turn, were occupying the same run as had previously been used by their progenitors. I Was confronted by /this disconcerting chat- lenge,." What ails these?" It certainly was a difficulty to. say with any degree of certainty what was wrong. There was apparently noth- ing the matter with them. The fact, however, remains that it is infinitely better to allow them freedom from m all restriction until the time arrives when all their reserve force can be • utilised in the direction for which fowls are , destined. The case above quoted was probably the exception that proved the rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</head>
