      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>LIVE STOCK DISEASES.</p>
        <p>The returns for the week show that there were nine outbreaks of anthrax, as against 10, 19, and 33 in the three preceding years; the total since the opening of , the year (40 weeks) is 420, as against 604 in 1912, 667 in 1911,. and 1,114 in 1910. Of glanders there were no outbreaks in the week, as against 2; 3 and 11; the toil since the beginning of the year is 122, as,against 141, 156, and 299. Of parasitic mange there were 15 outbreaks ;,the number in the corresponding week of 1912 was 19; the total for the 40 weeks is 2,018, as against 2,431 last year. There were two sheep scab outbreaks; the numbers in the three preceding years were 3,2, I and 4; the total since January is 136, as against 1180, 313, and 3* Swine fever outbreaks num- bered 36, as against 26, 51, and 24; the total since the beginning of the year is 1,904, as against 2,371, 1,972, and 1,114. Anthrax is re- ported from 6 English and 2 Scottish, counties; swine fever from 18 English. 1 Welsh, and 1 Scottish, counties; sheep scab from 1 English and 1 Welsh counties; parasitic mange from 8 English and 1 Welsh counties. The number of bovine animals suffering from tuberce;osis of the udder, tuberculosis with ema.2iiii;ion, or giving tuberculous milk, in reSpeet ci which notice of intention to slaughter has betni re- ceived in England, Scotland and frellid, W 35 678.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>BUSK OH 'MOSE IN CALVES.</head>
        <p>BUSK OH 'MOSE IN CALVES.</p>
        <p>_ " Husk" or " hoose " is a parasitic disease which attacks calves, and sometimes lambs. The disease is caused by small thread-like worms in the windpipe. Whether the worms enter the windpipe direct, or first enter the stomach with the grass and then work_ their way back up the gullet and into the windpipe, is not known. , That they are present in the windpipe of affected animals, however, is cer- which tain. Their presence there sets up an irritation. animalCworms faeuedse,sbytt,iseu,ckitg l the a lowering of condition naturally follows from the coughing and loss of blood. The chief symptom of attack is a hard, husky The animal becomes dull and listless and rapidly loses condition, and the skin be- comes dry and hard. These symptoms are usually -noticeable in the months of August, September .and • October, and unless remedial measures are promptly adopted death may result. In most case's the animals attacked have. been kept 'under one or more of the fol- lowing conditions :—(a) The calves have been grazing during the late summer and auturrin on strong, wet, or low-lying, marshy-, undrained land. (b) The calves have been allowed to lie out at nights during autumn, and have thus become chilled by the Cold, damp atmosphere so characteristic of autumn evenings. (c) The calves may have been starved through lack of the good nourishing food so ,necessaey for young growing animals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WISE PRECAUTIONS.</head>
        <p>WISE PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>,It will be readily understood therefore that prevention of the disease can best be effected by attention to the following precautions :—(a) Calves should not be allowed to graze on wet Or damp ground. The driest field and the finest and freShest herbage should be reserved for them., The application", in early winter, of either a medium dressing of lime, or of from 8 cwt. to 10 cwt. of salt per statute acre is often beneficial on,land where calves. are subject to the disease. Where possible calves shOuld be changed frequently to fresffpasture. (b):Calves should not be allowed to lie out at nights dur- ing autumn, but should be comfortably housed. (c) Calves should receive good, wholesome food, such as hay.. and a little linseed ,cake. . Those that are well housed and welt fed rarely suffei-- frOm husk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HOW TO TREAT TIIE DISEASE.</head>
        <p>HOW TO TREAT TIIE DISEASE.</p>
        <p>If the -proper steps be taken „qIA SOon as the symptonis of husk are obse'rved, a cure may be effected, but it cannot be too strongly insisted that every day that passes between the appear- ance of the symptoniS and the adoption of reme- dial measures. renders the chance of successful treatment more -remote, because the victim loses strength.so rapidly that parasites soon get the upper hand. The Irish Department of Agri- culture have found the following treatment effective :--dive once daily to each affected calf. according to size arid age, from one to two tablespoonfuls of a mixture composed of: 1 drachm oil of cloves, 8 oz. spirits of turpentine, 240 Z. linseed oil. The dose should be given alone and before the animal is fed, and may be repeated daily for four or live days. More dras- tic methods of curing the disease by intra- tracheal injections and by fumigation are some- times adopted; _these, however, are delicate operations, and should not be undertaken except by qualified veterinary surgeons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Lift and divide clumps of Chrysanthemum maximum or Shasta Daisy. _ _</p>
        <p>..... _ Plant evergreens--Hollies, Laurels, Box. Sonie of the summer bedding plants should now be lifted and placed in a.cool house. Cannas and Salvia patens may be left out until.cut back by frost. Arabis makes a good groundwork for bulbs of sorts,.such as scarlet, red, and blue flowers. Take cuttings of Pentstemons; and insert in sandy soil in a frame. . Lift Potatoes, dry and store in a cool place. Lift Carrots and store in sand.</p>
        <p>When lifting Beet, twist off the tops„ not cut. • Collect all garden foliage, 'Stems, hedge cut- tings, leaves, &amp;c., as soon as dry burn.. them, and save the ashes for use in the spring. Valu- able food will thus be supplied to the land. . Iceland Poppies and Spanish Irises make a good do4ble, and may be planted now. Plant Spring Cabbage, utilising ground that has been previously well manured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LONG-FLOWERING PERENNIAL.</head>
        <p>A LONG-FLOWERING PERENNIAL.</p>
        <p>Anchusa italica (Drop-nose variety) Is con- sidered by many to be the best of the blue- flowering plants. A succession of flowers is maintained for three or four months, com- mencing in May. The readiest means of pro- pagation is by root Cuttings, which should be two to three inches in length. In cutting a root to pieces, cut the top straight across and the bottom slanting. Plant in a cold frame for safety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MANURES FOR PRESENT USE.</head>
        <p>MANURES FOR PRESENT USE.</p>
        <p>Basic Slag, which contains phosphates, should be used to soils deficient in lime. It is slow acting, and should be applied to the soil as soon as vacant, Use about six ounces per square yard, and dig it into the soil.</p>
        <p>_ Lime counteracts acidity in the soil 'caused by over-manuring. It sweetens the soil and keeps it in good "heart." Lime lightens heavy soils, and helps to consolidate light soils. Use ground lime, that is, unslaked limestone ground fine, at the'rate of four ounces each square yard. If slaked lime is used Six ounces to the same area will be required. Kainit contains common salt, magnesium salts, and about a quarter of its mass uS SUl- phate of potash. It is best used on a light soil, and if applied in the autumn or winter will kill many injurious insects and Weeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>STORING BEET.</head>
        <p>STORING BEET.</p>
        <p>Beetroot should now be lifted and stored, and if good coloured roots are expected, careful at- tention should be.given. to this work, says C. Hive. in "The Smallholder." In digging up tilt: roots, avoid breaking the long tap-roots and do not bruise thetn in any way. The outer leaves only shnuld be carefully broken off .after lifting. Remove the beet to a cool shed and store them there in fine soil, keeping them in layers, with the tops outwards. A covering of litter, such as ferns or straw, should be placed over them to keep the air front them. A too damp atmosphere will cause the top of the roots to 'rot. Where' shed' room is scarce beet may he clamped, outside in dry soil in a gheltered spit, but they must be .well protected against rains and severe weather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NUTS 81 SLACK, WASHED</head>
        <p>NUTS 81 SLACK, WASHED</p>
        <p>NUTS, BEANS 81 PEAS</p>
        <p>CROMPTON &amp; SHAWCROSS LTD., Colliery Proprietors, DALLAM LANE, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>and at Car Terminus, Latchford. All inquiries to F. W. PRAEGER, District Agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
