      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>rttterS ta the Oita</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE EDITOR of the "Examiner" invites corre- spondence on all subjects of public interest.</p>
        <p>LETTERS should be brief and ,pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. •</p>
        <p>LETTgus must in all cases be accompanied by the</p>
        <p>name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GRAPPEN HALL SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>GRAPPEN HALL SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The thanks of every elector in Grappen- halt is due to Mr. Lister Kaye for the able and .consistent manner in which he has opposed the original and extravagant scheme of the Run- corn Council, and held up to the ridicule of every business man the methods by which they work. That perfect outsiders should have the absolute say in a matter of this importance is neither proper nor right. Mr. Pilling, the chairman of the Sewerage Sub-committee, may say that Grappenhall started the thing, but will he say if he ever opposed it anywhere, or in any form? As soon as the chance presented itself, the electors of Grappenhall showed what they thought of the scheme. I notice the letter of "Vigilant," "and what Mr. Hatton said to the ratepayers at: Thelwall," but if the, electors of Latchford Without and Thelwall 'are satisfied, as they appear to be, with their representatives, after what they have done and said, then serve them right whatever may be the result.• If compulsory, ...pgwers are applied for, and obtained, to enable, the authorities to get the land which they were said to have purchased for the outlet- of the sewer, where Will be the original estimate of £18,500? And what of the gentlemen who rule the ,Council and the pur- chase of „land, without a-right of way to it? To build a Ilciue without a doorway by which to enter Obalis vdlums Concerning the Runcorn Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>BATEPAYEn</p>
        <p>Grappentiall</p>
        <p>elicits a„ polite acknowledgment, but very sel- dom any tangible iesults. An Anti-Smoke League would have more strength and influence than the individual, because of its being the organised protest of the population. Instead of the individual making a protest to-a strong wrong-doers he could make his complaint to the League, who, being stronger than the individual, could deal with more effect.</p>
        <p>LABOR OM NIA VINCIT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SALE'OF DRINK TO YOUNG PEOPLE</head>
        <p>SALE'OF DRINK TO YOUNG PEOPLE</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. It. B. Batty, honorary secretary', of the United Kingdom Alliance, has made, in The Contemporary Review,- an impressive appeal for It short Bill ,prohibiting the sale of intbxicating drink to those under 18 years of age. It is such a moderate proposal, and would effect. such salutary ad far-reaching conse- quenees that , ono earnestly hopes that such a Bill will be pasSed without" delay.• But while greatly desire to see such a Bill promoted I must frankly confess that I have .no great ex- pectation of seeing it passed just now, and that for the follow,ing reason. When the Scottish Temperance Bill was before life House .of Com- mons' Committee in June last an amendment in the following terms was moved • by Mr. J. M. Hogge, one of the honorary secretaries .of the Temperance Legislation League: - • "From the passing Of this Act every certificate granted by any Licensing Court shall be subject to the con- ditions that no exciseabte liquors shall be sup- plied for consumption on the premises to any per- son under eighteen years of age." That was a recent and an excellent opportunity for securing quell a reform. There are over '7O members on the committee, but 0n1y.19 voted on that amend- ment, and of the 19 only four voted in favour of it, viz., Dr. Chapple, Mr., Arthur Sherwell, Mr.' A. F. 'Whyte, and Mr. Hogge. Several Liberals and Temperance Reformers, e.g.. Mr. McKinnon Wood, the Lord Advocate; and Mr.' Charles Roberts, votM against the amendment. It may also be stated that such a regulation as is proposed 11. Mr. Batty has been in force for many years in the public houses in _Scandi- navia. Controlled by the. Disinterested Manage- ment Comfmnies, and works admirably. " The law in Sweden 'enacts that no one. under 15 be served, but the Companies have impOSed on themselves a self-denying ordinance, making 18 the age limit at which one may be served. These- facts I venture to think, show the im- mense advantage of placing our liquor traffic under what is 'known as the Gothenburg System. REFORMER. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SMOKE PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE SMOKE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As a workman, I think the formation of an Anti-Smoke League or Smoke Abatement Society, will fill a great want and do much good for the town. The congregation of atoms of thought into a body, and using that volume of toree in conSidering an eVil,-aiid alSo.trying to eradicate or mitigate that evil, must tend to- wards the communal well-being, and doing what is commonly termed good. For after all, the small 'words, good and evil, cover prac- tically the whole activities of life. Tohn Ruskili described god Wand evil as wealth and ilth, and I think there will be very little disagreement if we term smoke as evil or ilth.,</p>
        <p>I think next to poverty and slum r wettings, the most foul exCresence from modern society is smoke. The cummunal energy has been so much devoted to production that every result- ant evil protruding therefrom seems to have been considered as notning so long as we have learned to produce more and still more.. But I feel confident in saying that an evil such as smoke, which does .so much against our health and happiness, cannot be allowed to exist for ever, if there is a.way to„aboliSh or mitigate it. I /think one of the most gruesOme sights that one could wish to see is to gaze down upon Warrington from any surrounding prothThence, such as Hill- Cliffe, on any day of the town's activities. Certainly the most notable feature is the great pall of thick smoke slowly rising from the dimly discernable mass of buildings in, the valley below. It needs a strong Wort, of the mind to irmigine that it is at all ,possible for thousands of human beings to be spending their lives under such horribly 'black conditions. In spite of medical evidence to the contrary,' feel of 'the opinion that nothing clSe but, ilth clin come out of smoke.</p>
        <p>arn.Pne of thOse unorthodox persons whrn. believe tliaife is mainly a chemiCal prbeess; ,In order to hitvg health arab happiness, we must have etwiiiiCal .:,-'eoriditions that comply with nature's requiretnents ane', natures requirements is Am re Besides pure rood, in order-to have pure blood to build heartily bone and intwle and create a clear brain, the ..body requires ire unadulter- ated air: Can any one say that ttal- inhabitants of Warrington get a copious 5up1)1.7...kf pure bracing air? I think not ! ‘`.N. And then again, since the lendency of then and-women is to live in communities. and sine communities. same a•s individuals, want some- thing more than working and eating, we find that smoke is again ,an impediment. Most people desire to see \viva William Morris de- sired to see—more of the country brought into the town, and 1110 re of the town br_ought into the country. Since the same laws of health govern plants, trees and flowers, as human beings, we find it practu!ally an impossibility to introduce any decent quantity of cbutitry into the town. The plants and flowers and trees that do manage to survive are the same as the human beings. weak, sickly and degenerate. nese things, I think, hardly ample wiil con- tradict. And I also think there will be little disagreement that smoke is a great factor in colitributing to these cuuses. The duly of an Anti-smokc League or Smoke ,thatement Society would be to protect as far as possible the general community from any ex- cessive quantity of smoke " being emitted amongst the inhabirimts of the town, and to had Out if it is possible to make the abolition of smoke a realised fact. The immediate duty would be, I think, to find; out how far the law controlied the smoke nuis- ance, and bring pressure to- bear upon local authorities to put that law into operation, and bring to the notice of the general public any new appliances that will fulfil the present need,- and that would create a less amount or abso- lutely no smOke whateyer.</p>
        <p>The individual at present has no effective means of . bringing any complaint to notice A letter written 1.)1/4v a private individual to a big firm or to some property owner generalTy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IRISH DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,-4 regret that so much time and space must be wasted in. convincing Ireland's so,called friends, but really her enemies, of the futility of their ideas and arguments. We are not discus- sing whether • there was any mud-throwing at the last General Election. What is under dis- cussion is whether Home Rule, Under its pre- sent or any other form, is good for Ireland. Let me inform " r.w.n." and all his confreres that I hold no brief for any. political party, nor do I belong to any political party or association, but am, in my own tumble way, endeavouring to show- what is best for Ireland in particular, and the Empire in general. Let me also point out to him that Mr. Lecky's 'history and the might be Royal Commission on Orangeism of 1857 are both very old and most decidedly out of date for the present-day discussion of Irish affairs. shortly, A great deal. of. water has flowed under War- ringtonand Bridge since the date when these works were 'issued. ' Their have been many. changes, stated,in iy , aabnsdolwuthtealyt rfnailgshet nhoax been true then My argument was most explicitly laid down y in the letteris a as f State has a right ..to, modify the . Constitution in. such a way as that taken by the present Ooyer/F. ment to pass Home Rule " , 2nd; "That the oollfowS•senesint,b`e"rf present state Of affairs in the sister island are such that no sane 'Government -would entrust it with full legislative Powers" ; 3rd, 4‘ That the powers ,to be - given, to Ireland called 'Home Rule' are inimical to the State and a menace to .the best interests of the Empire." Such Was my statement, IMO I need not repeat the argument I then advanced, but proceed to consider " F.W.R.'s " argument in his last 'reply. To begin with, " F.W,R.'s " determined denun- ciation of Orangeism and antagonism towards it is proof positive of the unwisdom -and dan,.,-IV of placing these two elements rogethtkr. It is the old story.</p>
        <p>" Fighting like devils for conciliation, And hating each other for the love of God."</p>
        <p>Why is lieo bitter•lfgainst these peoPle, Who Will undoubtedly form the almost permanent opposition in an Irish Parliament? These are the people he ought to endeavour to Conciliate: and endeavour to Show to them the folly of their opposition, 'but, instead of that, he does all he eillt to exasperate and prolong the bitterness. His references to Lecky's history and the Orange Commissioki of 185 w. are out of date. What has been done this -last fifty years for Ireland?, The. pages of our Legislature- are covered with beneficent pleasures for the good of Ire- land. My country has poured out HS treasures of gold for the amelioration of its lot. All honour to the Statesmen. who have matured add passed these measures. . . All honour to those Irishmen who have accepted and pros pered under them: I have ninny Irish friends, both Roman Catholic and Protestant. But what must We say of the 'fanatics, the men of '67 and the 'Bo's? "-F.W.R." seems to adore them so much that he singles out for his praise the three men who paid the appropriate penalty of -a dastardly murder in Manchester. -When' the olive branch of feace was held out by Lohl F. Cavendish, one of Ireland's best friends, he was, responded to with a dagger; delights to - and these are the men ." F.W.B.'! honour. He writes of a nation in despair, but \Oat are they in despair about? Is it not mostly from fear of the professional agitator? Look 'at the Irishmen about Our streets- and see if they look like an oppressed- race? Look at those in the employ of the State in various cap- acities,. and it would be difficult to find more contentment. Then, why this miserable agita- tion for Home . Rule? Why are we for ever having the same role repeated? Simply be- cause it pays to 'be an Irish. agitator. Take'. the Land League agitators--:--where are they? .-Look at the names of the then leaders, and look at their positions of to-day., .and ask them why they have abandoned or abstained from the present crisis. The cry of downtrodden Ireland is a farce, organised to provide a cheap living for a -few idle agitators. Those ,of us who are old enough to remember the Land League have a vivid recollection of the time when the hat was sent all over the world to raise funds for the agita4 tors,, and many •of those agitators are still .living--,--but where are they? They are too well circumstanced to bother their heads with the so-called wrongs of Ireland. In regard to the Hibernian Brotherhood, I am pleased to hear that the organisation is of the innocent character F.w.n.71 describes, but, I ask, why exclusively:Catholic? I may point out that the Orange Brotherhood is only a friendly society, exclusively Protestant, and always loyal to the British Crown. My friend, by quo- tations from an old Commission that sat nearly 60 years ago, has been demonstrating what a terrible organisation it is; and I dare say there are ,plenty of Orangemen who would be capable of proving ditto to the Hibernians. One fact we must bear in mind-,that the Loyal North has always been faithful to the British connec- tion. . In conclusion, I may still claim tthat my scheme of devolution and federation evolved in your issue of September 27th still holds the field as a way out of the Irish difficulty, satis- factory to' all concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PHIL SQUOD.</head>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.</p>
        <p>SPIRITISM AND ROMAN CATHOLICISM.</p>
        <p>don't think it is wise in controversy to write complacently about • other people's fallacies unless all the fats- are known. Kindly allOw me, at the outset of my .reply to the Bev. P. L. Buggins, ki say for the benefit of those who may not have had opportunities of 'informing themselves about' psychical phenomena, that, the .Psychical • liesearch Society of London has been investigating, ex- amining and comparing these things more than thirty years ,;-,that eminent .scientific Men are at the head of it; that large sums of money have been spent, and no pains have been spared.' to . weigh evidence to verify testimony and to make observations as accurate as possible. A branch of the society has done similar work in America, and M. Camille • Flanunarion, the eminent French astronomer, has conducted in- vestigations in France for a generation. So that a mass of evidence about every kind of phenomena and spiritual manifestations is already available, and Much of it may be found An:books at the Warrington Library. In my optnion, immortality has been proved as scien lilicallly as the revolution of,the earth, and we .knOir now quite sufficient about the next world to feel sure that 'everyone will have an equal chance of salvation. It will depend upon them- selves more than upon any form' of religion they have profess. It will be deeds not creeds which,will save us, raid this is a far higher con- ception of Divine justice than eternal punish- ment for sins which arc,. largely the result; of ignorance. With regard to the distinCtibn which the.nev. P. L. Buggins makes between vhat he calls ." spontaneous" and other phenomena, it has no scientific! importni ice whatever. It is an idea which Mr. Godfrey Moped put forward sonic time ago, but I don't think he convinced any- one except 1,11.0 who want an excuse for 'un- belief. and scepticism. "Spontaneous '7 is not a word which properly applies to any phenomena. ,If it is intended to mean " volun- tary, all phenonionn and manifestations are voluntary. The spirits ,Ilre not commanded or dictated to in any shape dr form, and there is nothing which compels them to manifest if they don't wish to do \so. At a seance the medium is simply expected to be calm and passive, and those Who are in charge make the best possible arrangements to secure pure air, healthy condi- tions and harmony. They obey, the Scriptural injunction, "Seek and ye shall find, knock and , it shall be opened unto you," but the door of communication cannot at present be. opened with unfailing regularity, as there are difficul- ties yet at both ends of the tunnel. With re- card to evil spirits,. there are in the next state of existence, good, bad and indifferent, as there are here, but again we have the. Scriptural in- junction, "Try the, spirits, whether they be</p>
        <p>The up-to-date method of treating constipation, approved by the leaders in medicine, is by means of lubrication. The action of pills and aperients is not natural. They do not promote normal functional activity, but instead, have an opposite effect, so that after a time their regular use becomes a necessity and leads to a harmful habit of body. Quite re-ently, an eminent physician, in search- ing for a satisfactory method of treating constipa- tion that Would have none of these harmful effects, decided in favour of purified petroleum. Unlike animal or vegetable oils, petroleum is not affected by the digestive juices, but enters the intestines unchanged, where its soothing, lubricat- ing, anti-fermentative action is exercised to the fullest extent. Because of these prdperties, petroleum is especially adapted to the treatment of constipation and of bowel disorders generally, especially when of a catarrhal, fermentative, ulcerative or tubercular nature. The best method of administering petroleum is unquestionably in the form of Angtees Emulsion, In this form, the petroleum globules are minutely subdivided and combined with the hypophosphites of lime and soda in perfect Emulsion. Angier's Emulsion is like cream in appearance and almost like cream in taste. •It mixes perfectly with water and other liquids, and can therefore be</p>
        <p>good or evil," and "By . their fruits ye shall know them."</p>
        <p>At a well-conducted seance the bad ones can be kept at a distance or kept in subjection by appropriate means, just as well as they.can be at any other gathering of friends, and this has been proved by the Psychical Research Society in connection with the sittings held with Mrs. Piper for over 20 years. Surely the scientists who have had this long experience are better judges than the Rev. P. L. Buggins as to whether the atmosphere is necessarily unwhole- some, either spiritually or otherwise. If he condemns Spiritualism because he hears of a seance here and there not being well conducted, should I be justified in 'condemning Roman Catholicism if I hear of some members of his Church misconducting themselves? Monsignor Benson continues to insinuate in his lectures that Spiritualism causes insanity, although it has been contradicted, and he has not given any proofs of it. If he really believes it, his duty, pro bong public°, is to produce evidence without delay. He says Catholics firmly-be- lieve that many of the Messages from: mediums and discarnate spirits do come from the next world, but not from the persons they were represented as' coming from, and a little while ago Monsignor Robinson said it was impossible that the figures of departed friends or relatives could appear. Yet Father Hubert and other Passionist Fathers of Ardoyne 'Monastery, said they recognised the ghost as a former member of the community who had died, and they paw it repeatedly. Let me now 'point out in conclusion how Roman Catholics, when they allow themselves to talk and act With natural freedom and escape temporarily from the mental fetters of dogma, run unwittingly against the teachings of their Church. Father Hubert and his brother priests gave to the, world voluntarily some plain, straightforward testimony, which bore the stamp of truth, and they thought that one part of it, what the ghost said, supported the doc- trine of Purgatory, but they failed to see that the other part, the recognition of the ghost's personality, refuted that doctrine and supported Spiritualism. Perhaps the Rev. P. L. Buggins now sees the point of my challenge„and will tell us whether to support dogma the Church should publicly discredit, and rebuke these Pas- siohist Fathers. I will take tinotheli. case from the .Rev. P. L. Buggins' own letter. He makes comparisons in-favour of Joan of Arc and her "spirit control." I agree with him that h'th- mediumship was or a very high order, but why did the Church of Rome treat her with such cruelty? What had that "Saviour of France" done to deserve the Church's condemnation? One of the most sublime figures in the world's history was a Spiritualist inart,7r. We claim her with pleasure: and with 'pride. and like Christ, she was rejected and betrayed by those who should have defended her.</p>
        <p>Let us have more criticisms by all means They are doing a lot of good. W. CIIIIIMES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>-_ „ •</p>
        <p>taken either undiluted 'or in water, ISM chocolate, coffee, wine or any desired beverage- It agrees so perfectly with delicate, irritable stomachs that it is often retained when the most delicately prepared food is rejected. Angier's Emulsion is the original and standard preparation of petroleum for internal use, aad it has the endorsement of the medical profession. by whom it has been largely employed forthe past twenty-one years. Angier's Emulsion not only soothes and Inb. ricates the intestinal mucous membrane, thus, increasing peristaltis and promoting normal functional activity, but it likewise arrests fe- -1 mentation, checks bacterial growth and greatir improves absorption, assimilation and nutrition- Moreover, the addition of the hypophosphites te Angier's Emulsion makes it a valuable nerve tonic, hence its efficacy in nervous disorders. more especially in the neurasthenia associated with mucous colitis and other intestinal disorders. Angier's Emulsion is sold in three sizes at 2/9 and 4/6. A free sample bottle will be sent to any address in the United Kingdom o=l receipt of 3d. for postage. Please mention this paper. The Angier Chemical Co., Ltd., 86, Road, London, E.C.</p>
