      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MAYOR THANKED.</head>
        <p>MAYOR THANKED.</p>
        <p>His Worship Advocates No Politics</p>
        <p>in the Council.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>The customary vote of thanks to the retiriPg Major was proposed at the Tuesday meeting of the Warrington Town Council by Alder Mall SMETHURST. fie spoke of the exacting work the Mayor had periormed. fie had attended 25° meetings last year and had been 80 times on the Bench.</p>
        <p>Councillor BGBERTS seconded, and the resolu- tion was supported by Aldermen Bennett, Bur- ton and• James Evans, and Councillors Pember- ton, Birtles, Wilson, Locker, Wilkinson uPd Skelland.</p>
        <p>The MAron, in reply, reviewed the events which had occurred during his three years of office. With regard to the housing question, he said three years ago he had raised the sob" jeCt of cheap houses for working men. He re- gretted that the project had got no further filo° an aspiration, especially in view of the fact that some other town had now forestalled Warring" ton in carrying our what some menibers of the Council might look upon as a dream. He still intended to try, as a "common private," 10 carry Out a. scheme of that kind.</p>
        <p>Speaking of the proposed extension f lhe Town Hall, lie was glad to think that proposal was assuming definite shape. He agreed Ivith Alderman Bennett that it would be a pitY t° interfere with the present building. If P°S- sible, he would like to see a pretty extensive addition to the present Town Hall, so as t° adommodatO the municipal offices. Anot/0 desire pf his was the abolition of party politics in niplcipal affairg He noticed that the L'ord Mayor of Liverpool expressed the opinion thfit criticism of. public affairs was impossible with- out party political divisions. The Lord Afay•or or Liverpool Was a 'greater and abler man than him, but he (Dr. Joseph had come to the ste°ll4" conclusion that political criticism within a municipal body was downright obstructioll' rather than a help to municipal work.</p>
        <p>The Sewage Scheme. Touching upon the sewage question, the Mayor said there was a great tendency to run down the pail system, and to urge the adoption of the water carriage system. Ile approved of that plan, but he, hoped that before anything was done, they would carefully consider the whole question from its foundations. Before deciding to adopt the water carriage system in its entirety, they should consider the practic- ability of. treating and purifying the results ef that System ' before the refuse was turned /hit; the river. If such a scheme had been ,-;arr' out by other municipalities, there would he much less infectious disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meetineof the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on TuesdaY, Mr. Antwiss (vice-chairman) presiding. The Chief Inspector of Factories gave notice, that the area of the -Stockport and LiverPO Districts having been altered, Mr. H. E. Brothers was now Inspector of Factories for the nevrir formed Warrington district, which comprises part of Lancashire—the Union of Warrington. the County Borough_of St. Helens, the Borough, of Widnes, the Urban District of Rainford, nu" ,the ;civil parishes of Bold, Ditton, and Windle, the Unions of Runcorn, Chester, Tarvin, Nont" wich, and Whitchurch. Dr. Adams reported cases of scarlet fever Lower Walton tone), Helsby (one), and NorleY (three); diphtheria: Stretton (one), Sutton (one), Weston (one); one case of typhoid fever, from Thelwall, and pulmonary tuberculosis - Weston (one), Stockton Heath (three), Fro&amp; 'sham (one). Other tubercular diseases: Helsby (one), Lower Walton (one). The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>SMALLEST PAINTING IN THE WORLD°. Mr. Samuel Schultz, who has a scenic rural painting studio at Wilmington, in 101: ware, has won, despite a lifetime spent in Pain, ing subjects in heroic size, the unique dist tion ot having Made the smallest landsear" painting in the world: This picture- was cuted on a grain of corn, and the, painter only now recovered it., after having lost P(15: session of it for more than forty years. 111/.‘,, ing lost track of the picture which he made 1:: 1869, when he was only 19 Years old, Seilll" decided recently to try to recover it. He adver: tised in several: foreign newspapers, with ttl.nt; result that the tiny • landscape came to hall ,rl.- its original frame a few days•ago, the patnilllr4 if! colour and line, being as sharp and clear on the day of its execution. A well-hnov,!: artist, who saw it many years ago, declared 1' Was a masterpiece of miniature painting'</p>
        <p>• „ Thomas Hall, painter, of Tarbot-street, caster,•died at Bancaster Infirmary on Saturclda: from injuries to the head. A man has bee!' iii tamed by the police in connection with death.</p>
        <p>_ FOR INDIGESTION MOTHER ~ :,. • r'• „.•;?, S •,</p>
