      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CONVICTIONS FOR DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>CONVICTIONS FOR DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. J. T. Bhys' letter appearing in your issue of the 11th inst., by drawing comparisons between the convictions for drunkenness for the years 1905 and 1912, commits the error unat students of the Blue Book from which the figures are extracted, are expressly warned 'against, by those responsible for the information, in :these words :</p>
        <p>greatest caution must be exercise" in drawing conclusions from the differences be- tween the proportions of convictions ...tor drunkenness shown, for 1905 and 1912. In the first place, the conditions of the two years may be different in innumerable respects; and, in the second place, it has often been pointed out, and must be repeated, that a rise • or drop in these figures does•notNnecessarily mean an in- crease and decrease respectively in drunken- ness. It is, particularly important to remember this in .comparison with changes in the number of licensed premises. A decrease in licences and an increase in convictions may be con- current and may be due to one and the same cause."</p>
        <p>But even accepting the conclusions which Mr. Rhys draws, irrespective of the warning given above, it might he as well to sum up the results of ,the attempt to make men sober by Act of Parliament. The Areduction in the number of convictidns works out, roughly, three convic- tions for every one licence extinguished, over a period of eig,ht•years. On the other hand;clubs' and 'club-drinking has increased enormously. Surely, .no real advantage. has been gained by closing , the police-regulated and supervised public-house in favour Of the club with un- limited hours and prutically no supervision. It. is the old story of faddist remedies being worse than the disease itself. -</p>
        <p>G. E. HOLNIE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LIVERPOOL &amp; NORTH WALES</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL &amp; NORTH WALES</p>
        <p>STEAMSHIP CO.</p>
        <p>Dividend and Bonus.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of the Liverpool and .North Wales Steamship Co., Ltd., of the 23rd year's working of the company's Steamers states :</p>
        <p>The season has been a favourable one, and the company's- various services have been satis-. factorily performed. . The increase in the number of passengers carried is no doubt owing to the favourable Weather. conditions pre- vailing during the summer, and the result of the season's work shows a considerable advance in receipts as compared with the previous year. The steamers of .the company have been well kept up, and the service has been conducted with regularity. . In -consequence of the advance in the price of 'coal and the higher rates of wages paid to the crews, the working expenses of each steamer have been largely increased, but notwithstand- ing this increased/ expenditure there is a , fair stun to the credit of the profit and loss account. After dedacting the sum of £10,446 18s. Idd. for depreciation on steamers, investments, Sze., the amount available for dividend and other pur- poses, including the, balance brought forward from last season, is 1:7,851 19s. 3d., out of which the directors recommend the payment of a dividend of 6 per. 'cent. per annum, together with a bonus of V,T .per cent. (both free of income tax), .carrying. forward a - balance of J.:3,074. 9s. 3d. The directors retiring by rota- tion are Mr. Henry Mclver and Sir R. Williams Bulkelev, Bart, Whki are eligible for re-election. The retiring aditors, Me3s,.s. Chalmers, 'Wade and Co., are al ti. eligible for iVappointmenit .</p>
        <p>Excavations in Church-street. Maryport, lyre resulted in the discovery of a Roman well 191 t deep, Sit. in diameter at the top, and containing Sit. of water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>FOOD ADULTERATION.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst Alleges Criminal Negligence.</p>
        <p>"TO WAKEN SOMEBODY UP."</p>
        <p>The question of food adulteration was raised by Councillor Broadhurst at a meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday-evening. Ile drew attention to the request made to the Local Government Board to issue regulations governing the use of boracie acid' in fdodsfuffs. He said coldly, calmly and frigidly that the Health Committee had been .criminally riegli- gent with regard to the 'adulteration' of food- stuffs in the town. ("Oh! Oh !") He was glad to hear his remark brought " Oh's !" He wanted to waken somebody up a bit. The MAYOII : I think you ought to withdraw the word "criminal" Councillor BROADHURST : If you say it ought to J)e withdrawn I .will, without any mockery, withdraw it. The Ninon: The word is most improper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>For Five Years.</head>
        <p>For Five Years.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST : For five years I have been asking the Health Committee to stir them- selves on these lines, and they. have entirely failed to do so. Why should they have waited until they received a recommendation from Southport before moving in the matter? Boracic acid had been used in foodstuffs, to lie knowledge of the committee, to an extent which in other towns had resulted in the in- fliction of fines :• _•</p>
        <p>If I cannot make- my voicetheard in the Coun- cil Chamber, he said, in the future I will speak at every street corner in Warrington until. we get pure food supplied. Alderman BURTON said the Health Committee had approached the Local Government Boarcl about the question several times, without result. I feel satisfied, he said, there is .not a town in England where the food is more pure and more free from adulteration thnn Wn-rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PARR HALL ORGAN,</head>
        <p>PARR HALL ORGAN,</p>
        <p>Is the Hall a White Elephant?</p>
        <p>TENDER APPROVED.</p>
        <p>There was a discussion at the Warrington Town Council on .Tuesday on the proposed new organ for Parr Hall, to be erected at a cost of £1,490. The General Purposes Committee recommended the acceptance of a tender for that amount.</p>
        <p>Councillor Goudn moved that the ' matter be referred back till the end' of the • financial, year. I think, he said, the Council should have time to consider the matter. ,(" Oh! Oh !") The Ninon How long has it been consid- ered?</p>
        <p>Councillor Goi:on : It has been something like the smoke. (Laughter.) It has *almost, got on our brains. The hall is nothing but a white elephant, and I think this organ ought never to be built. We have not got the sanction of the Local Government Board for the borrowing of this 'money.. The MAYOR: We want the Council's consent first. Councillor GOT7GH : The Local Government Board might refuse it. Thpre is no demand for an ' organ, except by the Musical Society, who only take the hall two or three times a year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>"IN THIS LUXURY."</head>
        <p>"IN THIS LUXURY."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I second, and say we ought to have the. position of the finances before us ,before indulging in this Iluxury. It means a Id. in the .X in the rates. Councillor WILKINSON: You have raised the rent of the hall. , Councillor DICKENSON : We have clear proof the hall is a white elephant all the lime. The TOWN CLERK: Excuse me, it is not. The accounts show a clear profit of £54 last year. Councillor DICKENSON : If that is the. case we have been led to believe that it was a, white elephant. Councillor WILKINSON said before that tender was given they Would have received the sanc- tion of the Local Government Board .to the bor- rowing of the money. If the Local Government Board did not give them power to borrow £1,600, they. would not let the contract, and they would, not have an organ. Nothing was being done that was underhand. Councillor HAWTHORN said they had had noth- ing but discord tiboat the organ so far, and he hoped that when.they did get it there would be more harmony. The existing organ was an old one when it was put in the building, and it would have been far better for a new organ, to have been built in the first instance.</p>
        <p>On a divigion,..the amendment was lost by an 'overwhelming majority., five only voting for it..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ELUSIVE MISS LENTON.</head>
        <p>THE ELUSIVE MISS LENTON.</p>
        <p>I,oul i sister of -Airs. Kendal and for- an a Iress, died on Sunday at Liverpool.</p>
        <p>E</p>
        <p>F 0</p>
        <p>Miss Lilian Lenton, who was remanded on a charge of burning the pavilion at Kew, again outwitted the police on Saturday. Since her release a fen days ago from Holloway, after hunger-striking, Miss Lenton had stayed at ill( residence of a friend in the West End. Strict watch was kept on the house by the police, but. notwithstanding, Miss Lenton managed to ge away, and her -whereabouts are not known. This is at least the third time the Suffragette liar managed to escape police vigilance.</p>
        <p>From Reynolds's Newspaper.</p>
        <p>7- -</p>
      </div>
