      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Child and the Church.</head>
        <p>The Child and the Church.</p>
        <p>Preaching on "The Child and the Church" at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday morning, the Rev. T. T. James said that many people to-day were anxious ,about the Church. There were disquieting facts in connection with church attendance and religious observance which made them pause. They should forge afresh the 'link between the child and the Church. The problem they had to solve was the identification of the child with the Church, and both should be connected from the earliest days. He commended to their notice the forma- tion of the Young Worshippers' League in con- nection with that church, which was attempting to form a stronger link than existed at present with the chursa and the school, and to per- suade them mi. and more to worship in their families.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Bold Street Wesleyan Children's _Day.</head>
        <p>Bold Street Wesleyan Children's _Day.</p>
        <p>The third Sunday in October is regarded in Wesleyan circles as a special day, of intercession for the children. On Sunday the services were therefore marked by their. careful attention to the young life of the Church. As early as 8.30 in., the morning members and teachers were gathered in prayer, asking for a blessing to rest on the efforts of the day. At the morning and afternoon sessions of the Sunday. School ad- dresses were given to the scholars. The preachers at both the chapel services dealt with topics suited to the occasion, and in the evening the Rev. H. Williamson con- ducted a prayer meeting, which was well at- tended. ' The Rev. W. A. Beckett, speaking from the words found in Hebrews ii. 1, pointed out the terrible dangers Of the neglect of‘God's revelations. God, he said; has revealed Him- self through His Son, Jesus Christ; and it be- hoves everyone to concentrate mind and will upon what God speaks. The peril of drifting away' from the* truth is ever present; and the text is intended to arrest those who have com- menced to slip, and to prevent those whose tendency it is to .pay little heed to the divine warnings.</p>
        <p>In the evening the Rev. H. Williamson con- trasted the attitudes of mind to Christ's call, as revealed in the conduct of the rich young ruler and. the Apostle Paul. Both came face to face with the stern demands of. the • Saviour. The former, unable to rise to the heights of his manhood, ended a miserable failure; the latter accepted the Master's conditions, and forever left a limitless influence upon the history of the world. The two lives illustrate exactly all that is involved in our acceptance or rejection of the appeal of Jesus Christ. Both were infinitely in- fluenced by the one decision. • Paul stepped from glory to glory; the unfortunate young ruler fell to insignificance and shame. During the service' the solo "Behold I stand at the door" was feelingly rendered by Mr. J. McArthur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Another grand meeting was held on Sunday. It was presided over by Mr. Moss, of St. Helens, secretary of a Brotherhood "there. He referred to the good work done in the many parts of the world with the Brotherhood movement, and said they are for the uplifting of ,the social, as well as the spiritual side of man. Man is a brother and needs help and sympathy, and they were the men to give it. .</p>
        <p>The Rev.,'H. Williamson,, of Bold-street, was the speaker, and spoke, upon "Manliness and the Christ."_ His text was "The people passed by and wagged their heads and railed upon Him, saying 'Art Thou the Christ?" The apostles, he said, somewhat vary in their awount of this event. The Centurion said truly: "This man is a Son of God." The Centurion had great experience in the army, and he was favourably impressed with the on of God. Christ on the Cross was an example of manliness. He gave no appeal for sympathy. Many people crave for sympathy; and are ever ready to be letting others know of their atlli6- tion. Don't be too ready to tell others of your afflictions and sorrows ;,don't trouble others too readily, as they themselves have plenty to bear. We are here not to get sympathy, but to give it; don't fail to give it.</p>
        <p>Christ never asked for sympathy. He carried His own sorrows, and with no one to help Him. Let us ever give encouragement, but never seek it. Christ had power to finish His work alone. Men give and expect in return; don't repine and regret because your efforts are not acknowledged, and say "I will do no more." This is not a good spirit. Love not to be loved again. Christ loved always, and to the end. To do good is not luxury. The man who did the most good was put upon the Cross. An entire Christian must know Christ in His suffer- ings., We delight in Him who went about doing good: Christ fought evil wherever He met with it, and worked for men's souls. They must be wholehearted, fight against evil anywhere, and everywhere; let self-denial have it place; be men and serve God.</p>
        <p>Mr. William ,Gleave read . the lesson. The Secretary led in special prayer for the many now suffering by bereavement by sea, land and air. A special collection. was taken for the suffering women and children connected with the Dublin strike, and .21 is. 6d. was realised and handed Over to the Mayor, to be forwarded to a special fund for .that purpose. Miss Woodland sang sweetly ". Rock of ages" and "Entreat me not to leave Thee," .which were greatly appreciated. Mr. Payton presided at the organ. The Rev. T. T. James closet' the meeting with prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Tragic Week. The Rev. T. T. Jamts and the Beauty of</head>
        <p>A Tragic Week. The Rev. T. T. Jamts and the Beauty of</p>
        <p>S if-Sacrifice.</p>
        <p>"This has been a, week of tragedy which has stirred our souls.. It is seldom that within .the compass of a few .days the catastrophe" and calamity of human life are brought home to us with such telling force. We have almost shrunk from /opening the papers lest more bad news had arrived."</p>
        <p>Such were the words of the Rev. T. T. Jams, who preached on " A week of tragedy" at Wycliffe Congregational 'Church on Sunday evening. Continuing, he said that hardly had the first horror of the burning Volturno broken in upon us than in our own homeland the news was flashed of the grim terror that had stricken the Welsh coal mine, imprisoning hundreds of their fellows in a living grave, and plunging a multitude of women and children into the -sudden. horror of a blighted home. It was but the flight of hours -before the gaunt spectre .of pain and sorrow-knocked at our very doors, for just outside Liverpool the desperate uncertainty of life was again demonstrated. • Our German neighbours had been through the darkness, for the finest of the Zeppelin fleet had been blown to atoms, with the instant plunge of all the :yretv into eternity. It was strange indeed if the stories of disaster did not make us feel-in stronger reality our membership of one an- other. Passing wonderful it was how sometimes .:;ometiting happened which lifted for Us the veil from off the commonplace, and we saw the heroism of the Common task. Our whole indus- trial life, beneath its sordidness, its apparent lack of romance and beauty, revealed on its other side the beauty of sacrifice. . It was not on the battlefield alone men risked their lives; it was in the common round ot'littmdrum duty and of daily' work. Men went out • every day with their lives in their hands in order that the needs of their fellows might be met. Did we realise hoiv we entrusted to the. very humblest members of the community sometimes the keep- ing of our lives and the lives of those we loved? The signalman at the Central Station, Liver- pool, with a frankness that was commended by the inspector, explained that the accident on the Cheshire Lines Railway was due tO a mis- a ke on his part. Then it was the failure of the human element which-sent the train to disaster and brought human lives to detail. But what witness to the loyalty and faithfulness and con- sistency of the human element the history of our huge travelling system was. ITere were men, of humble station to whom was given cold ion! ally the guarding and keeping of human I.‘ es. Continuing„ Mr. James said.: " We have been travelling to and fro for many years, some- times for continuous days we have made con- secutive journeys in this country, and :o-night Ive pay our homage of honour and respect to the</p>
        <p>brave men who fail but seldom in the right Ler- formancie of their onerous task. I wish v. e all did our work as faithfully and with as few mis- takes. When we think of the previous f, eight , of human life entrusted to the workers of this country, we ought to be ready -to say that Lot one of them shall suffer from overwork or underpay." The rescues from the Volturno wreck revealed the use of scientific knowledge for the highest purposes and noblest ends. There was one question that had yet to be fought out in our modern world It was the,spirit in which our mastery of the great elemental forces was to be used, and the end to which it was to be directed. Was our•genius to be turned ever to the manu- facture of more destructive engines of war, more terrible implements of destruction; or, seized and enthused ,by an opposite principle, were we to give ourselves to ministries of mutual help and co-operation? In the vision of the line of vessels, members of different nationalities, manned with crews speaking diverse- tongues and born under different skies, standing side by side in a mission of co-operation and mutual helpfulness, he besought them to catch the ideal of the future, the future when alone God •it ould be satisffed and Christ would be King. The inspiration of seeing nations gathered in common service and in co-operative helpfulness, must speak to them of the day when racial an- tagonisms should be vanished out of sight, and national hatreds and feuds beThuried for ern.- more. It might be that it was far, far off as yet; it might be that many suns should rise and set before the Kingdom of God was here, but. near or far, no man who had caught the heavenly vision could ever cease to wait its coming. P _</p>
        <p>lie:wanted to pay his debt of :honour to the father's love. The thing that sent many a man to his post of •danger and kept him there was the love of the little ones at home</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Canon Stevenson and the Disasters.</head>
        <p>Canon Stevenson and the Disasters.</p>
        <p>Canon Stevenson, at the close of his sermon at the Parish Church on 'Sunday evening, alluded to the terrible disasters which had occurred during the past week, one of which had come very near to them in Warrington. . He said that they had sent a thrill of sym- pathy through the length and breadth of our land. These • sudden and unexpected deaths should have their lesson in warning all of us to be ready when the time came for us to cross the river and enter the Better Land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian ,Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian ,Church.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Foreign Mission work of the Presbyterian Church of England, mainly in China, the annual missionary sermons were preached on Sunday at St. John's by the Rev. William Reid, and special offerings were made. In the evening the ground covered •by the preacher was the controversial area ,provided by the Book of Jonah. After making reference to the historic Jonah Of the Bth century 8.C., Mr. Reid proceeded to •discuss the character of the book bearing Jonah's name, giving reasons for refusing it as historic, and for accepting it as parabolic narrative. The lessons to,, be drawn therefrom were the fact of God's presence in the heathen world being the imperative de- mand for mission work, the dangers of paroch- ialism and race hatred, and the patience of God, as seen in His gentleness with 'heathen peoples, but especially seen in His long-suffering with •His prejudiced and narrow-minded servants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>"Where Man Is."</head>
        <p>"Where Man Is."</p>
        <p>The series of three lectures on the New Church doctrines • was concluded on Tuesday night; when the Rev. J.; Howarth, of Bolton, based his remarks upon "-Where man is." They saw, he said, that while man engaged in either good or evil designs, hi,s bodily constitution remained the same, and Zonsequently they looked for the change in .that which they had already seen to be the man himself—the soul, which was the man. That led them on to the fact that while in the world of earth-life, a man had a con- sciousness that he lived and acted, yet that part of him which was the ego, the " me" so to speak, is affected and 'changed, though then he was, not so much as conscious of its being or existence. Surely then man must be a two- fold being while he lived and moved, and had his being in this world. There was that part of him, the outer part, so closely and inseparably attached to the things of the earth and or which he was conscious, and that inner unseen part, though for the time beyond 'the grasp of his consciousness, which was indeed the man him- self, the truth being that the part of which they were unconscious for,the time being was the very active motive and living force which directed and guided the body as its instrument.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>SCHOOL CLINICS.</head>
        <p>THE SCHOOL CLINICS.</p>
        <p>The Income Scale Basis Removed.</p>
        <p>There was a lengthy report submitted to the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening on the subject of the school clinics. The Medical Inspection Committee recommended that the scales of income at present in operation be suspended for a period of 12 months, and that ,the matter, be re-considered at the end of that time.</p>
        <p>Mr.NonoN said the new arrangement was so that children might be more promptly treated than if they had to go to the Infirmary or doe- tors. He did not cast reflection on anyone, but ; delay necessarily arose, • and delay meant the loss of the school grants. In the new scheme, instead of undermining parental responsibility, they: were really enforcing it. The Committee were practically unanimous in suspending the scale as an -experiment. He moved the report. Mr. PARKER seconded and said the present sys- tem was very clumsy. They took the income that came into a family, but did not recognise the money spent. There might be a sick wife or four children, He .thought the new arrange- ment would do less to undermine parental , authority than did the existing scale. The chief object was to get the children back to school as quickly as possible. Councillor PEMBERTON thought they should have more information.</p>
        <p>Mr. KINGDON : You can always join the com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Mr. tesoN said it was absolutely essential medical. treatment should besgiven as quickly. as possible, and instanced a Case where a delay of eight days, arose before a child got the necessary treatment, That meant a loss in grants. The scheme of the • Committee was adopted unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>COUGHED FOR 8 YEARS. Lady's Extraordinary Recovery from Bronchitis and Asthma. A SCOTTISH MIRACLE.</head>
        <p>COUGHED FOR 8 YEARS. Lady's Extraordinary Recovery from Bronchitis and Asthma. A SCOTTISH MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>Are the days of miracles returning to put to shame the science of the 20th century? One 'could almost believe so. Here is another of 'those fitartling recoveries from severe illness which are becoming so frequent Of late. It is the case Of a Mrs. Margaret Gray, who lives at Southfields Longniddry, Haddingtont Scotland, and who has recoVered health after eight years' suffering -from Bronchitis and Asthma, which doctors coul&amp; not cure. This. is her story to a reporter :—" My trouble came on with an ordin- ary cough,", she said, "which got worse and worse, and though I had doctors.' got no relief. I -used to cough till I was utterly exhausted, . while :Isthma kept choking me up till I could hardly breathe. After eight years of this suffer- ing I got Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and then I soon felt easier:\ Veno's brought up the phelgni, and gave me instant relief, and in a few 'weeks my trouble dis,a ppeared entirely." - This is one of the numberless rases of equally amaz- ing cures effected by thb same great rethedy. Veno's Lightning Cough Core is a perfect remedy for all diseases of chest, lungs, and throat, in children or adults.- Price 9Ad., is. lid., and 2s. 9d. of all chemists.</p>
        <p>Lydia Morgan, the wife of Tipton (Stafford- shire) labourer, died on Saturday as the result of blood poisoning caused by 'running a pin ipoto her ankle last April.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WARRINGTON (14, nrCht • •</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON (14, nrCht • •</p>
        <p>MOTHER'S POINTED QUERY</p>
        <p>Pathetic Scene in the Warrington</p>
        <p>Coroner's Court.</p>
        <p>DEFECTIVE TRAM LIFEGUARDS</p>
        <p>. There was'a pathetic scene in the Warrington Coroner's Court on Monday, when Mr. Brig- llouse held an inquest on the little victim of the tramway accident. I tarry Hough. one year and months, of 234, Liverpool-road, Warring- ton. The Mayor, Mr.. A. T..fiallaway (deputy town .clerk),- and Alderman. James Evans ;Mended. The boy slipped off the kerbstone in Liver- Pool-road into the front of The car, and .was knocked down by the corner of the lifeguard. He was conveyed to the Infirmary, Where he died. The, Mo6ier said on Friday evening she took her husband's tea to Houghton's wire works, leaving deceased •in the charge of her two daughters, aged six and four years respectively, Whom she told to take the boy out a-minute No that he would not cry after her. On her return she heard that flurry. had been knocked - down by a tramcar. Bertha Jones, wife of Edward Thomas Jones, , general labourer, 10, Bond-street, said she was .sianding. in Liverpool-road, when she saw de- ceased step in front of the car, which knocked him down. She screamed and the car stopped. Nl° did not think the driver had any chance to 0\ old the accident. The car was not going very fast ht the time. The Coroner : How far would the cal be away when the child stepped` off the kerb?-7-About three yards. The Coroner : What is the distance from the !ram lines to the kerbstone? - Betective-Sergeant Holland; About seyen feet. Mary Elizabeth Bond.' wife of Itichard Bond, Liverpool-road. said the boy went. straight I nder the car, , • The Coroner : Cou Id the driver hiuv (1O It More than, he did?—No.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MOTHER CHEATES A :s.CENI</head>
        <p>MOTHER CHEATES A :s.CENI</p>
        <p>ainuel Smith, the driver, 40. Wilderspool- ad, said that when the child stepped off the l'otpalli he applied the emergency .brake and PPed in about . Iwo yards. The child wins iYing between the lifeguard•and the wheel, lie thought he would have to lift the car up, but 14 conductor got the boy out. The Coroner: how is it the guard did not Ikct? Witness: The boy was caught by the corner. if the boy had 13ecii in the Middle the guard \Mulct liave dropi)e(t, down. " - Airs. Hough (ex(itedly): How is it he was</p>
        <p>not cut to pie,.cs by the wheel? lie NA-as/a!lowed L o get ahnosi lo the wheel before the lifeguard \vas put in action. What use is it having a lifeguard? The Coroner: Seeing that Mrs. 41 lough was delivering her husband's Leh, she could not see litat. No one cross-examined her for leaving three children in the house by Illeinselves, qiihough there was ample room lo do so. I :have not said anythint; about that. \tin-think- ing people say parents should not leae their 'llildren like that. but I quite appreciate other People are not all in the same position as the Ainyor or myself—they cannot afford servants, 4ind therefore have to do the best they can Miicier the circumstances. If it can be pre- Vifitcd, you, the jury, will agree with me it is nilvisable •to leave children of this age with otne grOwn-up person. It is a difficult pro- 'hem, hat, I am not attaching any blame to ninl. They would nut' do these things if they ere better off in life. Yoh gpnllt•tnen of the I"i7V know the life in the streets nf Warrington • "Ater than I do, perhaps, and know. that, when '1 Man is driving-on lines, he is not like the Oliver of a motor car. he cannot gel out of the \vaY. When he sees anything comitbyr he has -- ado the hest he can to stop the car. Children O like shot's out of a gun off the footway into 'lie. street, and if a man.did'his.best he could not be blamed. A verdict of "Accidental death"' was re- turned, no blame rittnehing to the driver. The Mayor, on behalf of the Corporation, ex- i?ressed his personal sympathy and that, of the ,I:orporation with the relatives. . Ile thoroughly 'treed with all the Coroner had, said.</p>
        <p>'RUSKIN AND DRESS LENGTHS. lilt will be news to the majority of our..readers t,t4t :Ruskin founded .a mill' in the Isle of Man e:-the making of cloths for suitings and ladies' ihesses. Disgusted by the trickery in textiles, fei started the St. George's Mill at Laxey, vhere :)ries, the best that could be produced, v.ere 41111factured, irrespective of profit. Any reader ti 10 'wishes to procure the beautiful products of oletaill, which inclades homespuus, costume n„ "uls, blankets, travel ling rugs, &amp;c., should send ,stPosteard for patterns to Ruskin Homespun, d• George's Mill, Laxey, Isle of Man, or to the N,`,stributors, Messrs. C. Holroyd and Son, 110, '4%1i-street, Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>" WAR AND PEACE." s' War and Pcace,' the new journal of inter- Ith.tional relations, is concerned with living `!llestions Of far-reaching importance to every ''„lintrY, and therefore to every man and woman. 8 '• NOrllian Angell's campaign for a true per- .llective with reo"'‘-ird tb these vital questions of Int emotional relations has gripped the attention ..t411(1 should now arouse the sympathhy of a ~lolightful public. The contributors to "War Peace" are distinguished, and their atti- pat . litte (though not colourless), and the ;ulna' has the official support of the Angell Ltoti ement.</p>
