      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>FARMER'S DEA H.</head>
        <p>FARMER'S DEA H.</p>
        <p>Jury Returns Verdict of "Found Drowned" in Padgate Sensation.</p>
        <p>MYSTERY STILL UNSOL.</p>
        <p>Mr.. S. Brighouse 'held an inquest at the Farmers' AIMS Hotel, Pa.dgate, on Monday, on Thomas Ashley, a farmer, residing with his mol her at Cinnamon Brow Farm,- Poul- ton, whose body was .recovered from A pit in the vicinity Of the farm on Friday morning last -week., .</p>
        <p>Ann Ashley, ,mother of the deceased, said that her son xviis 44 years of age, and was a single man. She last saw him alive on Friday morning, the 17Igh ing., when he went out to attend to the farm. The previous day he had ben engaged in the potato field, when his work was not done to his brother's satisfactiOn, but there was no quarrel. The deceased had never threatened to take his life: On Thursday night her son, John, went away and did not return until Friday. There was no trouble between them.</p>
        <p>On Friendly Terms.</p>
        <p>John Ashley, employed on his mother's farm, Saul that he and his brother were on friendly tennis, and he knew of no reason why "deceased should have done this. There was no insanity in the family.</p>
        <p>Si‘rgt. Clark: I have made inquiries into this. There is no insanity in the family.</p>
        <p>-„ - John Unsworth, a farm labourer, in the em- ploy of Mrs. Ashley, deposed to finding the body in a pit 300 yards from the house. On Friday morning he foUnd deceased's cap on the bank-, and later he recovered the •body which was fully dressed. It was face downwards in the water. On the cloy before lie heard nothing wrong between the two brothers. The pit would be about three or four feet deep.</p>
        <p>P.C. Butterworth stated that he 'received in- formation about the occurrence on Friday morning, and found the •deceased lying fully dresscd.on the embankment. Ills body, so far as he could tell, would have been near the side d the nit, which had about a foot of. mud at the bottom..</p>
        <p>John Ashley, re-called, stated , that his brother never had a fit in 'his lifetime. A juryman pointed out that deceased had not MI anything behind to indicate that he was going to take his life, The Coroner remarked that. if he was going to commit suicide he Would not call his wife and children and tell them that he was about to shoot. himself.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of "Found trowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE COUNTRY. Grafilying Reports.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE COUNTRY. Grafilying Reports.</p>
        <p>- - From all over England reports are being re- ceived showing that cases of digestive and stomach complaints arb rapidly decreasing. This is greatly due to the growing use of the new ozone preparation—Stomoxygen. Oxygen has long been recognised by the highest medical authorities as being a wonderful healing and curative agent, having also a disinfecting and nutrition-stimulating effect upon the digestive organs. Stomoxygen is practically oxygen or ozone in tablet form. • Indigestion, constipation, flatulence, kidney, stomach, and liver complaints are, in the main, the direct result of bacteria and toxins in the stomach and intestines. Stomoxygen Tablets, after being swallowed, give oft quantities of oxygen, which destroys these microbe organ- isms. thus eliminating digestive and stomach troubles by removing the cause. You can get Stomoxygen Tablets from your chemist, in large packets, at 2/9 each, or a postal order .for 2/9 will bring them nost free direct from the Stomoxygen Company, Norwich- street, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>GIRL SWIMMERS.</head>
        <p>THE GIRL SWIMMERS.</p>
        <p>Why They are Less Expert.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday Alderman Bennett drew a comparison between the swimming abilities of the boy and girl scholars. The figures showed that 12 boys were able to swim 100 yards; 22, 50 yards; 121, 25 yards; and 181 less than 25 yards. Nineteen girls only could swim 25 yards, and 16 under that distance. Alderman BENNETT said he was allot familiar with anything in the female sex which would render them less capable as swimmers than the boys. They should be able to swim., quite as long lengths. lie hoped the statistics, as to the girl swimmers next year would be more favour- able. •</p>
        <p>COllll6llol' PEMBERTON pointed out that they could not get a fair. comparison, as there were four times more boys than girls learning to swim. There was no earthly reason why the girls should nqt swim its far as the boys if they got the chance. They wanted better facilities for the girls.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ifrsoii said Owe was another factor to be considered. Many boys had attended the baths before this ,season. Some had learned to Swim two years ago, and the girls had never been to the baths before this season. They could only Send a very small number of girls. If the councillors on the Education Committee would . only speak up—(Councillor PEMBERTON "Oh, oh !")—they would- get better' facilities for the. girls. Owing to the tremendous demand On the limited accommodation many of the scholars could .not go to the baths at all. There were many boys leaving school who never had an opportunity- of learning to swim because of the extremely- limited facilities. The subject then dropped.</p>
        <p>So unpalatable was the three months' supply of coffee bought by the Metropolitan A.sylums Board on the advice of their expert that it has been sold at a heavy loss, according to a report of the Contract Committee of the Board on Sat- urday, and it has been decided to experiment with open competition tenders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RELIGIOUS SO C/A L</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS SO C/A L</p>
        <p>A tiULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Are the Conceptions of 'Parenthood being Undermined by Pleasure ?'</p>
        <p>TRADE UNION HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday morning, at the Bewsey Men's Adult School, the topic wils " Youth and his development." There was a general opinion that at this critical period of life parental responsi- bility, with its attendant duties, was not a -marked feature of modern town life. Youth had just left the elementary school with its discip- line and restraints, and found a certain freedom in the larger life of the woidd. On the physical and moral sides of life he was particularly ex- posed to new temptations. His destiny in a manner was largely determined by what hap- pened in this stage, and it was generally felt by those who took part in the discussion that parents left too much to outside people in the control of youth. The, primary duties and 'con- ceptions of parenthood were being undermined by pleasure, ignorance and carelessness. The, home life must be the controlling centre for all that tends to the higher and better development of youth. In the evening Mr. G. m Mortimer, of Eccles, addressed the meeting. As the school was hold- ing its harvest festival, the speaker used the occasion to suggest certain thoughts associated with harvest. In plant and flowers one finds an infinite variety in form and colour. Nature seems bent on variety, yet along with it there is always harmony. Then in human life God had not fashiOned all men alike, for every man had some peculiar function of work allotted to him. Nature, in her processes, makes little noise. Some of the mightiest work in the work of men is done without fuss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Academy-street Men's.</head>
        <p>Academy-street Men's.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning an inspiring and edifying lecturette on the poet " Longfellow " was given by Mr. James Ball. After referring briefly to the early life of the poet he specially emphasised the fact that this poet wrote with more feeling and sincerity than many of the others. In all his poems there was set forth a high ideal, a lofty tone, and a deep measing. No one could read Longfellow without 'feeling the better for the time so spent. The lecture was made more interesting by the speaker reading several of his poems. Each member was urged to read the great poets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The new home of the Stockton Heath School in Greenbank-street was formally opened by a series of meetings at the week-end. On Sa-t:ur- day a social and musical evening was held, with Mr. John A. Ransome as,cimirman. The artistes were: Madame Lily Fairhurst, Miss -May Isher- wood, Messrs. William Hankey, Alex. White, and others. Short speeches were made during the evening by the Presidents of the other War- rington Schools.</p>
        <p>• At the Men's School on Sunday morning a lecturette on " Giants " was given by Mr. W. Drewitt. The Bible lesson was on the "Train- ing and 'Development of Youth." Mr. Alec Morrison opened the discussion very suggestively and brought forward. a number of practical points as to the influences around young people. The blackboard was used to tabulate the head- ings of the discussion—raising of the school age, choice of employment, recreation halls and grounds, Scouts and .Brigades, and the drama= all finding a place on the board. A lively con- versation centred on the question of the best age for leaving school, in which the opinions ex- pressed were by no means unanimous. nere was some criticism as to local continuation schools, regarding size and discipline of classes, choice of subjects, &amp;c. Differing views were expressed as to the, value of Boy Scouts and Brigades. Some thought that there was a lot of unnecessary tramping and trumpeting at hours when little boys ought to be asleep. One or two members spoke of the need of recreation halls for young people if they were to be kept off the streets. Altogether the conclusion of an animated talk seemed .to be that the adolescent requires a lot of attention.</p>
        <p>In the evening a fellowship meeting brought the series to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Sankey Bridges Men's: Mr. Elwell and Trade</head>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Men's: Mr. Elwell and Trade</p>
        <p>Unionism.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning Mr. los. Elwell gave the second of his lecturettes on "The History of Trade 'Unionism," taking from 11869 to the pre- sent day. He said that in the send and third quarters of the. 19th century the employers were a law unto themselves. The efforts to unite the workers were • opposed on every hand, in some instances agitators were imprisoned for peace- fully persuading men to join unions. One very important thing aristng ,out of trade union activity was the ' passing of the Education Act of 1870. Education was kept back, not o much by the opposition of the upper classes as by the working. people themselves, who were not pise pared to sacrifice themselves in order that their children might stay at st hool. The most im- portant step forward, after the reaction against Trade .Unionism brought on by the Franco- German War, was the legalisation of trade unions, folibwed by the passing of the Ballot Act, Factory Act, and other legislation affecting industrial life.. Dealing with the present posi- tion, Mr. Elwell said that Much mischief was caused through union members not taking enough interest in their own affairs. What was wanted Was higher intelligence, which would enable both masters and .men to understand better the complex questions' which arise from time to time. Another great step forward was made when it was decided to run candidates for Purl lament, working-class opinion being repre- sented.by such men as Bradlaugh, Joseph Arch, Thomas Burt,' Keir Hardie, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The Bible lesson on " Youth .and its develop- ment" was iiltroduced by -Robert Williams, who said that in order to produce men according to the principles laid down by Jesus Christ parents needed to learn -to look at life' fro ia the point of view of- the youth who was just stepping into the industrial area. To put a child to: work because his money was necessary to the upkeep of a house was A sad reflection on civilisation, (Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>1 , , 5.,,7 . ••*, 1,.; ..„,, .„...71.,3/4, .„4... -..,,- .4,. ~;s .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FAMOUS ORGAN BUILDERS.</head>
        <p>FAMOUS ORGAN BUILDERS.</p>
        <p>Abraham Jordan, in 1712, invented the swell organ; originally the front of the swell box was opened like the sash of a window-frame and balanced by a weight, the Venetian shutters were not invented until a later period. C. J. Bishop established about the end of the 18th century, was the inventor of the composition pedal, and the Clarabella stop. This firm is now in existence as Bishop and Son. Other well-known organ ,builders were the Grays. \ There were several members of this fannly, and the firm is still in existence as GraY and Davison! Established 1750, they do high- class work; they built the organs at Leeds Town Hall, Crystal Palace, and St. George's Chapel, Windsor., and numerous instruments are ,to be found of theirs all over the country. • About 1750, John Snetzler, from Passau in Germany, settled in this country and built a noted . organ in St. Margaret's Church, Lynn Regis, Norfolk,, in 1754. The 'famous firm of William Hill and Sons are his successors. Their organs are noted for the fine, diapasons. West- Minster - Abbey, Manchester and Lichfield Cathedrals, also Town Hall, Sydney, are amongst the many instruments for 'which they are noted.</p>
        <p>Schulze is another well-known name. He- erected an organ -at the Exhibition of 1851, and in 1862 built the famous organ at Doncaster, of the fii•st instruments with five manuals in this counky,.and which is noted for its mag- nificent lone., It bears favourable comparison with most instruments.</p>
        <p>Henry Willis,- who was the pioneer of the modern organ, made himself famous by the instrument he erected in St. George's Hall, Liverpool, in 1855, an organ that has never yet been surpassed in tone, a fact that few will dispute. His reed stops were noted for their brilliancy and power, and his methods of voicing them were copied with varying success by other builders. The name ()f Willis is still 'a guneantee of everything that is best in organs. He was one of the first to make full use of the pneumatic lever; and the many cathedral, church, and concert organs he erected will long remain as evidence of his skill.—John 11. Brom-. ham, in 'The Sunday at Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SEASON OF COLDS.</head>
        <p>THE SEASON OF COLDS.</p>
        <p>Peps Banish the Health Dangers of Damp</p>
        <p>Days St Chilly Nights.</p>
        <p>This is the season of coughs and colds when dangerous weather changes reap a harvest of victims. One person in every ten is a sufferer, and the chemists report a big demand for Pops, the novel breatheable tablets for the throat and chest. Colds are caused by invisible germs, which enter the throat and lungs through the nose and mouth. You cannot keep these germs- out altogether, but with the aid of Peps you can prevent them living and doing mischief after they have entered. By letting one or two Peps tablets dissolve in the mouth, you bathe every remote corner of the nostrils, throat and lungs, with an antiseptic and germ-destroying vapour, that acts as a powerful disinfectant, and provides the soundest defence against the germs of chills and colds. The Peps treatment is based on the knowledge gained from the. latest medical discoveries re- garding throat and chest ailments. Being in into direct form of air, the Peps medicine comes direct .contact with all the delicate breathing tubes wherein the, "cold-germ" lurks, and also penetrates deep down into the lungs. It is im- , possible ,for ordinary lozenges, cough mixtures, or liquid " cold-cures " to reach the lungs direct; they are simply swallowed into the stomach and fail 'entirely to touch the real cause of a cold.</p>
        <p>The Peps direct breatheable method means immediate protection for the delicate membrane that covers all the air-passages extending from the mouth to the lungs. It means also that the breathing organs are kept up. to concert pitch, the bronchial tubes clear of phlegm, and the lungs protected against disease. For the old-folk, the middle-aged, and the children, Peps are the best safeguard against the health dangers of " chill October."</p>
        <p>TIIE LITTLE VILLAGE."</p>
        <p>Last summer I went to London ,for my hali-. days, and wilt describe one of the days I spent seeing the principal streets and buildings of the. Metropolis. We took the car to Westminster, which car- ried us light across the bridge. . Llefore we erossod the bridge, however, we had pasSed. St. Thomas's Hospital. When we - got out of the car we were right outside the. Houses of Par- liament and gig Ben. We then walked along a Hi, and cane to Westminster Abbey, where we entered, and saw the tombs -61 many great men. We then walked 'along the embankment, on which is Cleopatra's Needle; which, after ninny storms and dangers, was brought to London tram Egypt. We also passed Somerset. fibuse; Hole! Cecil, and the Savoy. After that we went up to Ludgate Hill to St. Paul's Cathedral, into which we went, and saw the beautiful altar, on Which were Sonic lovely lilies. Coining out or Si. Paul's We walked up Chdapside, which brought us, to the Mansion Muse,. the Boyal Exchange and Bank of England. Going on we snw the monument; winch was \put up to coni- incinorale the great Fire of-London. We then. (TOSS ii I a til ii Bridge, took a, peep- at South- wark Cathedral, crossed the road to :London Bridge Station. taking the train to Peckhati , where I WNS staying, and thus brought 4o a close a very enjoyable day.--LGLAilys.</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOULS—continued. and while admitting the difficulties still in the way, he thought that Sufficient thought was not taken with regard to the responsibility.- of parent- hood.</p>
        <p>, Bewsey Women's School. . An open school was held on Sunday for the. arves h estival, when a service Of song, entitled " CHI Robbie," was rendered by the choir in an cient manner. Mrs. Tanner very ably gave the reaclsnr, connections. Mrs. Parkes and Miss Wright were the soloists.. . A choice selec- tion of fruit anti flowers, given by members and friends, was tastefully displayed. On Monday cvcAilig a fruit banquet and concert was given.</p>
      </div>
