      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PALE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>PALE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Ist PRIZE: Xio POCKET MONEY</p>
        <p>and Free Return Ticket to London</p>
        <p>(Or Cash if desired.</p>
        <p>NINE 2nd PRIZES: £5 POCKET MONEY and Free Return Ticket to London (Cash if desired).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SIMPLE CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>SIMPLE CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>NO ENTRANCE FEE.</p>
        <p>All that is asked for is either a '" catch- phrase," or a testimonial limited to 25 words for Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds. Of course, the proprietors of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds would prefer you to test the value of their wonderful remedy, and to write your opinion, but if you do not suffer from stomach or liver trouble, then send along a " catch- phrass " describing Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds. Here are a few "catch-phrases" to show you what is wanted (in case you decide to compete) :</p>
        <p>"THE SEEDS OF VIGOROUS REALM" "LITTLE AND GOOD." ' " NATURE'S OWN REMEDY."</p>
        <p>Read the rules below, get your blue "wrapper," and enter at once. The first to enter gets the best chance. Last day for send- ing in is Monday, 3rd November. The names and addresses of the 10 prize-winners will he published in these columns on Friday, 7th November.</p>
        <p>kULFS. (Read these carefuily before entering.)</p>
        <p>(1) Everyone resident in Great Britain is eligible, and you may send in as many attempts as you like.</p>
        <p>(2) The- prize w- ill be awarded to the best "catch-phrase " or best testimonial limited to 25 words -received, but if two attempts are considered ' as of equal merit the prize will,be given to the one first opened. Priority will be given to those who enter earliest, as under no circumstances will the prizes be sub- divided. In order to give everyone the same chance, no attempt will be opened before next Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>(8) All atte;npts posted up to the last post on Monday, 3rd IVovember,will be eligible to compete:</p>
        <p>(4) No attempt will be considered nihich is not accompanied by the COUPON OPPOSITE AND AN OUTSIDE BLUE WRAPPER from a bottle of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds.</p>
        <p>(5) The winners' names and addresses will be published in these columns on Friday, 7th November.</p>
        <p>SOAP AND LEATHER.</p>
        <p>Discussion Over P, Wan ington Laundry</p>
        <p>Contract.</p>
        <p>A COUNCILLOR'S "PROTECTION."</p>
        <p>- There was a somewhat amusing discussion at the Warrington Education Committee' on Monday evening over a minute of the -Finance Committee, .which stated that that committee had entered into an arrangement with the Latchford Steam Laundry for the washing and ironing of the towels at the various schools at these charges: Holler towels, Gd. per dozen; towels, 3d. per dozen;, teachers' towels, ad. per dozen. Councillor PEMBERTON : I understand there has been no, competition for this 'contract. We have several laundries in the town, and I think this is not fair, to them. I think this should have been thrown open to competition, and it might have, been advertised. - Councillor lioscaTs (the chairman): Surely you don't Want to get the towels washed for less than a halfpenny per piece? (Laughter.) Councillor PEMEERTON : I'm .not saying that.. I say there are other firms in the town who ought to have got a chance to tender. I'm not grumbling at the price at all. .1 move that the matter be referred back. Councillor ARCHER seconded, and Said if the Latchford Steam Laundry were prepared to take the work at a la. lier towel and lose money, the committee ought to. protect them (the company) against themselves. .(Laughter.) Warrington Soap and Leather.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, chairman of the Finance Committee, said the Baths Committee had hitherto done the work by mutual arrangement, but it had not been satisfactory, and that was provisional, effort, to get a better arrange- ment. The amount involved was very small. It was purely a tentative suggestion, and the time to talk would be after they had some ex- perience as to whether the new method would work. They were now near the end of 'the financial year; and were only giving the scheme a trial.</p>
        <p>'Councillor ManEs thought it was linie they had a change. At tli‘2 end of the year they could make new arrangements by public com- petition, but in the meantime something had Lo be done. • Mr. PARKER: Could it not be laid down that Warrington soap should be used? (Laughter.) Councillor PEMBERTON Ido not object,to that, but you will want to get Warrington leather next. (Laughter.) ,</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : The contract will be open for public competition in January next. Th\e amendment was withdrawn and the sub- jet evaporated. •</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING 1914 ALMANAC. The 1914 edition of the "Green Book of Pro- phecies," issued by the Proprietors of Zam-Buk, is now to hand, and it is, as usual, replete with Matters of great interest to the housewife, and the family circle generally. Judging by the predictions of their astrologer- contributor, Sepharial, some stirring events are to happen in 1914, and if the success Sepharial had in forseeing the demise of King Edward is to be repeated, then every line of these predic- tions will he read with rapt attention. Internal difficulty in India, scenes of recrimination lu the English Parliament, Socialist and Suffra- gette activities, and war-like alarms in Europe are among the "indications of the stars." There are also personal warnings and monthly weather forecasts to gratify the reader's curios- ity, and the particulars are published in this Almanac of an interesting Essay Competition, for which £lOO in prizes will be awarded. Copies of the book may be had from the Pro- prietors of Za.m-Buk at Leeds on application by postcard, mentioning the name of our' paper.</p>
        <p>In order to focus public attention upon the merits of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds, the pro- prietors of these Wonderful little aids to vigorous health have decided to offer for compe- tition the above magnificent prizes. There is no entrance fee, and everyone resident in Grelit'Britain is eligible. All you have to do to enable you to enter is to cut out the coupon below and procure a blue wrapper from a bottle of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds.</p>
        <p>(6) No correspondence can be entered into concerning this competition, and the decision of the Directors of the Sands' Medicine Co., Ltd., will be final. (7) It is 'understood that competitors have read the above rules and agree to abide by them. (8) All attempts sent in are to be the property of the Sands' Medicine Co. Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FAMOUS MARKET WRECKED.</head>
        <p>FAMOUS MARKET WRECKED.</p>
        <p>The most destructive fire ever known in South- port broke out late on Saturday night, and re- sulted in the total destruction of the public market. Despite the strenuous efforts of the Southport and Birkdale Fire Brigades the build- ing was gutted completely, and instead of I a palatial market hall the scene is now one of bare walls and twisted ironwork. The damage is estimated at £BO,OOO.</p>
        <p>• '0 -317 A •,„„ft, , i7r '' ;0. " • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ALL YOU HAVE TO DO.</head>
        <p>ALL YOU HAVE TO DO.</p>
        <p>(1) Write out on a sheet of notepaper either / a "catch-phrase" for Dr. Sands' Liver , Seeds, or a testimonial, whichever you prefer. (Testimonial limited to 25 words.) (2) Cut out the coupon below after filling in the particulars required. (3) Cut off the outside blue wrapper from a bottle of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds. (4) Pin the above three things 'together (1) , your testimonial or "catch-phrase." (2) the filled-up coupon, and (3) the whole of - _ .</p>
        <p>the "Dr. Sands' 'Liver' 'Seeds" blue wrapper. (5) Address an envelope to Sands' Medicine Co., Ltd., 97, New Oxford-street, London, W.C., carefully marking the envelope on the left-hand corner "Competition Depart- ment." (6) Place your attempt, the coupon, and the wrapper in the addressed envelope, gum down securely, affix a penny stamp, and post as early as possible.</p>
        <p>•----------- OR. -SANDS' LIVER 5hE0.5. PRIZE COMPETITION. ENTRY COUPON. Attached to this coupon is my attempt for the above competition. I enclose also a complete blue wrapper taken from the outside of a bottle of Dr. (-ands' Liver Seeds, and .agrce to 'abide by the rules, which I have carefully read, and to accept the Directors' decision. ,Name Address,. Chemist's Name Address Please purchase of your own chemist if possible, as if you win a Prize he will be awarded a guinea because he supplied a winning competitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TOO OLD AT THIRTY-ONE.</head>
        <p>TOO OLD AT THIRTY-ONE.</p>
        <p>An Interesting Discussion at the Education Committee Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MIS-WORDED ADVERTISEMENT.</head>
        <p>MIS-WORDED ADVERTISEMENT.</p>
        <p>There was a discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday night as to, whether an age limit. should be fixed to .the adi'ertisement, for a stores clerk at a salary of £l2O per annum. Councillor ARCHER said the Finance Com- mittee had not fixed the age at 30 years, as appeared in the .advertisement. A stores clerk had responsible work and a, man of thirty was not mature enough for it. There was a prin- ciple in that advertisement which should not obtain in any appointment under the Corpora- tion. There were men over 31 years fully qualified to do the work, but they were de- barred by the advertisement. If they were going to set up age standards against such men he said "More shame to them." He moved the minute back'for further consideration.</p>
        <p>Councilor PEmBERTON seconded. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : The Finance Committee did ,not decide anything about an age limit. The subject was informally spoken about and 'there has been some misunderstanding. There is nothing about it in the minute. Councillor PEMBERTON : It is a serious matter what it says in the advertisement. Will Not Be Repeated.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY : The advertisement, when re- peated, will not have an age limit. It is an accident that it has been put in. It does not require a formal amendment. Councillor PEMBERTON : Too old at 31 is com- ing it a bit too strong. I object most strongly to things being done that have not got the sanc- tion. of the committee or the sub-committee. You, Mr. Jolley, are the chairman, and if you undertake to take it out, we withdraw the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr. jOLLEY : I give that undertaking. Councillor PtMBERTON : If you don't, there is going to be a strong expression Of public Councillor.lloßEllTS ([lie chairman): There is no expression at</p>
        <p>e:01111ClifOr PI.AUTERTON.: Aren't you going to take an, expression of opinion.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I am satisfied to take what the Chairman of the Finance Committee has just said, and unless you members raise the question, the position will be that the next ad, vertisement will not contain the objectiondble words.</p>
        <p>The amendment was withdrawn, and the matter dropped. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>GUARDIANS AND SMOKE.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS AND SMOKE.</p>
        <p>Notice to Abate Alleged Nuisance</p>
        <p>Produced.</p>
        <p>As the Guardians proceeded to the fortnightly meeting on Wednesday, the chimney, which has been complained ,of, was emitting volumes .of black smoke.</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced the notice he had received stating that there was a nuisance at the Work- house arising from the new chimney adjoin- ing the men's sleeping 'quarters, which sent out black smoke in such a quantity as to create a nuisance. The Guardians were given notice to abate the same.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER : I propose that the matter be attended to at once. This is the first we have known about it, and we ought to have no more. Mr. FURNESS : IS there a summons? The CLERK: No. A notice to abate.</p>
        <p>Mr. FURNESS : It must be a matter of regret to the poor. If the borough officials were half so assiduous in other directions as they are hero, we should have the town far better clarified. Mr. BRooxns: I think it is in the formation of the new flues in the chimney. If it were re- ferred to the architect of the boiler arrange- ments, the nuisance might be avoided. Mr. • MATHER : The Guardians have done all they can before this. They had ordered some steam devices for the boiler to prevent any nuisances. There must be some little fault in connection with the boiler that we know noth- ing about. It was decided that the matter should be attended to at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>REASONS WHY YOU SHOULD DRINK</head>
        <p>REASONS WHY YOU SHOULD DRINK</p>
