      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>OPEN OR CLOSED?</p>
        <p>Education Committee and the Com- petition for New School Plans.</p>
        <p>A PUBLIC CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Should there be free trade in architects? This was the tenor of a long discussion at the meet- ing of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening, over which Councillor 11. Roberts presided. Tho Sites and Buildings Committee recom- mended the rescinding of a resolution connected with the planning of a new school, which throw the designs open to architects all over the country, and recommending that only Warring- ton architects be invited to compete for the now school in Lovely-lane, each architect to receive a fee of £lO 10s., which fee was to be included in the commission of the successful architect. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH said if the original reso- lution was carried out it would cost 58 guineas for an architect and 50 guineas for an assessor. They would get it done cheaper on the suggested lines, and the Committee believed that if they secured a local architect he would be more responsible for the work being thoroughly car- ried out. They would get a better built school, and the maintenance in the future would cost less. Mr. G. WALKER seconded. Mr. JARMAN : Is this one of the schools about which we had so much trouble? The CHAIRMAN: No; this is an entirely new school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Mr. Pemberton Extremely Sorry.</head>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton Extremely Sorry.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON moved that the minute be referred back. He was extremely 'sorry to see what the Committee had done, The matter was definitely settled some time 'ago, and now the Sites Committee wanted to narrow the mat- ter down to very small limits. He could _ not agree with Mr. Fairclough that the new scheme would cost less. He did not know what local architects would compete, but it seemed to him they were going back on the conditions the architects asked for, and they were going to ride rough-shod over the Architects' Association. If they limited the competition to Warrington, it meant limiting it to only one firm of archi- tects. • Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded. lie did not know where they would get to if they limited the coinL pAition. He thought they might as well build a 'wall round the town and go back to the old days when everything had to be done in the town. Ho added that they were making a great mistake in doing anything which would prevent them getting the best advice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>An Example for Others.</head>
        <p>An Example for Others.</p>
        <p>comest hs. I:t oi principle b an wl and a. s Ka El 1.1 said lie we do this thing, continued ,Mr. Parker, in our town, it will be an example for other towns why an architect outside the town c to do it. I do not understand where the saving responsible' for looking after the uO. architect in e town. I Object to through. 3 nerf cannot a while sympathising with Mr. Parker's arguments, he would abide by the minute. The Sites Committee felt they could not accept the conditions laid down by the archi- tects. It - was not true that the competition would be limited to one local ,firm Of architects. What the committee had done was to eliminate the assessor from the question. The Committee and their officials were quite competentto deter- mine what they wanted, and, therefore they ' failed to see why they should spend 58 guineas in gsiedtetii•necgl the epjnion of a man whom they con- little better Tan themselves. (Coun- cillor PEMBERTON : "Oh !")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>"Exorbitant Fee."</head>
        <p>"Exorbitant Fee."</p>
        <p>The Sites Committee felt that they could determine what they required without payiiig an exorbitant fee to an assessor. In A previous disciission Mr. Pemberton took up the line that an assessor was not necessary. Now he said an assessor was necessary, but the Sites Committee were not convinced that he was. That would be a question which would always arise, as long as they were ill-advised not to appoint an archi- tect of their own., If they had such an architect they would already have saved between X7OO and XBOO, which would have gone a long way to pay the salary of such an official. (Hear, hear.) Mr. H. KINGDON said the argument was that if they invited plans from Warrington architects they would not need an assessor, but if they threw it open they would need an assessor. It was like inviting plans from all England, barring the Isle of Anglesey and the Hebrides. (Laughter.) He thought they should have the best competition possible so that they might get the lonefit of the latest ideas.</p>
        <p>An Inconsistent Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UrsoN was surprised that the amend- ment had not been instantly seconded by twenty members. He was also surprised that Messrs. Fairclough and Walker, did not second the amendment. On two occasions when he brought forward a notice that appointments for vacant headmastershi Ps should be confined to assist- ant masters in the town he got little support. They had in Warrington schools over 100 men eligible for headmasterships, and the Committee in the past had deliberately refused to restrict the competition to Warrington. Now the Sites' Committee proposed that they should limit the architectural competition amongst a giTiup of men that could be counted on the fingers of one hand. (Hear, hear.) The very men, continued Mr. tipson,who opposed my notice of motion to select a headmaster from the' 100 Warrington teachers now bring forward a resolution to select one architect out of the five or six in Warrington. Where. is' the con- sistency? Surely if it is wrong in the case of the teachers it Is wrong in the case of the archi- tects. We have not got sufficient reasons why we should adopt this resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Changed His Mind.</head>
        <p>Changed His Mind.</p>
        <p>Mr. WALKER: Although I seconded, I am in favour of open competition. (Mr. PEMBERTON : Hear, hear.) Councillor PEMBERTON : On a point of explana- tion. Mr. Walker said I had changed my mind. The CHAIRMAN: We don't mind about that. Councillor PEMBERTON : I do. I have learned from' experience— _ .</p>
        <p>The 6IAIRMAN : I wish to congratulate you then. (Laughter.) , AiderMito BENNETT thought if it was a new Town Hall they wore designing the competition should he open to ngland. (Mr. PEMBERTON : "Shame.") With a school it was different. If the local architects could do the work he believed they should be given a chance. It was good that the money should be spent in the town. The local architects were quite capable of doing the work. It seemed too arbitrary of the Architects' Association to say that they could not have a competition without the ap- pointment of an assessor. The committee and their officials were quite able to adjudicate upon the plans. Great Misrepresentation.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES said there was a great dohl of misrepresentation in the statement that only one local firm would be able to compete. All the architects in Warrington would be justi- fied in competing if the resolution was passed If there was an open competition, and they did not appoint an assessor nominated by the Presi- dent of the association, the members of that association in Warrington would be debarred from the competition. Mr. UPSON asked for an authoritative state- ment as to the exact position. ,</p>
        <p>Air. Timms said if it was a private competi- tion an assessor was not necessary.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN asked what 58 guineas meant on an expenditure of „214,000? He had already voted to secure the best men and material, and could not go back on that vote.</p>
        <p>Mr. FAni-cuouou said an assessor had absolutely nothing to do with the quality of the work. Eleven voted for the amendment and 11 against. The Chairman gave his casting vote in favour of the Pemberton Amendment, which was carried, and the matter thus goes back to the Sites Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>The Mayor Replies to the Assertions of</p>
        <p>Drs. Manson and Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SMOKE AS A DISINFECTANT.</head>
        <p>SMOKE AS A DISINFECTANT.</p>
        <p>Smoke was again discussed at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on• Tuesday night. The Mayor quoted "The Examiner" report of the formation of a branch of the Smoke Abate- ment League. He replied to some of the remarks made by doctors who spoke at that meeting last week. The Mayor argued that itUas not proved that black smoke was injurious to the public health.</p>
        <p>Smoke contains sulphurous acid, he said, which, until recently, had been largely used as a dis- infectant.</p>
        <p>He strongly resented to a medical brother refer- ring to what he had said as being childish.</p>
        <p>It was that stalwart smoke crusader, Alaerman Bennett, who opened the lengthy discussion, by referring to this item on the minutes of the Health Committee: "A 'report on the emission of black smoke was considered. The Town Clerk was instructed to serve notice upon the War- rington Guardians in connection with the emis- sion of black smoke from a chimney at the workhouse."</p>
        <p>The .Alderraan said he was not quite sure whether that was merely a report or whether they had decided to serve notice. Were they to single out their public institutions only and pillory them? Councillor ARCHER said the Health Committee were not selecting public institutions to put them in the pillory. •</p>
        <p>rington Guardians had not been singled out for special mention. Their chimney was referred to in view of the expression of opinion at the last Council meeting that the names of alleged offenders should be published and not kept on the private agenda,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MAYOR'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, then entered into the arena of debate. He said: I think it my duty to myself, as a member- of the Health Committee, as the Mayor, and as a medical man, to reply to an attack—a most unfair attack—made upon me at .a meeting called in Warrington for the ob- ject of forming a branch of the Anti-Smoke League. You will all have seen the report in the ." Examiner." I believe that attack was directed at me. 'lave great pleasure in alluding to my friend, Alderman Bennett, in connection with this meeting without the,least acrimony, as I have always had the pleasure of doing. On this occasion the Alderman was very mild, but, like many brave men, he is more forcible in the face of the enemy than he is in the background. (Laughter.) I sympathise with him when he says he was like someone crying in the wilderness when he has been crying here against black smoke. I have felt, in another department, I have also been crying in the wilderness, and I sympatbkise with him. The point I intend to refer to mainly is that made by a brother practitioner, Dr. Manson. I will quote his woids from the "Examiner," and I shall reply to his attack seriatim.</p>
        <p>My position was that I made a very mild sug- gestion on the strength of reasonable arguments. The position is this: They have thought fit tc attack me, they have made a very strong asser- tion, with little or no argument, or little or no proof. I can prove what I said. In regard to Dr. Manson I -might mention this—l have beer, nearly 40 years in practice, and I was Medical Officer of Health probably about the time or before the time Dr. Manson was born. I was also on an important committee relating to a smoke inquiry, and I was thought worthy of being selected to give evidence in relation to that, so I am not talking without some show of knowledge on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HEALTH BEFORE MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH BEFORE MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Manson, in supporting the resolution, speaks in this manner—l quote from the " Examiner " :—" The men in his profession ;would sooner try to keep the people healthy than to have to give them a bottle of medicine." I do not know whether I should take the opposite of it. (Laughter.) He mentions my name in the next sentence. He said: "Ho noticed that the Mayor (Dr. Joseph)..agreed with the Chair- man of the Health Committee (Alderman James Evans) that smoke had not been proved to be injurious to health."</p>
        <p>agree with the Chairman of the Health Committee, and a great many more eminent men agree, that it has never been proved that smoke is injurious. Nothing Dr. Manson says proves it. He goes on to talk: "The Mayor went fur- ther and said it was possibly .beneficial." I leave it to you whether it is a proper thing for a medical man to hold up a brother medical man, especially when that medical man is much his senior, and occupies my position, to ridicule. I do not call it a proper thing, and I would not have 'done it to him on a small assertion, like that, and without giving the context ,of my statement.</p>
        <p>In making the statement I did, I put it as possibly beneficial, by virtue (if the fact that sulphurous acid, which is a normal constituent of smoke, is a disinfectant used to destroy scar- let fever and smallpox poison. I thought that, as far as that went, it was beneficial—more than that, the statistics which you had the oppor- tunity of seeing the other night proved con- clusively that Warrington had not only a low geheral death-rate, but that in respiratory diseases, which smoke is supposed to influence, it had persistently, for about 12 or 13 years, heed a lower death-rate than , Several other large towns, such as Manchester and Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>IN A FOG, THEN.</head>
        <p>IN A FOG, THEN.</p>
        <p>lie mentions Dr. Niven as an authority. I admit he is an authority, but, unfortunately, he couples smoke with fog, and in stating the injuries from smoke shows that Dr. Nivin has coupled fog with smoke. Everybody knows that fog and smoke are two quite different things. Fog is , a mysterious compound of smoke and other ingredients, and is, I believe, not confined, to towns; it has been even found in mid-ocean in the North Atlantic, so to blame Warrington for fog also,is not at all a reasonable argument. Councillor lIENSIIALL : Re was in a fog then. (Laughter-)</p>
        <p>The- MAYOR: It looked like it. Ido not think there is much more to be said about that. (Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear.) I under- stand Dr. Bennett also spoke—he does not name me—but I can reply to one or two of his asser- tions. He backed up Dr. Manson very dram- atically by saying there is not the slightest doubt, as Dr. Manson had said, that smoke was injurious to health, and that no unbiased medi- cal man could have two opinions about that. He does not give us any proof of that assertion. It is a mere assertion without any proof.</p>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL SUNSETS. Dr. Bennett also says: "They noticed espe- cially during the coal strike the clearness of the atmosphere and beautiful_ sunsets.''</p>
        <p>I put it to you whether there is another aspect of the coal strike. I saw a great deal of distress, privation and misery, and to relieve which we subscribed over £1,200, and that is clear proof that we cannot live upon fine atmospheres. (A Voice: And beautiful sunsets.) Yes; there is something more required to maintain us than these.</p>
        <p>There is another statement by Dr. Bennett. That is in regard to transferring patients, suffer- ing from pneumonia, from the town into the country, and the benefit they derive. There may be some truth in that or not. lao not dispute it. I know it is well known that with regard to asth.matical patients they sometimes benefit by going into the country, but the con- verse also obtains. I know cases where patients have been removed fiora the pure air of Fearn- head to a smoky town like Warrington, and have been relieved.</p>
        <p>I still assert there is no clear proof that smoke is directly dangerous to health. I am sorry to have to allude to the subject to-night, yet I have no other alternative but to defend myself, now or never, against what I look upon as an unfair attack. DOCTORS DIFFER: PATIENTS DIE.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADITURST : Doctors will differ and patients will die. kLaughter.) The MAYOR, I am not aware of that. The DErun TOWN CLERK said that the War-</p>
        <p>CLOTIIS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suitings, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to</p>
        <p>THE -S-KIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill. Laxey, Isle of Man; or G. HOLYROYD &amp; SON, 110, Station-street, Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"HE CALLED ME CHILDISH."</head>
        <p>"HE CALLED ME CHILDISH."</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : That answers my ques- tion. I should not have liked to think that the .Corporation or Guardians were the chief offenders, or to assume that in order to show a semblance of activity the Guardians were named and others offending against the public health Of doing that. were not named. Fools .rush in where angels fear to tread, and I am not going to rush into this dispute between you read this attack re- medical men. The doctors spoke without any on the matter. It is not for me to take sides! dogmatical feeling, I would let the doctors pursue their own course, gsr: edaeasn da ri little h: aablvile btmloeoy sensitiveow n iilii. ° 71 Ia ntldh, The len i. ct hi iisnAcrko, bßyoo, Uul a Have ported in the "Examiner "? Alderman BENNETT : I have and I heard it. Why, in public life we must learn to take and give. The MAYOR: He called me childish. .. (Laugh- ter.) , Alderman BENNETT : They have called me much worse things than that many times. Councillor PEMBERTON : And,. you deserved it. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: What do you think? They have 1 made assertions - I have given ertions without proof. Alderman I3ENNETT : This is a matter in which I will not presume to interfere. I have no medi- cal knowledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ONLY COMMON-SENSE,</head>
        <p>ONLY COMMON-SENSE,</p>
        <p>The lIIA-Yok : Common-sense it onl-3-; requires. Alderman BENNETT : My common-sense tells me clearly, apart from science, that black smoke cannot be conducive to public health, whatever benefit it may be as an antiseptic and 'disin- fectant. If it is conducive to health you would find people flocking to the towns instead of going to the country to gain health. I say that in all good feeling— The MAYog : Good feeling may not be good sense. • Alderman BENNETT : I will not take that as an insult, or I should be very angry—(laughter)— but I am sue you did not intend it as such. The MAYOR: No; I did not.</p>
        <p>A-1.-d-an BENNETT : I don't think you mean it. I am sure you don't. My relative was not arguing in favour of strikes, but that it was better to have a strike and La clear atmospher than have no strike and a smoky atmosphere. He meant that we can have it by the proper fulfilment of, the Public Health Act. I say. that we cannot have trade without smoke. If it is an alternative between no trade and no smoke, I should say let us have Amoke and smoketr e- aUrdy point is that we can minimise the still retain the trade. I am sorry feeling has been .introduced. I would be the last person to introduce it. I have too great a respect for you, sir.. I hope you will take it in the spirit in which it is meant. • We all have our convictions.</p>
        <p>- The MAYOR,: I am merely replying to an at- tack made •upon me, as you or anybody else would do. Surely you understand I am not thin-skinned. In replying to an apparent attack I am quite within my rights. If I lost the vote of thanks to be proposed I -Would still speak My mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MR. 'PEMBERTON'S_ " FANATICS."</head>
        <p>MR. 'PEMBERTON'S_ " FANATICS."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said it was all very well for those persons to raise the question, and it was all very well for a person with one tannery in a country place, with no other chimney for miles around, to say, "We don't create much smoke." But let them put sixty works chim- neys together and see What the effect was. The whole case against smoke was exaggerated, and he looked upon those who were pushing the movement as fanatics; they were not to be treated seriously, because no amount of fact or logic seemed to influence them. What had been said about the effects of smoke might be true' if people were on the tops of the chimneys breathing it, but most of the works chimneys were 120 feet high. He objected to those who were handing to- gether to vilify the town and to persecute people who were fliulitlg the capital and the work to keep the inhabitants going. It was most un- called for. If they wanted an alteration of the law, let them worry Members of Parliament, instead of trying to induce the Council, by untruths, to do something they were not legally entitled to do. Parliament was the place to " barge" over the question.</p>
        <p>KERNEL OF THE TRUTH." Councillor Romans congratulated Councillor Pemberton on having discovered the kernel of the truth in this matter, although he had done his best to hide it by a multitude of aspersions upon people that were not justified. The diffi- culty was that the law was not adequate for the purposes which those who had in vie W the modi- fication of the output of black smoke wished to see accomplished. The difficulties under which the Health Committee laboured with regard to the administration of the law did not seem to be appreciated. The principal offenders were practically exempt from prosecution, and the methods, they adopted made it almost impos- sible to discover which chimney was responsible for the offence. To prosecute the manufacturer who had an odd chimney for occasional offences, while the great mass of the. smoke produced in Warrington was allowed to pass without any attack being made upon the producers, was an anomaly which the Health Committee found gricat difficulty in getting over. While he ad- mired the public spirit of those taking part in the anti-smoke campaign, he would point out that while they sat outside the Council and occasionally apnlied the spur to the Council, they did not lift their little fingers to relieve them or their burdens. • Why did' they not be- come members of the Corporation, and so assist their great protagonist, Alderman Bennett?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TOO DARK TO READ.</head>
        <p>TOO DARK TO READ.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST said he could assure Councillor Pemberton that sometimes, while in Bewsey-road, he had not been able to see McMinnies' house. Not six weeks ago he was reading a newspaper, when a cloud of smoke passed over, and he had to put the paper down until the smoke had cleared away. Iron pipes were now being put up, instead of chimneys, in puddling furnaces, and they were not half the height that 'Councillor Pemberton had spoken of. 'Riglit out of 12 manufacturers in Warring- ton could dispense with smoke if they would. He would not prosecute where the emission of smoke could not be avoided, but where it could be avoided he would. 'Councillor PEMBERTON : I distinctly stated that in this case it was a question of the law being altered.</p>
        <p>councillor BIRTLES thought an experienced stoker could do a great deal to diminish the black smoke. Smoke was certainly dirty and disagreeable in a house when the windows were open, and white curtains must suffer, but it must be remembered that the people could not do without smoke. They had to live by it.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON said the Board of Guardians had made a great mistake in build- ing the chimney, to which reference had been made. They did not know what they were doing, as the chimney ought to have been twice the size and height.</p>
        <p>The llea-lth Committee's minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>A German Woman Who Saw the Retreat from Moscow.</p>
        <p>KING'S MOST TRUSTED FRIEND.</p>
        <p>. In the preparations. for a wedding, whether Royal or otherwise, the bride always- over- shadows the bridegroom in importance. This thuch. fuller particulars of the personality of the ,Duchess of Fife have been 'given to the public than of her husband. Prince Arthur has no trace of his German ancestry, and none of ,pis mother's natural reserve. He is popular wherever he goes, from his cheery and hearty manner, and possibly because neither he nor King George has a brother, they are something more to each other than cousins. Prince Arthur may be considered his Majesty's most trusted friend. ' -He bears a name that has been somewhat uncommon in our Royal House, and it seems to have been considered unlucky ,in the estimation 'of Royal parents. For tile Hamilton Prayer-book, used by the Archbishop of Canterbury at, the marriage of the Duchess and Prince Arthur, Queen Vidoria had ,almost a superstitious belief in the good luck it brought. It has been used at nearly every Royal wedding since that of George 111., whose boast it was that, unlike his father and grand- father, he was born and bred an Englishman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FRIEND OF WOMEN.</head>
        <p>A FRIEND OF WOMEN.</p>
        <p>The late Professor Stuart did splendid ser- vice, not only in promoting the higher educa- tion of women, but in being ever ready to re- dress their wrongs, and to uphold their rights. The University ,Extension Lecpres were the result of his initiative, and he was the first Cam- bridge professor to lecture to women. Always grimly in earnest, he 'was a true and loyal friend, and never more pleasurably, employed than when improving the conditions of life of the employes of the firm in Norwich with which through his wife he was connected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>GERMAN CENTENARIAN.</head>
        <p>GERMAN CENTENARIAN.</p>
        <p>A woman in Germany, the daughter of an innkeeper,has attained the wonderful age of one hundred and twenty years. Born at Pleschen, on the RuSsian frontier, in 1794, she saw the right wing of the army, under Prince. Jerome, pass through her native village on its way to Moscow. She also saw the remnant of Napo- leon's grand army chased by. the CossaCks on UR! return -march. She is the only. person. liv- ing in Germany who witnessed the passing of tins army. She is a marvellous woman, and Ui possession.of all her faculties.. Her memory for the events of her youth is altogether sur- prising.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NEW POCKET.</head>
        <p>THE NEW POCKET.</p>
        <p>The fashionable draperies and the width of dresses round the hips admit of the return of that useful adjunct the pocket, which however, thanks to the bag, most women have learned to do without. The receptacle that served our grandmothers as a safe resting-place, for all sorts of cherished objects, would not only spoii the set of present-day dresses—as indeed it did in former ,times—but tear the transparent and delicate materials now in general use. Conse- quently the new pockets are made to receive a tiny purse, a powder puff case, a pencil, and the lightest of pocket handkerchiefs. The latter alone finds its way to the bottom of the pocket, the other articles, being cleverly suspended by the up-to-date dressmaker, the whole being tucked neatly away under the folds of the draperies, where, however, it is easily got at. Purses have been specially designed for. use with panniers; they are very' light and made of the softest leather. Patch pockets have been worn for a long time on tailored gowns for country wear, but these are of a more prac- tical and useful variety. Satin bags, suspended by chains, and thickly pleated into metal frames, are among the newest designs for these useful and indispensable accessories. I NEW FEATHER TRIMMING.</p>
        <p>One of the prettiest of novelties in the trim- ming department is the new feather edging, made from the plumes of the ostrich in quite a new way, that suggests a delicately curled fringe, and when used judiciously the effect is extremely, artistic and becoming. Dressmakers are using it freely on rest and tea gowns, where it finishes sleeves, fichus . and decol- letages. It makes quaint choux, and is used by milliners for the trimming of hats, but here it is, perhaps, less happy in effect. The fibre feather is another novelty used for wreaths and aigrettes, especially on the übiquitous velvet Tit. This feather is rather stiff, and its novelty may be said to be its chief attraction. The velvet hat shows no sign of losing its hold on popular favour. The Vienna, ready-to-wear make of this hat is less expensive than the home-made, but the latter is decidedly. the better of the two. 'Peacocks' feathers, as trim- mings for velvet and other •hats, are seen this autumn, and may also be included among in- novations of this season. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RIPPLE CLOTH.</head>
        <p>RIPPLE CLOTH.</p>
        <p>A simple tea gown, but very elegant, is modelled on Grecian lines, which are unbroken from neck, to hem. The waist line may be either high or low, and is marked by a sash, finished by tassels at either end. The dress fastens in front, is decollete, and finished .at the sleeves and neck with net or any other filmy frills. The dressing gown, which used to be the most shapeless and dowdiest of garments, is now a thing of beauty, and in this season's ripple cloth, in wool or ,cotton, is a very' com- fortable .and pretty garment. In dressing jackets this Material is thoroughly cosy, and the colours are lovely. The shades known as camel and sand are most attractive in all warm materials suitable for either dressing gowns or jackets, and come rather as a relief after the vivid greens, purples and yellows of the last few months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>"GHOST" IN AN ENGINE SHED.</head>
        <p>"GHOST" IN AN ENGINE SHED.</p>
        <p>, Strabane, Ireland, is talking about a ghost story that has been told by some railwaymen, and the head constable has taken down the statements of those who claim to have seen the apparition. John Pinkerton, an engine cleaner, describing the first visit of the "ghost," says that it appeared in the cleaning-shed after knocking loudly at the door. It gave unearthly yells. Armed with a crowbar, a signalman made a dash at the spirit, but could not .strike it down. On the second night seven men saw the "ghost." It appeared again on Saturday night. and "disappeared through a window." "The form was that of a man of stout build," stated another engine cleaner. • "It ran under the engine, in front of it, behind it, and everywhere. We shiTered."-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>A MARRIAGE TANGLE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Woman who was Told</p>
        <p>She Could Marry Again.</p>
      </div>
