      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CARSON'S ARMY.</head>
        <p>CARSON'S ARMY.</p>
        <p>_AL The 170,000 Men in Buckram Shrink Under Examination.</p>
        <p>SOME "TARIFF REFORM" RECKONINGS.</p>
        <p>No More admirable, more impressive, and cer- tainly characteristic example of the "Orange" campaigners' old, old game of bluff, blood and bluster can be excelled than that which " Our Member" gave last week but one. This gentle- man, who has been in Ulster once, tells uAthat 170,000, working men of Ulster—the flower of Ireland—are going to light against Home Pide. Possibly he may have. his communication from 'the various " War Correspondents "—Lord Northcliffe and Mr. Philip G. Cambray—for these brave Ulster men, ,:who are going to liar down their lives, " are',betweenAhe ages ef 18 and 35 " ; ttiey are picked especial.ly for their _excellent physique and endurance. Therefore there is not a lame man nor a one- armed man, a paralytic nor a consumptive, a sufferer from indigestion or a weakling of any kind amongst the "Cinematograph Braves the noble 170,000 who were inspected by the ecm- posite re-incarnation of Solon and Napoieon, Who has come amongst us. Each one of the pro- digious army of 170,000 is a tried ;Ind proveu " Galloper," fit to run or to fight (for jobs), to jump and throw weights, to shoot with deadly accuracy, and to " riot " with disastrous con- sequences to glass windows. The Secretary tor State for Ulster—Mr. Cambray—has soleuini.y declared as much to the British -public, and those who heard Mr. Harold Sthith must have been greatly impressed—provided that all state- ments made were extremely credulous. Now for a few simple and incontrovertible figures and. facts. When the census of 1911 was taken there were 242,627 boys and, men in Ulster between the ages of 15 and 35. Of this number 74,815 were between 15 and 20. It is easy to calculate that about 44,889 were over 15, but less than-18. Take the Mere youths away, and we find 197,738 male per- sons in Ulster between the ages of 18 and 35— the limits specifically mentioned by Mr. P. G. Cambray in connection with the 170,000 braves Who were recently "inspected." It can be taken for granted that no Catholics were included in . the mighty force. Catholics from 43.7 per cent, of the . population of Ulster, and their proportion of Ulster's manhood is a little greater; but the official figures are suffi- cient for the purposes of a calculation that can be easily followed. On this unchallengeable basis there' are at least 86,411 Catholics between 18 and 35 in Ulster. Take these non-" Volun- teers" from 107,738—the entire available 18 to 735 manhood of the nine counties—and we have a total of 111,327 non-Catholics between 18 and 35 in the Whole province. There is a wide gap between 170,000 and 111,327 —a gap through which 68,373 able-bodied per- sons should have passed • for inspection, and Whose wives and children Might have come amongst us during warlike times. But there are other statistical considerations that must not be ignored:. It is on record that</p>
        <p>More than 100,000 non-Catholic Ulstermen over :the age of 16 declined, despite "canvassing," /threats, active intimidation, and open mis- representation, to sign Sir. Edward Carson's covenant" last year; and as .to the number (4 those who did sign, but felt like the two Newry " covenanters," who swore on their oaths that they had not the remotest idea or intention of fighting, the census returns give no clue. We have been very modest—far too modest—all along in claiming only 10 per cent. of the non- Catholic population of all Ulster as staunch home Rulers. Anti-Home Rulers are most num- erous amongst the elderly men, who have been unable to divest themselves of the evil heritage °f prejudice, ignorance, and fear to which they and their fathers were condemned by the inter- ested predecessors of the present interested generation of ascendancy, partisans and cham- Pions. .Young people are far wiser—politically— than their elders; and it is absolutely certain that ,at the very lowest estimate 20 per cent. of lion-Catholic Ulstermen between 18 and 35 have no sympathy whatever with the doctrines of l'ancour and hate as fostered, by the "Grand Panjandrum," and that they absolutely ,smile at the idea of a "war."</p>
        <p>REDUCED TO 80,000. Take 20 per cent. from the highest possible number of Ulster Volunteers that could be enrolled from Malin to ,Kilkeel, and we have 89,062 remaining. The fatter 'figures include all - the men between the prescribed ages who would in the least degree likely to give in their Intines to Sir Edward Carson's "organisers," and, the 89,062 include cripples and consumptives, ilind men and deaf men, imbeciles and crimin- -425---all the members of that very considerable 'aetion of every population who are "not fit for thaetive service" through -one cause or another. he odd 9,062 may he "struck off "—and the °stip:tate is absurdly low. Then there are, on Vie most generous computation, 80,000 men .3e- Ireen 18 and 35 who could be numbered as Ostet Volunteers—if all of them "enlisted." 110 w many of the possible 80,000 are. realli -azious to come to terms with their " friends, ' th 1 0 R.I.C. ? How many desirp to die upon the i'attlefield? One never knows, and it is no c.)asiness of ours to venture an estimate. The ~ .;:arY told by the figures that cannot- be dis- ltod serves our purpose. Between 170,000 and u.OOO there is only a difference of 90,000. ,Yossibly Mr. Harold Smith will tell us during 'lO next "Orange toui" how it happened .that !E4 One of Sir Edward Carson's " agents-in- 'tl!rance" he was not given a better supply of „ a numbers of warriors than a vague round 'uniber, which would make the proprietor of a tri!Y " peep-show " blush for the dishonesty. - his confederates.</p>
        <p>P. W. IL</p>
        <p>Had Mr. Harold Smith been present at the great Liberal demonstration in the Parr Hall on Wednesday night, he would have learned that Warrington does not approve of his " Ulsteria."</p>
        <p>The hall was crowded, the speeches were pointed and sincere, and on a vote being taken, Mr. Smith had only one supporter in the huge audience.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield dealt firmly and effectively with the " Ulsteria" challenges which Mr. Harold Smith had thrown out at his. street corner meetings a fortnight ago, and proved the absolute absurdity of these challenges.</p>
        <p>It was not, said Mr. Crotfield, and everyone who had the least knowledge of political pro- cedure will corroborate him, for Mr. Harold Smith or himself to fix the issues of the next election.</p>
        <p>It was for the ejectors—THE PEOPLE—to settle what were to be the issues. Mr. Crop- field resented, as any•reasonable man would, this Smithsonian attempt to dictate to the free and independent' electors, of Warrington what they should or should not discuss.</p>
        <p>Any attempt to dictate what should be the issue, said Mr. Crosfield, amidst a loud outburst of applause, "would be nothing else than a piece of the grossest impertinence.v Then he ground the Smithsonian challenges into dust.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift Mac Neill proved that the Protestants in Ireland could live-On amicable terms with the Catholics. He was a Protestant, a descend- -ant of Protestant clergymen from the time of Dean Swift, and he was sent to Parliament by Catholics. Thus the Smithsonian religious cry foundered miserably.</p>
        <p>The great Liberal meeting in the Parr Hall on Wednesday night will ever rank as one of the most memorable political gatherings in the annals of Warrington. The building was filled, the speeches were pointed and telling, and the audience were eager and enthusiastic, two features which bid high promise to the future of Liberalism in Warrington: Mr. A. IL Crosfield presided, and the speaker of the evening was Mr. Swift Mac Neill, M.P., K.C., a noted Nationalist, Who is honoured by all parties in the House of Commons because of his outstanding abilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HOME RULE NOT NEW. Principles of 43 Years Ago.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE NOT NEW. Principles of 43 Years Ago.</p>
        <p>i• F. L. Crilly, general secretary ,of , the ‘:llited Irish Leaguq of Great Britain, gave a ,si'3r instructive limelight address •in the ' lie's Hall in Winwick-street, on Sunday tthtlT dealing with the history of the fight for rrne Rule. Mr. Crilly„ explained that the ieinburgh Hotel (late Bilton Hotel), Dublin, was o,le scene of the originating of .the Home Bute sganisation, in May, 1870. Isaac Butt, a Con. klentl'ative, Was elected the leader, and there also many other prominent Conservatives preß„ At these early meetius a programme .h -r• drawn up, and some of those principles, po,9,,P,ted 43 years ago, they could recognise einbodied in the Bill which had already the House of Commons twice, and which liti",7-1u be lawn May next year. (Cheers.) ." We Nlev e not changed our .policy," remarked the others may have done." (Hear, Mr lish ; Crilly spoke of the severity of the En.,g- -t4l, law upon the Irish, and by the aid of plc- -I,:ilcies, illustrated the methods adopted by the tleh'llish Government to evict tenants, and inci- .lllW'ullY mentioned that if others took pity on of hviettms and helped them, they ran the risk sh.,LJeing themselves so evicted. ("Shame, Mr, • ttna Asquith was a man they all had faith in, IllheY believed that he would carry forward wav''e Nile, and hoped that he would not give thEti t° any of the suggestions of compromise ssAvere being spoken about in the Tory „e. The rept. lecture was followed by an excellent con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>The Liberal Candidate replies to the " Challenges " recently made and scores heavily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MR. S , IFT MaciNEILL LAYS THE MISERABLE RELIGIOUS BOGEY</head>
        <p>MR. S , IFT MaciNEILL LAYS THE MISERABLE RELIGIOUS BOGEY</p>
        <p>And mentions "a kind of a Moon to an F.E. Sun."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>GREAT HOME RULE MEETING.</head>
        <p>GREAT HOME RULE MEETING.</p>
        <p>tinent. The next election iS a couple of years off. (Hear, hear.) Why, ladies and gentlemen, the lace of politics may be entirely changed in a couple of years' time, and a pretty pair Mr. Harold Smith and your humble servant—(laugh- ter)—would make if we came before this great industrial constituency in Lancashire prattling about dead issues—(hear, hear, and applause)— to audiences absorbed in altogether different questions and interested in new issues that are coming up for solution. Yes, and a nice laugh- ing-stock we should make of Warrington if we so played the fool. (Laughter and cheers.) • I assure you, ladies and gentlemen, that when I read that challenge .1 was puzzled to think what Mr. Smith was driving at, and I wonder now whether he has been asleep? cLoud laugh- ter.) Well, if he has been asleep; friends, the sooner he wakes up and realises that the Par- liament Act is passed—(hear, hear, and applause)—and that .it is an Act which has been passed not for mere show, but for use— (hear, hear)—an. Act by which we intend to teach Mr. Harold Sniith and his colleagues that the British nation is not going to be baulked in its work—(hear, hear)—is not going to have its work in the House of Commons frustrated or eternally delayed, the Sooner he realises that and the better.</p>
        <p>HOME nuLg DISCUSSED AND SETTLED. Why, friends, for the last seven and twenty years I have Shad the privilege, on scores of plat- forms in. Warrington, the privilege-of debating, I will venture to say, every side of this great Home Rule question, We have proved our case, friends; from the Imperial point of view; we have drawn an all-important illustration from the United States of America, with its enormous Irish and Irish descended population; pointed to the importance for the peace of the world, for the progress of the world, of main- taining good relations between the British Empire and the United States of America, and the direct bearing upon that which' the settle- ment of this Irish controversy has; we have defended our position from the Colonial Point of view, and in the same way have reminded ' the constituency of the great influence of the Irish in our Colonies, and the overwhelming. opinion in all our Colonies, which is so strongly in our favour. We have defended our, policy from the British point of view, and from the point of view of the British House of Commons; and Mr. Harold Smith himself should by this time realise .the urgency of -the question from that Parliamentary standpoint, patent as it is to every politician in the land. We have de- fended it Eis men who hold that even good gov- ernment is not a substitute for self-government. (Applause.) We defended it, and we have proved our case from the point of view of more economic administration in Ireland. And then we have urged and laid great stress on the healing power of self-government, of which we .have had never-to-be-forgotten examples within the confines of our own Empire—(cheers)—and all the time, all these long years, we have had to contend with ignorance, with prejudice, with unreasoning custom. Which in this case, as always, have been the obstacle in the way of Liberal work and the party of Reform. (Hear. bear.) But this ignorance, and prejudice, and unreasoning custom is at length worn away, and next year this Bill will be passed—(cheers)— and we shall then proceed with the other work waiting for us. What Mr. garold Smith pro- poses to do -at the next election I know not; but if he is then going to begin again talking about streams of American dollars and foreign gold— (laughter)—and the Battle of 'the Boyne— (laughter)—and all the rest of it, I wish him joy.' (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>MR. SMITH'S FIRST CHALLENGE— REJECTED. I do not know what is in his mind—(A Voice: "Does he know?")—my friend says he is not sure that Mr. Smith knows—l have wondered whether Mr. Smith is thinking, a couple of years hence, of starting an agitation in War- rington for the repeal of the Home Rule Bill. (Laughter and applause; If he does that he will .find his position just one degree worse than it is now. (Hear, hear.). So much for his first challenge, my answer to which is a flat and emphatic refusal. (Cheers.) I will do nothing of the kind; I am not going to tie my hands, friends—(hear, hear)—About the issues on which we will fight the next election, and I am still less going to commit myself to the idiotic idea of fighting it on a dead issue. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>are going to raise these questions, and he can no more shirk them than I intend to shirk his second challenge, which I read now: "I chal- lenge Mr. Crosfield again to say whether 'Ulster is bluffing—(loud laughter, and a Voice: "Yes") —and if she is not, what does he propose to do with her?" I am going to give Mr. Harold Smith a pretty complete, and I venture to. think, effective answer to that challenge. I want first to say a preliminary word or two. I am sure Mr. Swift Mac Neill will, bear me out when I tell you that during those long debates on the Home Rule question in the. House of Comfiaons we have shown consideration, and we have made concessions time after time—not, friends, in ray judgment, to any real or genuine danger, whatever—but concessions rather to the fears .of those who have developed an exagger- ated and abnormally suspicious frame of mind. The concessions have been made willingly— (applause)—most willingly, and yo.u. have only got to read, as I am sure most of you here have done, .the public utterances of leading States- men in Ireland and Britain, to understand that the door is still open to every reasonable pro- position that is put before us—(hear, hear, and. applause)—open to every suggestion, friends, that is made, and made honestly—(hear, hear)— with a view to the real. settlement of this ques- tion, a settlement .for Ireland as a whole, and compatible with the establishment of. a Parlia- ment in Ireland, with an Executive responsible to it. And there, I say, ladies and 'gentlemen, we must draw the line. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S REAL FEARS.</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S REAL FEARS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith proposes to confine himself entirely to the Irish question. What, then, about this first plank in the great political, plat- form of the' Tory party? 'What on earth has become of that? (A 'Voice: " Gone !" and laughter.) Mr. Harold Smith, like at! the other Tariff Reformers in Warrington—(laughter)— is committed up to the hilt to the whole Bir- mingham policy. They have burned their boats,. they have crossed the Rubicon, hut now what do they see? They see a lot of the ablest men in their own party, after ten years of this colossal expenditure, of their whirlwind cam- paigns and of all this raging, tearing propa- ganda, they see their own colleagues running away from their position. We have got them on the run—(A Voice: "Keep them on !") yes, we will keep them running. (Applause.) As I read these challenges from Mr. Smith I have begun to wonder whether he altogether likes that first plank in the Tory platform. Perhaps he is a little chary of standing on any plank so hopelessly infected with dry-rot. (Laughter.) Perhaps Mr. •Harold Smith does not care for the comparison which we are going to draw at the next election between all those Tory promises to give old-age pensions year after year left unfulfilled—their hollow, insincere and dishonest promises, and the great measure triumphantly passed by our party. (Cheers.) Perhaps he begins to think that as the months and the years run on the immense blessing to our nation, as a whole,' of the Insurance Act, that has been passed—hear, hear)—will more and more impress itself on the minds of our people—yes, and I daresay Mr. Harold Smith does not altogether relish another prospect that is opening up before us—this mighty prospect of land reform—(hear, hear, and loud applause)— a Prospect unfolded before us even now, and the definite proposals in support of Which bene- ficent policy we shall be fighting for at the next election, when, I venture to say, friends, we shall. not only have the support, but the fervent, vigorous, enthusiastic support of the vast majority of the peopleof this country. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>THE SECOND 'CHALLENGE-ACCEPTED!</p>
        <p>• Well, if Mr. Harold Smith wants to shirk these issues we shall not let him. (Rear, hear.) We</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>"GO RIGHT ON!"</head>
        <p>"GO RIGHT ON!"</p>
        <p>When Mr. Harold Smith comes, on the plat- form in Warrington and talks about the men Of 'Ulster, I must, at the outset; tell him he is wholly unjustified in using language of that kind. (Applause.) With half of the represen- tatives of Ulster on our side, what are the facts that he is faced with? _ There are but four counties in Ulster where this is a really difficult problem—only four. And if you meet with a certain number of irreconcilables in four counties in Ireland, a fraction of a fraction of a province, which itself is a fraction of Ireland, which in turn is a fraction of the United King- dom, which again is a fraction or our whole Empire, are you, I say, going to allow a handful of such irreconcilables to. impose a veto against the whole Empire? ("No, no.") No, you have given him the answer already.' That attitude reminds me very much of the inversion of the old familiar simile, which we all know, about mountains and mice, and if we are met with these preposterous demands from what I have called irreeoncilables, well, when Mr. Smith says what are we going to do? my answer is this: "Go right on." (Loud applause.) We, as democrats, do not admit the right of any section of people in the United Kingdom to say "We will have this and we won't. have that." That way, friends, lies the stultifica- tion and the very negation of democracy. That way lies recklessness, disorder and disruption.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>IS ULSTER BLUFFING?</head>
        <p>IS ULSTER BLUFFING?</p>
        <p>Now I come to his other question—ls Ulster bluffing? • (A Voice: "He is bluffing," and laughter.) Well, friends, from what I have said just now, you will understand I must give that the absolute negative to begin with: Certainly, all Ulster is not bluffing, half of Ulster is with us, and in those four counties I was speaking about you have a powerful and a growing minority that are getting very sick of all this impudent aping of royalty—(laugh- ter)—this nefarious, disorderly, filibustering, folly, and this political braggadocio. (Loud laughter.) I have never disputed the fact that you will find some men in Ulster who, after careful investigation of the facts, have formed an opinion against us. What Ido say, ladies and gentlemen, is this—for every one man you will find in Ulster who gives you reasoned, dis- passionate judgment adverse to us on the ques- tion, you will find a score, or a hundred, or a thousand who have simply worked themselves sip into a condition of fanatical bigotry—(loud applause)—and when you are met with that, at this time of day, I do say there is nothing in modern 'civilisation against which it is our duty more resolutely to set our face- than bigoted fanaticism. (Applause.) One is sorry for the poor dupes, very sorry for the poor dupes that are misled. (Applause.) We mete out the severest censure for the leaders who mislead them, and who with their education and ex- perience ought to know better. (Hear, hear.) Some of the most prominent men in the Con- servative party are very quietly' working at. the thing; quietly tolerating it, or openly instigat- ing and encouraging it, and that, friends, is absolutely disgraceful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SUNKEN MODERN TORYISM.</head>
        <p>SUNKEN MODERN TORYISM.</p>
        <p>You have heard me time and again comment in no flattering terms on the exhibition we have had the last ten years of modern Toryism. It has sunk pretty low, but, upon my word, when you get theni allying.themselves with the forces of disloyalty, disruption and squalid rebellion in this way, I begin to think the once great Tory party has indeed reached the nadir of its existence. (Hear, hear.) I haveApoken of tho leaders, and I want to say a word about some other leaders, not quite in the front rank. (A Voice: "Lord Derby.") I thank my friend for the remark. I think the Member for Warring- ton has received a most well-deserved rebuke from Lord Derby. The Member for Warring- ton said, only the other day, "Sir Edward Carson believpd that no constitutional monarch, and therefore certainly not the present King, could ever give his assent to the present Bill." Ho (Mr._ Smith) shared that view.</p>
        <p>I should he very sorry to take away Mr. Mac. Neill's breath before he speaks to us—(laughter) —but Mr. Mac Neill is one of the greatest auth- orities we have in the House of Commons on constitutional history and constitutional law, and I can imagine his feelings when .he hears a statement like that made by our Member. But I was going to make a remark or two about the minor leaders in this campaign of disorder in Ulster. Some of them seem so asPerately anxious and in such a desperate hurry to get a little cheap political notoriety, to win for them- selves a saint' S halo or a martyr's crown, or .some decoration of that description. (Loud laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SITUATION IN VERSE.</head>
        <p>THE SITUATION IN VERSE.</p>
        <p>After relating a story illustrating the bom- bastic, bluffing methods of the "Ulster Fire- eaters," Mr. Crosfield continued :</p>
        <p>I have some verses here which seem to me exactly to hit off the situation, and I commend them to the attention of those gentlemen who are so fond of talking to us about the "Clashing of swords" and "The grim tragedy that awaits</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SMITHSONIAN IMPUDENCE.</head>
        <p>SMITHSONIAN IMPUDENCE.</p>
        <p>The Member's Modest Suggestion to Fix General Election Issue.</p>
        <p>HIS PRATTLING ABOUT DEAD ISSUES.</p>
        <p>Scathing Criticism by Mr. Arthur</p>
        <p>Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Mr. CRosnoLD, who was enthusiastically cheered, said: I do not know whether you have noticed that in the last few weeks Mr. Harold Smith—(laughter)—has been throwing out—(A Voice: "Prophecies ")—two or three challenges to me; and I propose immediately to address myself to these challenges. (Cheers) This is the first opportuni4 I have had to answer them, and as all of them are concerned with the Irish question, which we are met to discuss. to-night, I think, perhaps, Mr. Mac Neill, it. would not be an unfitting opportunity for dealing with them, (Mr. Mac Neill: ".Don't spare him," and laughter.) The first challenge which Mr. Smith throws out is a challenge to a political duel, and I dare- say you won't be surprised to hear'that because, whatever else they may be, the Brothers Smith take a great pride in being combative. This is the challenge, which I will read you verbatim: "Concerning Mr. Crosfield, I will Make a bar- gain with him. I will contest the next election on the Home Rule question, and no other issue. There must not be any other question stated in the election addresses and literature, or placarded on the hoardings, nothing except Home Rule." (Voices: "Tell him to resign" and "Toll him to go and gallop," and laughter.) I pass by the fact, with which Mr. Smith is familiar, that we on our side have not yet chosen our candidate to fight the next election. I am not going to baulk him of the answer to any of those challenges on that account. I .will assume, friends, for the sake of argument, that I had the good fortune and *privilege of being invited to tackle Mr. Harold Smith once more. (Loud applause.) And making that assump- tion, I must tell him at once that a more extra- ordinary proposition, and I will add a more futile and puerile proposition, I have never set eyes on. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>"A YOUNG POLITICIAN."</head>
        <p>"A YOUNG POLITICIAN."</p>
        <p>Now, friends, Mr. Harold Smith is a young politician--(A Voice: "Aye, he's all that!")— but he is not so young but that he should know by this time that it is not customary in Eng- land for a candidate in this constituency, or the other constituency, to determine the issues on which a General Election is going to be fought. (Applause.) That is not determined by the candidates; it is not determined by the Mem- ber; it is not determined by an isolated con- stituency; and we all know, friends, that often even the leaders of the great parties in the State here—the leaders themselves carinot deter- mine what are the issues that are to decide the fate of elections, because th( country itself takes a hand in the matter—(hear, hear)—and it is usual for constituencies to follow the lead, and to follow the decision given by the country; and, therefore, rMr. Smith and I have no power to settle what the issues are going to be at the next election; and, what is more, friends, we have no right. (Hear, hoar.) Fpr us to come and attempt to dictate to the people of Warrington the precise issue or issues on which the next election'is to be fought would be nothing less, in my judgment, than a piece of the grossest impertinence—(A Voice: "Sure enough Fl—and I should think it no less ludicrous than imper-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>us" in Ulster. (Laughter.) I will just read you four or Ave of these verses:</p>
        <p>"I'd like to lay a mild half-crown With any child of sporting habits, That Orangemen will not go down Like rabbits. . (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>"Deluded hooligans and roughs , May have to go to quad for arson, But blood will never speck the cuffs Of Carson. Laugh ter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>"At Walton Hall"</head>
        <p>"At Walton Hall"</p>
        <p>(Loud laughter.) Perhaps, friends, I ought to remind Mr. Swift Mac Neill that we are expect- ing a very large contingent of, I Am sure, very worthy ladies .from Ulster—(laughter)---personally conducted by the Member for Warrington, who no doubt will have his hands and arms full on that oceitsion. (Loud laughter.) We are favoured with the presence of many ladles to-night; and I want to know if they can tell us if they have seen any very active preparations going on' in the households of the lady Tariff Reformers of Warrington; .whether or not special preparation is being made at Walton Hall. • That brings me back to my verses</p>
        <p>"At Walton Hall no beds will air, . (Loud laughter.) No Amazons will come from Ulster To fling at us with frenzied glare, • One bolster. (Laughter.) "And yet, I fear, I do confess, Some peppery volunteer may be So wrath as to burn F.E.S. In eff-ee-gee. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>"But this will pass. The wise will own, . While babes are born and trade increases, Peace better is than being blown To pieces." (Loud and prolonged laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GLOOMY VIEW.</head>
        <p>THE GLOOMY VIEW.</p>
        <p>I think that is a sounder view of the situa- tion. (Hear, hear.) I have quoted a *ord or two from Mr. Smith, who is trying to harrow us with those gloomy references to Ulster, but I refuse to take, and I ask you to refuse to fake, a gloomy view of it. My time is up—(Voices: "No, no ")—and I do not want to stand between our gifted friend and you, and yet there is one story which I would like to tell him., (Voices: "Go on.'!) I have just three minutes left, and I can do it in the time. I am sorry to say it may be a chestnut to some of you, but not to all, and I Would like my friend (Mr. Mac Neill) to hear it. It is about taking a gloomy view of things. Well, our old friend, Mr. MacMinnies— (applause)—was once going to a funeral in Scot- land. This was long ago, in the early days of "The Warrington Examiner," which, as you know, is going ahead so splendidly now—(ap- plause)—in those early days Mr. McMinnies and my father used to take it in turn, week by week, to write the leading articles, and my father many a timd showed me a letter he. received. dated from Carlisle Station, from our dear old friend, Mr. McMinnies. In the. course of the letter he explained that my father would have to take an extra turn of leader writing; his absence from Warrington prevented him from doing Mint work. Mr. McMinnies went on and said: "I am going down to the funeral of a relative, which is to take place to-morrow, on a hillside in Scotland, near a place called New Cumnock. Thou never heard of it before, and thou will never hear of. it again—(laughter)—all I will say about New Cumnock, if there is an Old Cumnock, God help the people that live there. (Laughter.) As to the funeral to-mor- row, I am sure that Scotch hillside will be as cold and wet, and that Scotch parson as cold and dry, as Scotch hillsides at this season of -the year, and Scotch parsons at all seasons are. (Laughter.) Ile will have neither compunction nor compassion. (Laughter.) He will offer up a long prayer; he will deliver a longer exhorta- tion, and he will conclude with a longer ser- mon still, on the brevity and uncertainty of human life. My relative was 92." (Loth.' laugh- ter.) Mr. McMinnies wound up the letter: "Thine till death—that is, till to-morrow." (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ENOUGH BURLESQUE.</head>
        <p>ENOUGH BURLESQUE.</p>
        <p>But he survived the funeral and lived for many years after. His gloomy view was not justified; and I tell Mr. Smith that his gloomy view of the future of Ulster is not justified either. I will tell him from this platform that we have had enough of this political burlesque, which may be comical, but is also mischievous. I sugtest, *friends, that we turn ,our eyes away from that ,unedifying .spectacle to the prospect of something better and more profitable. Because, ladies and gentlemen, a far brighter prospect is before us, and even now the first few lines in a new chapter in the history of the relations between Great Britain and Ireland have been written. As time goes on, and the pages of that chapter are turned, we may looli *forward ,to' the nations moving .- on like the majestic sweep of some noble river to a more spacious, a more enlightened, more beneficent, and more blessed destiny. (Loud and prolonged cheering.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>" TOM-TIT " POLITICIAN.</head>
        <p>" TOM-TIT " POLITICIAN.</p>
        <p>Irish Protestants and Catholics Live in Concord When Allowed To Do So.</p>
        <p>MR. SWIFT MACNEILL'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>MT. SWIFT MACNEILL, in the course of a stir- rihg address, said: When I look at this great meeting I think Mr. Harold Smith may bid. good-bye to Warrington. (Applause.) Speak- ing about the challenges, Mr. Mac Neill said: I think Mr. Crosfield was over generous. It is plain he took too much notice of Mr. Smith. I hope he will not do it again. (Laughter.) I was not going to say much about Mr. Smith, and I will tell you why. Mr. Smith is a kind of a moon to an F.E. sun—(laughter)--and a kind of 'a Man Friday to an elder Robinson Crusoe brother. (Laughter.) He is a kind of appendage they always have in great political families. We have had in my experience several Cecils and Cccilettes, half-brothers and brothers-in-law, sons and sons-in-law, uncles and nephews. that have no parallel sinee the time of Noah's Ark—(laughter)—and why in heaven's name, if we have a Smith, why can't</p>
        <p>we have "a little" Smithie, too? (Loud laughter.) Mr. Harold Smith has challenged our Chair- man. I do not see why he should, except that Mr. Smith is a near rclative to that eminent military authority—the. great' horseman and galloper—(loud laughter)—who is, God help him, backing, this time, a very wrong horse. (Laughter and applause.) It reminds me .that when O'Connell- was challenged by a _ tom-tit politician of the day, O'Connell said to him: " My dear boy; I would not fight you with bul- rushes." (Laughter.) I commend that observa tion, with all respect, to the Chairman. Thi challenge is by this time an "off " event. Hom Rule is as good as won. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>KEEPER OF THE CARSON CONSCIENC Mr. Smith .said Sir Edward Carson said-1 Smith ,is .evidently Sir Edward Carson's Lo, Chancellor, he is keeper of . the Carson, con science—(laughter)—that no constitutional monarch, and therefore not. our' present King, could ever give his assent to the .present Bill, and wcq.se still,.that he; Mr. Smith, shared that view. (Laughter.) I examine, in the National University, young men on constitutional law, and if a boy, a law student of six weeks' stand- ing, presented to me an answer such as Sir Edward Carson's belief, endorsed by Mr. Harold Smith, I am a merciful man, but I would pluck him, pluck him. (Loud laughter.) It is amuii- ing, but it has a serious side. The King's name is too sacred, he represents too great principles, too high and exalted, to have his name bandied about in party warfare. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>A PROTESTANT CHAMPION OF CATHOLIC TOLERANCE.</p>
        <p>Touching upon the religious _question, Mr. Mac Neill said: No man of the Protestant faith, or of any faith, wil&amp;bp hurt, or persecuted, or handicapped, or 'even socially ostracised, because of his faith, and to a maw who says that, I tell him he is a. conscientious liar. (Ap- plause.) You have heard a great deal of Pro- testant Ulsfer being so much afraid of her faith being endangered. Of Protestants in Ireland, about 11 per cent. are in absolute favour of Home Rule.. It is quite certain that in North- East Ulster John Devlin—(applause)—was elected .by a large Catholic electorate, but it was the Protestant vote that made his majority. At the present Moment the majority of the Members kir Ulster are Nationalists. I am a Nationalist, and what is more, I am a great deal better Protestant than Sir Edward Carson. My father was a Protestant clergy- man, my grandfather was a Protegtant clergy- man, my uncle was a bishop, and I am the nearest living relative of Dean Swift, so there is plenty of Protestantism about 'me. (Ap- plause.) How have they persecuted me? They have got me, in and -made me their member'for seven and twenty years, for one of the most Catholic constituencies in all Ireland. (ip- . plause.) . Those people who speak so much about their Protestantism—not many of them are any great exaMples of enormous religious devotions—were described by Edmund Burke, who knew that . class of Protestants, as "Persons who do not go to church, but damn the Pope." (Hear,, hear, and applause.) Protestantism in certain small minds means belonging to a privilege caste or sect, and you can see something of it in England when a very successful family leave the. little Bethel for the great Established Church. It means caste not faith, it means if . a man professes a certain creed he is to\ have a preferential tariff over the rest of the people. (Laughter.) We want to sweep away those factitious distinctions, and give everyone an equal footing in his own land,. and, to give the right hand of brotherhood to every Irishman who comps to live amongst us, (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>le le E. Ird 1-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAYL OCTOBER 25,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>– THIS HAPPY DAY."</head>
        <p>- THIS HAPPY DAY."</p>
        <p>They were not going to lf led astray by all this Carson humbug, and the Irish Parliament would be a national Parliament for the whole of the people, every. Irishman being represented there. (Cheers.) So far from breaking the "Union, this Act would cement the two coun- tries, and the two democracies would do their best for the advancement and freedom of them- selves and the rest of the world. (Cheers.) For thirty years he and the Irish party had followed a hard path, hut he and they would repeat it and gethrough it all again to have the happiness of this day. (Applause.) It was a happy day for every Irishman, because they had got their long-lost rights and the blessed privilege of being able to -manage their own affairs. (Loud cheers.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ONLY COSTUME BARRED.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY COSTUME BARRED.</p>
        <p>Mrs. CROSFIELD, who was loudly cheered, made an announcement respecting the Women Liberals' ball, to be held on the following night, and said, -amid laughter, that the only dress which would be debarred from winning a prize would be that of an Ulster volunteer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>QUID PRO QUO.</head>
        <p>QUID PRO QUO.</p>
        <p>LORD DERBY WILL HAVE "NO TRUCK" WITH DRAGGING IN THE KING'S NAME.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. WYNNE JONES proposed a resolution, welcoming the Home Rule Bill as a just, saga- cious and adequate solution of the Irish ques- tion, and recording the conviction that it should be placed on thq Statute Book. He announced an apology from Mr. P. M Oliver, the prospective Liberal candidate fk the Knutsford Division, who wrote: "Certainly, if, in 1915, Warrington is to 13, crowded with the mothers of Ulster, Dublin wi: be crowded with the English well-wishers Ireland, who will be there to see King GA-1;k V. open, in person, his first Parliament in Col- lege Green." (Loud applause.) Lord Derby told them the other day-7-and Mr. Harold Smith was there, and had to listen to it —" I am having no truck with the dragging of the King's name into this business." (Ap- plause.) Lord Derby also said that it was the duty of the King to be advised by his Ministers, and as the Home Rule Bill passed the House of Commons the King would give his consent to it, and as a result put into Ireland that great measure of liberty. (Applause.) Mr. Smith had devoted himself to trying to persuade the people of Warrington that he was sincere. If a man was sincere, like the gentle- men who had spoken to them that night, there was no need to, ask the people to believe he was sincere. (Hear, hear.) They could not believe a man was sincere who told the "Express" people in London that he had 6,000 members of the Tariff Reform League in Warrington, whilst the official organ in Warrington told them that there were only 2,500. (Laughter.) A man like that could not be sincere. Mr. Mac Neill had referred to the appointment of Mr. F. E. Smith as galloper. They would see, at the next elec- tion, that Harold was appointed galloper, and that he galloped from Warrington. (Ap- plause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded, and the resolution was carried with one dissentient. Mr. Mac Neill's comment about the latter was "Poor man."</p>
        <p>:or ie Lii if</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD A CONSISTENT HOME RULER.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD A CONSISTENT HOME RULER.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. LLOYD JONES proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the speakers and chairman. After listening to the excellent reply to ,the Member by Mr. Crosfield, he felt 'sorry that W. Smith had not been spending the evening with them, because it would have done him very much good. There was no doubt about the fact that, not- withstanding the inconsistencies they found in the Member, ever since Mr. -Crosfield entered the political arena of Warrington he had been most consistent on the Irish question. (Hear, hear.) . The Irish had no greater friend outside the House of Commons than Mr. Crosfield. (Hear, hear.) Not only was Mr. Crosfield con- sistent with regard to the issues that had been settled, but he was one of the most ardent sup- porters of the questions that were coming along. (Applause.) Mr. G. \IVALIMR • seconded, and the resolution wag curried with euthugiasm.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>WHAT BOUT TUT WINTER OVERCOAT?</p>
        <p>Have you given it a thought yet? Remember the great thing in this weather is to keel) 'warm, and the easiest way to do this is to wear one of our Famous WINTER OVERCOATS. They are a</p>
        <p>real comfort, and the prices are a lithe less than you would pay elsewhere. Why not call and see them?</p>
        <p>THOMAS &amp; SON, 57, 1101-ZSEMARKLT ST.,</p>
        <p>WARRiNi,iTJN.</p>
        <p>Horses thaVount and Spell</p>
        <p>• Mr., Edviard Bulloigh, Fellow of Gotiville and Caius College, Cambridge; and Mr. V: J. Wool- ley, M.D., have just' reported to the Psychical Research Society on their visit to Elberfeld in Germany to Inquire into the "thinking horses" of Mr. K. Krall. Mr. Krall explaintd •to his horses all the rudimentary proceduye of addi- tion, subtraction, multiplication, and: division, as II() would to tt child, and was'greatly aston- ished at the ease and quiCkness of their under- standing-4-for instance, in grasping the differ- ence between 3 by 2 and sby 2. With the aid of a simplified spelling-table the horses learnt to spell, each letter being represented by a num- ber. The spelling showed a tendency to omit, vowels. What do the Elberfeld horse § "think"' about the motor? ,</p>
        <p>The Genesis of the Yarmouth filloater.</p>
        <p>Probably most. readers know that a bloater is\ a Complete herring .slightly salted and smoked,_ .Not eVeryone; however, . may be.-aware of the genesis of tills article of diet, whiCh is given in an article in the September "Young Man" by Mr. T. E. Freeman. It was accidental.. A Yar- mouth herring-curer, when leaving his premises. one night after all his workpeople had , gone, folind a quantity of good herrings which had been overlooked. Fearing they would be spoiled- if left as the,y were, he springled them with salt and hung them in. his "smoke-house," in which oak-billet was then being burned. Next m'Orning it was evident that his experi- ment had been successful. lie began to special- ise in this direction, an example which WOO speedily • followed by others, until the fame of the bloater was establisned in the land.</p>
