      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>Even Manchester could not boast of finer fabrics or more exclusive designs. If you went to the most expensive tailors in Man- chester they could not show you better. Coming as they do from the foremost mill, in Britain, each one separately tested and examined by our cloth expert before being added to our extensive ranges, they are the pick of the weavers' art, and absolutely inimitable br beauty of design, choice colouring, and excellent hard-wearing quality. Yet their prices are not exorbitant.</p>
        <p>We will make to your measure perfect in fit and faultlessly tailored,</p>
        <p>The OVERCOAT from 45/- The LOUNGE SUIT from so/-</p>
        <p>Boyden Brothers, The Home of Fine Tailoring, 43, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>ksa-</p>
        <p>Ir.telsk %gmb. WlRik NelakAk•Vitai Nagift, ViiPk ViEilk-lo</p>
        <p>the great lie that is being told in the sacred name of religion—he is a Protestant, like Parnell and many more, chosen, by Catholics as well as Protestants, to fight the national cause of freedom without reference to creed. Never was there anything more eloquently or convincingly put than this point. Said Mr.</p>
        <p>I am a Nationalist, and, what is more, I am a great deal better Protestant than Sir Edward Carson. My father was a Protestant clergy- man, my grandfather was a Protestant clergy- man, my uncle was a bishop, and I am the nearest living relative of Dean Swift, so there is plenty of Protestantism about me. (Applause.) How have they persecuted me? They have got me in and made me their Member for seven and twenty years, for one of the most Catholic con- stituencies in all Ireland. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
      </div>
