      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>" MISS HOOK OF HOLLAND."</head>
        <p>" MISS HOOK OF HOLLAND."</p>
        <p>There was a great display in the way of pre- sents for our local amateurs at the Royal Court Theatre last Saturday night, when the curtain was rung down for the last time upon the week's performance of "Miss Hook of Hol- land." It would be tedious to mention all who were remembered, for the whole company seemed to be favourites. The house was crowded, and the takings reached the record stage since the society acted at the theatre. The-accounts have not yet been made up, but thet financial result will be quite satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MR. R. IT. PEARSON.</head>
        <p>MR. R. IT. PEARSON.</p>
        <p>It will be a surprise to many to know that Mr. Pearson, who Was at his accustonied place as leader of the Musical Society Orchestra on Saturday night, was critically ill by Sunday at noon. Dr. Langdale was called in, .and, fortunately, we learnt lust • night, that the Patioril was making - satisfactory progress towards recovery. The whole town will be pleased, to know this, for Mr. Pearson is deservedly, esteemed by a very large circle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>Every couple is not a pair.—Proverb. The milk of human kindness is generally Watered. by .self-interest.—" The .Tatler."</p>
        <p>There are times when a man wishes that Adam had died with all the ribs in his body. All women are good—good for nothing or good for something.—Cervantes. Never make a man regret owning up ; it is more blessed to forgive than be deceived. To say "Everyone is talking about him" is a eulogy, but to say "Everyone is talking about her" is an elegy. It is quite possible to grind a man down with- out making him sharp. The brightness of many a young man con- sists mainly of waistcoats and neckties.</p>
        <p>Have You?</p>
        <p>Have you ever noticed that an extreme pessi- mist always makes you feel optimistic, while an extreme optimist always makes you feel pessimistic?</p>
        <p>ihe Was Safe. At a local concert this week an attendant's voice was heard to command the ladies to take off their hats, whereupon an old Irish lady ex- claimed "Oh, sure, 1111 safe; mine's a bonnet !"</p>
        <p>Difficult to Please.</p>
        <p>A local farmer has been grumbling because his crops have been so heavy that. he has had to engage three Odra men. Surely we Britons are an ungrateful lot</p>
        <p>Somewhere.</p>
        <p>"It is'better tO be last than nowhere," was a morsel of worldly wiSdom Colonel Trimble handed out at the Crosfield Ambulance Shield Competition on Saturday to those who had not won the trophy.</p>
        <p>At the Opera. Father: I wonder why people think it neces- sary to "dress" to come to see amateurs in comic opera? Mother: I don't quite understand why it is. Young lady hopeful: Well, you know, they can be seen in the stalls.</p>
        <p>"Birds" at the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>When Dr. Nightingale was appointed ,assist- ant medical officer, Mr. Mather said: "We shall soon have a colony of birds-here ; we have Peacocks, Partridges, Woodcocks, and now we Shall have a Nightingale." "Birds of passage" often visit the institution.</p>
        <p>Mr. Nfather's Questions. Mr. Mather was in an inquisitive and facetious humour at the Guardians meeting on 'Wednescray. A reservist applied for a position as attendant, and Mr. Mather asked what a reservist was. Would he have tv go out if there was a War called between England and Ireland? —" What do they mean by hygiene; is it any- thing to do with whisky?" he asked another candidate.—To the Rev.. A. M. Mitchell he said, " You said something just now about angels and archangels. What is the difference?" Mr. Mitchell: "Archangels are chief angels." Mr. Mather: "Like bosses."—Mr. Mather 'once said something before about a "boss," didn't he?</p>
        <p>SILVER LININGS.</p>
        <p>What do I care if it's cloudy, Itlifditing the surt,,hine for me; Life has to have a few shadows,</p>
        <p>Sometimes its turbulent sea. When I reach home in the twilight, Sombre-lined clouds fade away, When Marie hugs Me and N‘llispers, "Baby was good girl to-day."</p>
        <p>NA•liat do 1 care if Illy lOilinff WParie‘, 111 V (Wring the day, Some II:IN e 10 bear extra burdens So that the others may play. My little home is a haven, Happiness there has full sway, Cares disappear with the greeting; "Baby was good girl to-day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SMOKE CONTROL.</head>
        <p>SMOKE CONTROL.</p>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR THE CANDIDATES.</p>
        <p>The newly-formed. Smoke Abatement League have addressed the following questions to the candidates at the forthcoming municipal elec- tions :</p>
        <p>1. Are you in favour of the Borough Council exercising supervision of the smoke arising from all works' fireplaces and furnaces under its juris- diction, in accordance with the powers entrusted to it, and the obligations placed upon it by the Public Health Act, 1875, Sections 91, 92, 93, 94, 95 and 334?</p>
        <p>2. Are you in favour of the consistent en- forcement of the local by-law limiting the emission of black smoke to five minutes for the half-hour, or three consecutive minutes? 3. If elected, will you give favourable con- sideration to any proposal which may be brought forward during your term of office to reduce the Warrington time limit for the emission of black smoke from ,power pla7lts to a point at which it will correspond with the time limits in force in other progressive towns? Note to 2 and, 3.—The time limit for the em is- sion of black , smoke for the half-hour in War- rington is five minutes, or three minutes con- secutive. This (for one boiler) is in excess of almost every other town in the country. '1 he average limit is slightly over three minutes for the half-hour, and this limit has been proved by experiedce to be more than ample for any power plant which is properly arranged and worked. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD V. SMITH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., has not had long to wait for a reply from Mr. Crosfield to his ill- considered challenges. The Member left the Borough a fortnight ago 'a much sadder and wiser man than he entered it for his Anti-Home Rule Campaign. We venture to think that when next he pays us-a visit the severe treat- ment he has received at the hands of his opponent this week will lie somewhat heavily upon his conscience. Mr. Crosfield, who spoke for thi•eequarters of an hour, as our report will show, again demonstrated that ,he has few betters at ranging facts 'and stating .his case in a convincing way. Of course, he had really very little to answer, Mr. Smith having made a very desperate plunge and left himself naked to his opponent. But Mr. Crosfield, having been provided with such a grand opening, pro- ceeded to administer the knock-out blow, which he did with admirable completeness. When a politician invites another to fight him two years hence on an issue that has been settled and will be completely cast aside long before two years are over, giving way to other vital and pressing matters, he does something that savours of the ridiculous. Mr. Crostield had only to ask his audience to imagine him- self and Mr. Smith fighting on Home Rule in Warrington, while the rest of the country were earnestly fighting on land reform, and the many vital questions that are dependent upon land. The thing is too absurd for words—</p>
        <p>almost as absurd as inviting women, who hu\-, covenanted to stay by the side of .their hus- bands in their extreme need, to come to War- rington—or to Walton—out of the noise. Mr. Crosfield simply refused to accept this challenge and so become a party to making Warrington into a laughing-stock. Since the Liverpool style of electioneering has been introduced here we have had enough of that kind of thing, and it is not for Liberals to assist the process further.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>"BLUFFING."</head>
        <p>"BLUFFING."</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith challenged Mr. Crosfield to say whether Ulster was bluffing, and if she was not, what he proposed to do with the Province. Mr. Crosfield declined to accept the definition Ulster, that would .ask us to believe that the fraction of a fractiOn is the whole, and said, in clear, unmistakable language," that. the -Liberal party will "g 6 right on," willing to concede where concession is justified, but refusing to be diverted- froni the path of duty by a band of irreconcilables, however much they May bluster. He dismissed Mr. Smith with a request for a truce to this burlesque; and the meeting, one of the largest and most enthu- siastic ever held in the Parr Hall; endorsed his attitude with determination and unanimity. Mr. Crosfield'has as much cause to be satisfied at his pro-Home Rule effort as he had to be gratified by the failure of his opponents Anti- Home little efforts.</p>
        <p>A PROTESTANT HOME RULE M.P. FROM ULSTER.</p>
        <p>The success di the meeting 'was largely due to the presence of Mr. Swift Mac Neill, M.P., who, in his own person, proved that "Ulster" does not necessarily mean Orangeism. He sits for an Ulster constituency, and, strange to say, he is one of a majority who sit for Ulster constituencies. tic is a living contradiction of</p>
      </div>
