      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>MR. HAWTHORN</head>
        <p>MR. HAWTHORN</p>
        <p>has made an ideal councillor and is ono of the most promising among its younger members. He was first elected in February, 1907. He is the eldest son of the late Mr. Thomas Hawthorn, who was a tradesman in the Whitecross Ward for 35 years.</p>
        <p>He served his apprenticeship at "The Examiner" Office, and for a number of years was a reporter for this journal, but for the past eight years he has been a member of the staff of the "Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury." In his first contest in Whitecross Ward Mr. Hawthorn defeated Mr. Broadhurst (now Coun- cillor Broadhurst), and in the contest three years ago he defeated Mr. Throssel, the Conser- vative nominee, by 356 votes.</p>
        <p>A man of progressive ideas, he was a pioneer of the movement to introduce motor 'buses, which have proved such a great benefit to the residents in Whitecross, Bewsey and Orford Wards.</p>
        <p>He strongly advocated the purchase by the Corporation of a plot of land in Lovely-lane from Lord Newton for providing additional school accommodation and a recreation ground. The land was secured two years ago. A ,portion of the site has been approved by the Board of Education for a new school, which it is pro- posed to erect next year.</p>
        <p>In June last Mr. Ildwthorn moved a resolution in the Town Council urging the Town Hall, parks and Baths Committee to lay out the re- maining portion of the ground for recreation purposes, and it was unanimously passed. The committee has since made a recommendation to place two bowling greens, tennis courts and gymnasia for children on the site. This breath- ing space in the midst of a thickly populated district is sure to be appreciated. Whitecross Ward is one of the largest wards in the town, and there are over 1,600 electors.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hawthorn is a member of the Health, Sanitary, Works, Paving and Sewerage, and Electricity and Tramways Committees. On many occasions he has shown his sympathy with the workers. He supported the proposal to pay the minimum wage of 245. per week to all able- bodied men employed by the Corporation, and also the proposal to pay all workmen in the employ of the Corporation time and a half for Sunday work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hawthorn took a prominent part in the organisation of the Young Liberals when first the movement was started in WatTington, and for some years was Chairman of the committee. Whitecross Ward will honour itself by again sending such a worker and such a gentleman as Mr. Hawthorn to represent them in the Council. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>THREE CONTESTS.</head>
        <p>THREE CONTESTS.</p>
