        <p>LIBERAL SEAT CHALLENGED.</p>
        <p>The nominations for the, municipal elections in Warrington closed yesterday afternoon. There were no surprises, and thred wards are to have contests. The wards and candidates are : Whitecross</p>
        <p>Joseph Hawthorn (L.) I'. G. Brookes (C.) Arthur Browne (I.) J. Broadhurst (C.) Robert Henshall (I.) A. Ashton (C.) Elected Unopposed.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's</p>
        <p>Latchford</p>
        <p>Orford Ward "John Wilkinson (L.) Town Hall Ward Firth Lindley (L.) Bewsey Ward Tames Skelland (L.) Fairfield Ward James T. Locker (L.) St. John's Ward Dr. McKee (C.) Howley Ward .. George Archer (C.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>' THE NOMINIITINIS.</head>
        <p>' THE NOMINIITINIS.</p>
        <p>7the last day for receiving nominations was yesterday, when the following were handed in:</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>Councillor James T. Locker (re-elected un- opposed) : Proposed by Rev. Canon Willis, seconded by Rev. Canon Stevenson; supported by B. W. Autwiss, J. L. Bate, W. Povey, H. Taylor, A. J. Jones, J. Willett, H. Wilcox, J. Jones. Proposed-by Councillor Craik, seconded by G. W. A. Brown; supported by Rev. G. H. Garnet, S. W. Jeffery, A. Podmore, F. Webster, W. Brant- hall, J. T. Whittle, T. F. Hughes, W. Whittaker. Proposed by • Eleanor Spinks; seconded by Ellen Singleton; supported by Mary Lawrence, Mary Hunt, Annie Fowler, Mary Andrews, Mary Taylor, Margretta Timewell, Elizabeth Nor- cott, Annie Winstanley. Proposed by B. Farrar, seconded by W. Haigh; supported by J. Massey, T. Broadhurst, W. Gallop, B. Jolley, S. Wych, S. Archer, B. Hall, J. Ellison. - • , Proposed .by A. E. Boardman, seconded by W. P. Ashton; supported by P. Hill, A. Gibson, W. Monnfield, J. Booth, J. Hill, T. Chidlaw, W. Hayes, B. Appleton. Proposed by J. Hardilan, seconded by J. C. Newton; supported by A. Kilburn, J. It Vernon, Ws Kilduff. IL Corney, J. P. Haselbtirst, J. ersley, Reardon, A. Ford. Proposed by J. T. Wilson, seconded by S. IL</p>
        <p>Walker; supported by E. Jolley, J. Cliff, W. Butterworth, R. H. nom ard, W. Foster, J. Briggs, C. W. Shaw, J. Ainsworth. • Proposed by H. Maddock, seconded by J. W. Jeffery; supported by J. W. Melbourne, H. Hey, T. Warhurst, T. Baldry, W. J. Birch, G. Crutch- loy, W. Scott, J. Gibson.</p>
        <p>-Proposed by G. T. Rushton, secOnded by J. Delaney; supported by G. Molyneaux, F. How- ard, T. Woods, J. McHugh, J. Edgar, J. Riley, M. Bibby, D. Watson. Proposed by E. Lawrence, seconded by W. Holbrook; supported by J. Birchall, T. J. Hop- kins, T. Weston, IT. V. Rathbone, J. Seed,. A. Pennington, R. Taylor, H. Winstanley. Proposed by P. T. Winskill, seconded by J. Jarman; supported by T. Massey, W. Kershaw, R. Britton, G. Monks, H. Farrar, J. Ratcliffe, W. O'Neill, G. Painter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>COUNCILLOR HENSHALL</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR HENSHALL</p>
        <p>has done more in three yeArs' membership of the Town Council than many men do in thirty years. Pleasant in manner, as full of reliable facts as the sun is full of light, and gifted in a .remarkable degree with -the command of good King's English, the junior representative of the Latchford Ward established himself at the Town Hall as soon as he reached it.</p>
        <p>He is as popular among his constituents in the ward as among his colleagues in the Council Chamber. They have found him to be a man of his word. When he sought their suffrages Ile promised to advocate improved facilities for the use of Victoria Park, the provision of" a suspen- sion bridge at Howley. He also promised to have an eye upon all matters that would lead to improved conditions for child life. He has been most active in the respect of the last- named. As a member of several committees of the Education Authority, be has taken a keen interest in the proper and humane administra- tion of the laws applying to children and their welfare, and his experience has been of marked value to the town in the building of new schools. It is common knowledge that Victoria Park has been improved, a bowling green added, and the bridge erected.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henshall has a live programme. At the present time he is the prime mover in a scheme that will make Victoria Park extend up to the bank of the canal. It will thus be in- creased from 55 acres ta.,,E,8 acres;. asd will, it is hoped, allow of the land being devoted to golf for the working classes. Councillor Hen- shall hopes to have a lake in the same grounds for boating and swimming. This is of special interest to his own people. Taking, a wider scope, Councillor Henshall is playing a leading part in an endeavour that is being made to secure some measure of relief from the pressure of the education rate. Since the Education Act came into operation the cost upon the local rates has increased about 250 per cent., and the contribution in aid made by. the National Ex- chequer has increased by leas than 100 per cent. An adjustment is needed here. Education should be a national and not a local charge, and Councillor Henshall is tireless in his demand for a re-adjustment that will make the division of the burden between the local rates and the Imperial taxes more equitable. He has I' lso championed the cause of the tramwaymen by demanding screens and refreshment places for their use.</p>
        <p>We cannot think that Latchford will desire to part with a representative who has done so much and who is in the midst of important pub- lic work that a change would bring to an end. Councillor Henshall, outside his public work, is known as a sincere Sunday School and Tem- perance worker, and an eloquent preacher in the Independent Methodist Connexion. He is head of the firm of Robert Henshall and Co., who, 20 years ago, established a business in Latchford of preserved provision merchants. This trade has brospered in a remarkable way, and to-day is in some respects the biggest of its kind in England, a valuable connection having been built up in the States and Argentina. There- are 1,800 electors in Latchford, most of whom must say, as they will • next Saturday, that Councillor Henshall is on all counts worthy of a continuance of that confidence with which they favoured him three years ago.</p>
      </div>
