      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Mayor in London.</head>
        <p>Mayor in London.</p>
        <p>_ 06 'Thursday evening the Mayor and Mayoress jWarrington were present at the Mansion -4Mise, London, on the occasion of the Lord •MaYor's annual ball.</p>
        <p>An Unlucky Slip. dimes Warburton (40), of 30, Warburton- i*reet, Warrington, whilst following his employ- nent at Messrs. Walker's Brewery, slipped and *lad the misfortune to break his leg, lie was t'nVeyed to the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Liberal Cueists.</head>
        <p>Liberal Cueists.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Club on Wednesday defeated the League of the Cross at billiards by 542 points to G. Simpson made the best. break of 41. 'Ole Liberal Club on Thursday defeated the -Junior Liberals by 818 points- to 775. n Pier- 'Point made a break of 48.</p>
        <p>nuttermarket-street Improvemtnts. . Alderman Bennett, at Warrington Council meeting on Tuesday, announced that the nego- tiations for the purchase of the necessary .pro 7 Party in Buttermarket4treet had been con-, (*hided, and the improvements would Dow go forward.</p>
        <p>Bandsmen's Appreciation. The bandsmen of the local ,Territorial Batt a- , lion of the South Lancashire Regiment last vvening held a dinner and • smoker in Atkin- . ,40n's Cafe, and presented Mrs. Elgood, wife of Adjutant Elgood, who is retiring, with a framed. . nhotograph of the band. Lieut.-Coionel Fair- vlough made the presentation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>I e Saturday Train.</head>
        <p>La I e Saturday Train.</p>
        <p>At present much. inconvenience is experienced owing to the fact that the last train on Satur- 'gay night from Warrington to Crewe is as early 4,ts 7.25. As will be seen from an advertisement 'n another column, to-night a train will leave Warrington for Crewe at 10 o'clock, calling at itoore and other intermediate stations. The train will be a permanent one on Saturdays, if it is sufficiently patronised by the travelling</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Growth of Infirmary Work.</head>
        <p>Growth of Infirmary Work.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the manager; of the Infirmary neld on Wednesday, after Dr. J.'Lutal; had been 4lipointed honorary pathologist, Mr. L. H. filiethurst said the in-patients would show an hicrease this year of 20 per cent. Operations 41ready numbered 736, against 587 for the whole "f 1912. He appealed to employers and 'liplcryes to combine to see that the Infirmary, the work of which was as necessary as ever, old not suffer for the want of funds.</p>
        <p>A "Working Man's" Query. The Mayor desires to state that he has this '4ek received an anonymous communication, igned "Working Man," appealing for advice In, regard to the proposal to raise rents within re borough. His Worship regrets very much °that the Matter is one in which, whilst his sym- l'athies are with those who are already suffering Ofli poverty, he feels unable to offer advice. feels sure, however,, that the landlords of warrington will not be harsh in cases of dis- tress brought to their notice.</p>
        <p>Warrington Plillomathic Society. . At a meeting of the above on Wednesday even- 'Z. the question was discussed—" Does the pre- !quit individualistic system tend to destroy Lndividuality?" The discussion was opened by -;11'. R. M. Monk, who was followed by Messrs. F. 12liadwick, E. G. Burgess, Dr. S. A. Mellor, N. Nield, A. Hodgkinson, and Dr. J. Bamforth. it "a 5 decided that, the issue having become in- -I"°lred in considerable chaos, the question'should iv left open, and that the discussion should, if sible, lie resumed at a fUture date. The lee-president, Mr. W. B. Forshaw. was in the</p>
        <p>A Suffrage .Concert. Under the auspices of the Warrington branch r)f the Women's Suitrage Societies' Lin excellent Concert was given in the Co-operative Hall last 'light There was a good 'attendance and a tiost entertaining programme was gone through. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, who is an able pianist, delighted the audience with her pianoforte solo. 1!-rs. Haviour's violin solos were much appre- :lated, whilst the monologues by Miss Corney :ere delightful. Mr. H. M. Ashton created i'leThY a laugh with his character sketches. Mr. (1. Ford (songs) and Miss Emily Taylor (songs) contributed to an entertaining evening. 148 Clarice Newell was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>, Death of Mr. T. Stringer. 141/Le death occurred under tragic circuill- ,l'ances, at Frodsham Railway Station on Wed- of Mr. T. Stringer, of Wilderspool- w'lseway, Warrington. Mr. Stringer was a fd-known local contractor, and on Wednesday trlilling had been engaged ou work in connec- ,t" %iith the Runcorn Rural District Council Netherton. After having -refreshments, he ied for the train, Sand died on the platform. wa addition to being a Freemason, Mr. Stringer :Gs Et prominent member of the St. John's esbYterian Church, where his activities were thi,',l appreciated. He leaves a widow and five 44. !dren. At the inquest yesterday a verdict Death from heart failure" was returned.</p>
        <p>e, Preachers for To-morrow. .1;iro-street Chapel—Morning and evening, t'• Mellon liold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. E. Mor- ; evening, Rev. IT. Williamson. wsey-road Wesleyan—Morning. *Rev. • H. lAdlewood; evening, Rev. E. Morgan. c. • t.• 'netadelphians—Evening, Mr. J. P. Jen- Warrington. ()eiety of Friends—Evening, Trances Thonip . Birkenhead. ih!'atchfcird Baptist Church—Morning and Zing, Rev. J. S. Chedburn. ‘E, "L John's Presbyterian Church—Morning and vo, • e°ng, Rev. W. Reid. in,lborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- H. Ogden. - el.- 'Yeti&amp; Congregationalist-Morning and Lflg Rev. T. T. James.</p>
        <p>4 Natiotial Union of Liberal Clubs. the half-yearly meeting of the Lancashire ! 4.:iptition of the al-Tye union, this afternoon. tle'ses will be delivered by Mr. A. IT. Cros- Mr. J. B. Martindale. Prominence •"1 the report to the land question-. 'rh!! Oaf -Do '-'litish land monopoly," it says, "is a • Moribund ; we want to hasten its end .and D{4.x:e,taate its carcase, as quickly as mti. be ' .‘lt lime." Mr. Lloyd George's noble measure -I'e,-,4(/i-al reform—National Insurance—adds the 4A,1/I.t. "is justifying itself more and more with daY that passes. Coupled up with ti le 4%.l4°rable Land Valuation Budget of 1909, _O.ll- ,4 Yensions, Home Rule, Welsh Disestablsl- 414i',c. and Anti-Plural Voting, it, promises to i 41tlIce 111?-an.unParalleled record of splendici work bese`',W by the present Government in real. ior the _Liberal party's fourth successt.e% ,01Rltriumph in 1915." ' AV., VTington Photographic Socie!Y- -I\-„ki-,l;e Old Halls of Lancashire and Cheshire" 'hyl"le subject of Tuesday's lecture at the ezIlY, Bridge-street. It had been prepared lilt, tr., T. Burton, of Oldham, and, as the lec- lit hinted, must have cost years of ardent illiwr°l and photographic effort. The hundred Lrations were superb in colour, in detail, *tlie °f,ten in pictorial value, and portrayed all qr .€lll°st celebrated of the ancient homesteads counties. Speke Hall. Hall o' th, ley"' IN orsley Hall, Moreton, Brainliall, Chor- lhuhAdlington, are only a few of the half- gems well .within reach of the War- 'txti4l eamerist, and if leave can also be ob- le,Cd to photograph the interiors, a rare col- No:r1 of beautiful.negatives might be obtained. oj!ie,.! ef these beauties hare only recently been ‘Thi;'vered under coats .of paint or plaster, !titer others, so the lecturer said, have just as been covered up with hideous modern hie:t ,y,t'ric• All lionnar to the men and women sue who 'spend so much .in preserving •RizxstiDerials so valuable. Mr. Burton was enthu- ' al -1S thanked for his lecture at the close. RANGES, FIRE GRATES, TlLES.—Large .410,1"1 reasoble nrices.—J. S. GARLANnaD, Iron- -51 to 55, Bridgd-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Shakespearean Society. . The first meeting of the session of the War- rington Shakespearean Society was held in the Church Rooms; Bridge-street, on Thursday evening. The Rev. L. J. T. Darwall presided over a moderate attendance. Two excellent readings were given by.. Miss Gladys Adams and Mr. H. M. Ashton from f` The Taming of the Shrew" and " Romeo and Juliet.' Mr. A. Perris gave a paper on " King Lear."</p>
        <p>Bank Quay Bowling Club. The Bank Quay Bowling Club brought the season to a close on Monday evening, when a. hot-pot supper, provided by Mr. and Mrs. Camp- 4ell, was 'partaken of in the club room at the Bowling Green Hotel, Liverpool-road. The President, Mr. Frederick T. Bennie, presided, and was supported by many _vice-presidents and the honorary secretary, Mr. William E. Clare. Almost. 50 members sat down to an excellent. supper, after which an assorted musical pro- gramme was gone through the artistes being Mr.'Ernest Sanders, Mr. *alter Sutton, Mr. John Houghton,, Mr. Peter Jenks, Mr. Fred Wolfenden, and Mr. John Houltram. The winner of the main was Mr. John Stewart, who beat Mr. John Gleave in the final by 13-10.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Motor 'Bus Accident.</head>
        <p>Motor 'Bus Accident.</p>
        <p>. Coming Events. To-day—Nursing 'Association Annual Meeting. Wednesday—Musical Society concert. . Thursday—Latchford Baptist Sale of Work, f1.:30 p.m.</p>
        <p>Stepping from the footpath in Horsemarket- street, on Saturday evening, Harold Glover, 25, Birchall-street, Warrington, was knocked down by a Corporation motor 'bus. He received a broken ankle and was detained in the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Died At His Work.</head>
        <p>Died At His Work.</p>
        <p>An old man of 65, Abel Mather, glassmaker, 7, Paradise-place, Warrington, employed at Messrs. Robinson, Son, and Skinner's Glass- works, collapsed on Wednesday morning and expired almost immediately. He had been under the care of Dr. Smith for some time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Parish Church Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Parish Church Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>This important annual event takes place next week in Fairfield Schools, Manchester-road. Mrs. Lyon will perform the opening ceremony on Thursday, Mrs. John Crosfield will similarly officiate on Friday, and on Saturday Mrs. John Whi,tley will open the proceedings. The pro- ceeds of the sale, which is one of the most popu- lar of its kind, are devoted to the various parish funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Licensing Compensations.</head>
        <p>Licensing Compensations.</p>
        <p>At a supplementary meeting of the Compensa- tion Authority at Warrington yesterday morn- ing, Messrs. Grednall; Whitley and Co. claimed in respeict of two deferred licences—Railway Tavern, .23,970 us. 6d., and the King's Arms, £1,70. The following were the awards: Rail- way Tavern, .23,838 us. Gd., and Air. W. E. Long- more (tenant), .2255; King's Arms, .21,650, and Mr. It Hankinson (manager) agreed to accept 1;20.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <p>THE BEST REPORTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lister-Kaye's Tribute to "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Another tribute to the excellent reports given, not only to local, but to country and district meetings, by-" The Examiner," was forthcoming at .the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday. Mr. , Spruce complainej. that a certain Warrington paper did not report the pro- ceedings fully, and he said: "We feel very much disappointed. I was wishing that some representation could be made to the conductors of the paper as to the main- tenance of a service of reports, so that we who happen to be absent can see what has. been going on." Mr. A. Lister-Kaye: You will always se.e a full account in "The Warrington Examiner." •</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling: The other paper ought to be written to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Mayor-Fleet and the Buffaloes.</head>
        <p>Mayor-Fleet and the Buffaloes.</p>
        <p>Councillor and Bro. Peter Peacock, the Mayor- elect, paid a visit to his mother lodge of the R.A.0.8. Order, held in the Market Hotel, and was accorded a hearty welcome. The various lodges in Warrington and also the Knights of the Golden Horn paid a special regalia. Bro. Peacock, who was introduced by Bro. James Walsh, K.0.M., was accompanied by Bros. Chrimes and the following Knights of the Order: T. Davies, J. W. Taylor, A. Roberts, T. Cooper, W. Bradbury, W. Miller, C. Gleave, Jos. Wil- kinson, W. H. Smalley, J. Holden and J. H. R. Pontefract. Sarmony was rendered by Bros. W. Chadwick, C.P., T. Davies, K.0.M., J. Wil- kinson, K.0.M., C. Gleave, ,K.0.M., and others. The toast of the Mayor-elect was given by the Worthy Primo, and Bros.sChrimes and A. Mas- se. A pleasant evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>STRIKE AVERTED,</head>
        <p>STRIKE AVERTED,</p>
        <p>Local Iron Work Firms Concede Wage Increase.</p>
        <p>LABOURERS' SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>It will come as a surprise to most Warring- tonians to learn that until a late hour last night they were threatened with a strike, which would have paralysed • the chief industries of the town, and Amid have proved very serious to the whole community. The danger was fortunately averted last night. For a considerable [line the labourers con- nected with the iron works of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles , and the Monks, Hall Company. as stated in "The Examiner" previously, have been seeking an advance in wages and shorter hours. Delays have arisen and this week the patience of the men has reached breaking point. *Yesterday the employers consented to make concessions, and this obviated a strike, which would, in a few days, have affected up- wards of 3,000 men, and the agreement was signed by both parties.</p>
        <p>The agreement was submitted to a mass meeting of the members of the Union of Gas Workers and General Labourers, who were in the forefront of the agitation, at the Socialist Hall last evening, and approved of unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>NEV CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NEV CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>Tile men's representatives, Messrs. C. Dukes and Fleming Eccles, claimed for a 235. minimum per week. The masters have offered, and it has been accepted, a 225. minimum for a 53-hour week, to come into operation on Mon- day. On the Ist May .next the men come also under the scale of the • Manchester District Engineering Trade Employers' Federation, and will thus gain further benefits in the future, while they have been conceded a point on the question of overtime, which may bring the wages up to 235. per week.</p>
        <p>" We have shown a conciliatory spirit," said Mr. Dukes to The Examiner" representalive late last night, "all through the negotiations, but we•were dangerously near a strike, and it has taxed all our energies to keep the men in check. A .strike would have stopped all the iron works, and ,000 men at least would have been affected.</p>
        <p>WORK OF THREE YEARS.</p>
        <p>' It is three - years since the Gas Workers' Union started to organise, and we have about 3,000 members. In that time the iron work labourers' wages have been • advanced from 17s. 10d. for 581 hours to 225., with 5} hours less work per week: The overtime rates, which come into operation under the Manchester scale in May, means ,at least ,another increase of is. per week, if not more, for overtime is very frequent. If Manchester men get advances, Warrington then N; i 1 1 also benefit. Warring- ton is now about the Nst ortanised district in the Manchester Federation area. • Generally, the men are satisfied with the points gained."</p>
      </div>
