        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>MR. ALFRED COREY.</p>
        <p>Resignation of the Headmaster of Wycliffe School.</p>
        <p>ILL-HEALTH &amp; DOMESTIC AFFLICTION</p>
        <p>As .we announced last week, Mr. Alfred Corney, Craig-Cowan, Lower Walton, who has been headmaster of the Wycliffe day school since June, 1883, a period of just over thirty years, has resigned. At the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday, the School Management Sub-committee reported that they had resolved, on the recommendation of the managers of the school, to accept the resignation; and further, that Mr. Corney be informed that the com- mittee regret that, owing to the state of his health, he feels unable to discharge satisfac- torily all the duties of his office; that the strongest possible support be given to the ap- plication which Mr. Corney purposes making to the Board of Education for a breakdown cllowance under the Elementary School Teachers (Superannuation) Acts, 1898 and 1912; and that leave of absence from duty With full salary, be granted to Mr. Corney for the re- mainder of his term of office, i.e., uritil the 31st January, 1914, in consequence of his failing health.</p>
        <p>Permission Was given for the appoinitment of</p>
        <p>headmaster to al the vacancy .thus caused.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UpsoN; moving the adoption of these minuted, said the committee. felt great regret in receiving the resignation. Mr.. Corney had been a headmaster for over 41 years, but it was no secret that during the past two or three years he had ill-health, domestic afflictions, and an accumulation of trouble and worry, and ,at had affected his health to such an extent that he felt it impossible to continUe perform- ing his duties. (Hear, hear.) "I a'm sure I am expressing," said Mr. Upson, ." the feelings not only of the School Manage- ment Committee, hut of every member of the EdUcation Committee, when I say we Are extremely sorry that these troubles have come. upon Mr. Corney, and I am sure our pod wishes will follow him into his retirement." (Hear, hear.) Alderman RENNErr said that he concurred 'en- tirely with all Mr. Upson had siid about Mr. Corney. The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>MR. CORNEY'S CAREER.</head>
        <p>MR. CORNEY'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Corney, who is 62 years of age-, is the son of the late Mr. John Corney, Bury, Huntingdon, who reached the age of 100. He was educated at Peterborough College, and at the age of 21 was appointed headmaster of All Saints' School, Fals- grave, Scarborough. Rapidly establishing him- self as an authority in all educational matters, he was asked to take charge of the art, science, mathematics and other classes under public control, and did his work so satisfactorily that on his leaving for Bedford he had several hand- some presents, After having served as head- master of Anipthill-road School, Bedford, he was appointed to the charge of Wycliffe School, Warrington, where he has had a notable and .lighly successful career. Unfortunately, fail- ing health, accelerated by a motor accident a few- months ago, now necessitates his retire- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carney, early in life, developed the liter- ary instinct, and a generation ago was contri- buting to the better-class magazines. As a writer of hymns and as a preacher, he has made a considerable reputation. Many War- ringtonians who have reached prominent posi- tions, and have "made 'good," as the Cana- dians say, have passed through Mr. Corney's hands, and they. as well as the general public, will hear with profound regret of the resigna- tion and the cause which has cut short so valu- able and so useful a career. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>ASSISTANT DIRECTOR.</head>
        <p>ASSISTANT DIRECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur S. Elliott Receives Deserved</p>
        <p>Promotion.</p>
        <p>A VALUED PUBLIC, SERVANT.</p>
        <p>who was appointed Assistant Director of Educa- tion for Warrington at Monday's meeting of the Education Committee; came here ten years ago as stores clerk. The Education Act had only just come into operation, and the sudden change which that measure effected made the position a most !important one. Mr. J. M. Murray, the directoi of education,, was fortunate in having so capable 'a subordinate as Mr. Elliott, who made the office much more than that of stores clerk. A man of wide reading and deep cul- ture? he became an adviser in the best sense of the word to the teachers of the town, many of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>MR. ARTHUR SPENCER ELLIOTT,</head>
        <p>MR. ARTHUR SPENCER ELLIOTT,</p>
        <p>whom willingly acknowledge that he has ele- vated his post far above its customary level, and that they\ had been delighted to be guided by his advice and assisted by his judgment, a fact which will be a very serviceable asset in the new sphere which is now opening out before him. As the Assistant Director of Education, he will be a worthy successor to Mr. R. B. Roberts, along with whom he has worked under Mr. Murray since the Education Act came into operation. •</p>
        <p>--- , . , ~ .... . • , . • ,', ~• ~ • ' - '' ' ••• ,'' ' , . ~,... ... .. , ~..: '•'_.,. ~ , . ,'• ,' • • , ~ , ~ , . , ....;'.:',..t:?:,'.. .' ~ ' • ' ..' , ' , , ..: '*l.X:'..:`.:E:',. '''.."''',',.4''':4'..;')441,,, ', ... '• ' ' ' :... .''i:.'".................... .i:'. ''' . . . , . • ' ..., ... ' ~. . , , . . .... • , • ... •, . • • ,' , -, - ' , .. . . ~ ' ' ' .‘ ~., , ' .. • ' ~ ' ' `.,.:. , ''. . ' ...... . , , , , . . . . . ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>TRIBUTES TO MR. R. B. ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>TRIBUTES TO MR. R. B. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>The, Warrington Education Committee on Monday paid a tribute to the work of Mr. R. B. Roberts, the assistant director of education, who is leaving to take up a, position under the Surrey County Council as Education Secretary at Woking; near London. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY expressed regret at Mr. Roberts' departure, but pleasure that he was going to undertake a larger sphere of work near London.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT congratulated Mr: Roberts on his promotion, and spoke of the valuable work he had done in Warrington. They were exceedingly sorry to lose his services, but there was the consolation that his promotion Made way for another able servant, and he was glad to know that Mr. Elliott was going to step into. his place. (Hear, hear.) Mr. PLINSTON, as chairman of the School At- tendance Committee, said he had had special facilities for observing the ability with which Mr. Roberts discharged his duties. ' A more effective and kindly disposed official than Mr. Roberts they had not in Warrington. He had given satisfaction to the parents with whom he had to deal, as well as to the School Attend- ance Committee. In losing- Mr. Roberts they were losing a valuable servant. qouncillor ROBERTS said the retiring Assistant Director had the good wishes of every Member of the Committee, and their cordial desire for his further promotion. 2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <p>BRILLIANT SCENE AT PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Special to "The Examiner": By a Lady Contributor.</p>
        <p>, - --- ...., .•,. .:, ....,-,.....,4,,,-;,..• ',.......,..' ''''.'''s , ~ ...4,' ' ..,..•V' I' ~,,,..;,... ..,,,..- ' ~' ' , .-,',..s -:. .•''r.i&lt;i4 '.''':''.:'..':'.. . ''''' ".'• ' ', ' . .. . -,: ..:.;.i.-,,',.1....,...,:;.„...„::-.4.."4.'..'....:::',..:?,-,4:44..t0.4.,k'it1.rii* ftti*;?...:11....'-'' -."."..!!*'- ''' ' . ::: , ... '• . 'e—:: 77 'w:74:4;i:T'',7**l?:.‘,.,.,gt:',tt.:'l'::: :.1,....,1-;:.,,s,:::;fttilii1Z.11::i.:::::.1.::::!tit?ti:*;;.**':-':;;ti.i.:.:17.:.•:.-;‘, . ' :::': ':. . • ::?... •.. --.: .• ; s ~..:7:ii'::::1':::: ' '-'-'".-‘ • s'''' '' ::'''''.c,":-.:•-': '- ' I'''''''''. s''': ,i,'' .'''',l ' '-'47`..*:,-Vg. #•:-: - ..4.:‘,,..r,...i...'1'.4.6'.-r:.;:!?.....,....!?;...:, . ',71.Ne,,,.. ~..,..,?.,,j.....:,'. 'tr;;Z:4,446:::'''., .'''',...,:z. .' ''.:.,,,-....•.:,:,&gt;;.,i'....',...!,,::;7. '''''l§'E:,,: . ' : . - .;. .: ' ' .:.'',','::";:f.,::?4,4,.';`.',...,;.''''...'t '-`..,,.. • . "•'''''''''..:..7 ,0-,....„ ...,—...,...„,,,,,7,,,...,,i,-..*;,,,,........,,,,,,,....,!,:,::::::,.:....,0.:.-..t.•:„..i.,,,,,_;.:-...,.%.„,1,....ir;,:1.A14„:.-....... .:4,,,,,,:...,.*.,73,...:3-;,:„.iti--..,..a.,..,,,,,,,,-..:.:,...,:4,,i, . ~,..':..4-r-'''.,te. '..." '''''..!4°l'4,r-.',...`;'''.."'*',";::.'1.*.,:;,,,i(:...'...•:-.,.. --1.. '&lt;:-.'.k:',.:'' .-kt...-.,.-...ti..:',-;KS:,.;',.:;' - ' ' :1mit,..... -'...., .. '' ''' ~ ,°:,..'.',,,•.: ~.)..C.1'...::: ",... '' ' ..:. \ . - .. 5'....4;i4V...:".)•'''' . , ''....2''.......,:',.;:t.::'....,...44.,.;;it:!......;,..4..i:-.. .' ' '4iiii.4.,;!!:l‘‘ . ...or• • • .• . . , • ' ,:" , , s., , , ~• , • , •', • ••• ' , . , .3,, .. . , , , • " ' ''j'. '• . ' • ' l: ' '. . ' •- . ' '.' ' '' • • ' • ti•••:•.•04*1 ' .. '. • .• i : ' '''', ' . ' —• •.• .¢. •,. s , ' ••••... • ' . . , •• •• . . • , , , , ..... . • '... ,1 .• ~--••• ' : .• . ....7'. . "'s ~.,!... 1:. . . • ','.'. '* ' ''.. .2., '';'..,, : ;;..., ,' .‘ , • " - ' .. ,' .''''4:o4 • •'•• . . ,••:. • .5 •.... • ' ' ' • 5 . • ... : •• • ..-, . , ,• • ..4,3114Mfrs ~. ~.. .• , ' ' . '' ' ' ••,. ' ',, —.•- . • " . , . • • , .. , . , , , , , • ... .. '5 S ...... . . , ......s: ..,,..&lt;4...4F0,..10g, '. .--',..r.. ... ,- .s., .. ~.....!`' s. ~... . .. . '''".' '''.'-''' .. ..::::...% k.. - . .' . ... . ....&lt; s'' 's' • .- '.ll***'. ..—.. ' ' ~...., ..,....: ..,...,. i., . , , r. ~..,: , . , ~, , ~. ~ ,--,...:•••',.5.:f.,.,.:..-..,,,,....,',-....—..,;••:,,,-.-.,..........,.:.•:.,,.._. ~ - _..,. . ~. .. . .... , . .. . ~ . . , ~,. .... ... , . . . .. . . ~. . - • , , . . . ... , . . .., . . .... . .. ~.....,,, ............. • ~.,'..,'....- -...,:,:-..:‘,..._,....,,.....:::::,...,,,,....4,Ly.:,:„....40.„.p..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>What a scene of different times and different climes was the fancy dress ball on Thursday last! The old mingled with the new, one could see a gallant Dragoon coquetting with an Indian squaw, and a Quaker girl looking quite coy in her fascinating bonnet. A Chinaman who seemed not to be troubled in the least with the state of his far-away country, was enjoying many a lively dance with a little Geisha girl from the neighbouring land, and Cleopatra, the queen, looked as though she was quite en- tranced with the glories of a regal court once more. The very name took me back to time past, but the whirling of the dances brought me to time present; and we shall witness a transformation before long, and see the Cleopatra of the ball blossom forth as the first lady of the town.</p>
        <p>one of the latter promptly tied his first favour round his left arm, so that all could see he wore his lady's colours. For, indeed, had not the handkerchief a coloured border? He was proud of that cotton square. What a pretty idea was the gift of roses I And then we saw roses everywhere, buttonholes adorned with this beautiful flower, dresses decorated and hair ornamented ;but' the- featUre of this 'dance was the lighted candle episode). A lady stood on a high chair holding high—as high as possible— a candlestick with a lighted candle, and then the gentlemen were told that the one who could blow it out could claim the fair one for his partner. Then what a rush! What laughing! One would-be candle extinguisher brought, a chair, but he was quickly disqualified. Another went down on his knees, probably to implore the lady to 'hold the candle lower, and then when they found that the living statue was adamant to any pleading to lower the flicker- ing light, they began to jump, and now can you imagine the scene? It was full of amusement for the onlookers until—ah ! out goes the light, the lady descends from her high pedestal, the gentleman claims his own and on goes the merry dance once more. This was done about three times, so that everyone had a splendid idea of ' the springing " propensities of the majority of the gentlemen present. Afterwards the ladies received many a pretty useful ornament, and they quickly repaid the donors with a comical mascot.</p>
        <p>Then the paper dresses! Marvels! Men will take heart now. Fancy using old match boxes as a ball dress for a wife! What a splendid idea! Surely newspapers would do also, and I give the suggestion for nothing. What a saving in dressmaker's bills! Blessed be he who in- vented the idea! For I've no doubt but that it was a man. It will be; "Tommy, what's that you have?" "The 'Examiner,' father." "Well, mind you don' it; it's for your mother's new c05tume."........</p>
        <p>THE EARLY SCENES. Truly, we live in a marvellous age, but after all, it's not much in advance of Eve—only an- other product of the ever-obliging old tree. One of the earliest arrivals was Mr. Rag Time, looking very fit in a suit that had seen better days. It let light in in several quarters. Never mind!</p>
        <p>Much merriment was occasioned by the screen arrangement. Imagine a large screen covered over with thin red paper, and•a bevy of pretty ladies hiding behind It; then the gentlemen, waiting patiently for instructions, standing in a row afar off, and wondering, no doubt, what was going to happen with all this mystery, but as soon as they were told they were expected to rush through the screen and take the partner they desired—oh! mere words fail to describe it! Probably, if your imagination can conjure up the scene, you could let your mind picture a Marathon race. Bravo! It was splendid! But, oh! what a treat when the order was re- versed! Every lady who entered into the arena was ouite a "sport," and how they all enjoyed it! I didn't quite see how many touched the tape first. Dear. me! I beg their pardon, I mean broke the paper, and got through to the waiting and wondering gentlemen biding be- hind. I think there were a good many "firsts," but as there was a prize (or, should I say, a partner?) for everyone: I presume that the one who came in last would not be disappointed.</p>
        <p>The poor and rich alike, Their nakedness display; The poor because they must, The rich because they may.</p>
        <p>Somebody said he must have been a million- aire from America, and had made his money from Rag Time. He seemed to have brought a lot of luggage. There was a charming Indian Squaw, a lassie from the Wild West, looking 'fearless and bold, and Miss Yankee, with plenty of stars and stripes covering her breast. They were really, a jolly "rag-time" party. When the hall was fast filling up, Mr. San- ders, as High Commander, began "to clear the decks for action." He requested all hands "on deck "—hands on deck" were not forthcom- ing, but " feet " were in plenty. The music struck up a jaunty air, and after much swaying and swinging, for which the deck boards were not Iresponsible, it was surprising to find that no one requisitioned the aid of the " stewards " —so much for the early "trips." Several were trying their fortunes in more ways than ven- turing on a slippery floor for the first time. The dark-skinned Italian Gipsy had to keep her little bird quite busy picking out fortunes. What did Mr. Crosfield learn from the mystic art? Will history ever relate? Or shall we leave it until we hear the proclamation_ of the next election? We surmise that Mr. Crosfield must have had a record day's hunt, for his gentlemen friends had, later in the evening, many "brushes" to distribute as favours to the ladies, who seemed quite proud to possess these trophies, for many of them promptly put them in their hair. Feathers claimed a big share of the evening in more ways than one. Conversation turned to feathers in different quarters. I overheard , many asking if "Britannia" ever put this plumy ornament in her headdress. Mrs. Crosfield wore an exquisite costume, and to many people it was an open question as to whether she was anything from Boadicea to Britannia, but as a charming Valkyrie, one of the twelve maidens of Odin, who served at the feasts in Valhalla, she solved every enigma of personality. Miss Elliadi was an effective contrast as a modest Quaker Girl, but notwithstanding, under her staid brown gown, beat a heart full of vivacity, so much so, that her dancing partner, a light-footed .Harlequin, soon brought all this liveliness to the surface. I Mr. Thoreld Lee, who, for the evening, had stepped into the shoes of the wooer of Columbine, looked extremely gallant and becoming in his well-fitting robe. Carmen was portrayed by Mrs. Crosfield's friend, Miss Sehilizzi, and she looked this Span- ish beauty from the top of her big back comb to the tip of her dainty golden shoo. In fact. several of the ladies were quite the prettiest in the room. I caught a glimpse of a tall bat, and when I looked again I recognised. that it was—not a gentleman from the city at all, .but a lady, who had travelled all the way from the land of the Chancellor. She was carrying a "nice-sized basket, but I should be, shy at suggesting that it contained eggs. When I saw Snow I wondered if she had dropped in from Snowdon, but at a closer view it looked as if she had come from a cotton plantation. There was a lively Dutch "girl," whose sabots did not in the least interfere with her enjoy- ment of many a lively dance, and she seemed quite on friendly terms with the dainty lady from King Charles' Court. If the immortal Pepys had seen them—thereby would have hung a tale.</p>
        <p>The chief guests of the evening provoked much laughter from the people on the gallery by- dancing the Lancers a la ragtime, enjoying it themselves not a whit the less.</p>
        <p>THE CROSFIELD PARTY FOR THE BALL. Left to right :—Standing: Mr. Elliadi (Mrs. Crosfield's father), Mrs. Crosfield, Miss Ellindi (Mrs. Crosfield's sister). Sitting: Miss Helena Schilizzi, Mr. Crosfield, Mrs. Elliadi (Mrs. Crosfield's mother).</p>
        <p>their "chapeaux"? I'm really no authority on such matters!] I remarked during the evening that the "Russian peasant woman" looked very well, so very well indeed that I don't think the author- ities would have the heart ever to send her to Siberia, because I think that His Majesty the Mikado (so it was rumoured) suggested that if ladies were so badly treated in Russia he would promptly annex the whole coUntry. I was puzzled by one action of the Chinaman. Why did he put that piece of soap down his back? Did his pig-tail tickle him? The mystery was too great for me;to solve, so I leave it. But, alas! that such sweet merriment should have an end :</p>
        <p>"There was a sound of revelry by night," When the Liberals gather'd in muster strong— The future Member, Mr. C.a and his wife, . Joined in the fun and mixed with the throng. Both young and old, in their beauteous dress, Danced with joy 'neath electric light. Ali! all too fleet went the hours so bright, In the old Parr Hall last Thursday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>COTILLON FUN.</head>
        <p>THE COTILLON FUN.</p>
        <p>'The commencement of the cotillon, which was the feature of the evening, found a great many people ready to partake in it, and when every ene began to dance, every inch- available floor space was occupied. I am sure that everyone enjoyed this to the fullest extent, for every few minutes there was a gladsome surprise, first for the ladies and then the gentlemen. I noticed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>PRETTY COSTUMES.</head>
        <p>PRETTY COSTUMES.</p>
        <p>As the dance went on, many pretty costumes passed before one's eyes. I fancy the "Christ- mas cracker" must have got her dress from the factory of Messrs. Merryface, Goodhart and Co. I know this is one of the best and most. reliable of firms for supplying the happiest "crackers,' so the lady is to be, congratulated on her choice. She was, often enjoying the tripping with the daintiest of little fairy girls. How her tiny feet kept time to the merry music as she whirled round in the mazy waltz! .The little daubing girl was very pretty. _</p>
        <p>Then the -worthy chef reminded everybody that supper time was near.. He recalled to my mind cream cakes and delicious dainties of every description, ,but never once 'did I think of "trifles." Oh, no I He didn't " trifle " with the ball; the cooking, was suspended for one evening. Well, I suppose even chefs demand a "night out" sometimes. However, if he doesn't sugar the soup for the next few days his mistress will be good to him and give him— when next the fancy dress ball comes round— another "night out." A robe which caused much admiration was a pretty satin of quite fashionable "B and S" tint. It 'must have given much satisfaction to the wearec to know that she wore the very "latest" colour.</p>
        <p>, The "Sunflower" lady, the "Violets,' the "Shamrocks," the Lattice "Woodwork," and many other "flowers" were quite a gay feature. Now, I wonder why Mrs. Crosfleld didn't give' the candle to the lady in the flowing white , gown? What a most appropriate study ! For- ward; flashlight man! And then Mr. Crosfield nearly lost " something" during the evening. Now, don't rush to conclusions. It did not come up to the " Honi soit qui mal y pense" idea. It was not from his garter at all—it was only a shoe buckle ! ! Why' didn't the gentleman with the domino dance with the tiny ballet girl? We know noblemen always come "incognito,' but, never mind! Although it's a modern fail- ing .to be fond of pretty ankles and charming footlight faces—we are all ready to forgive him. I never heard the order to " clear the refresh- ments at any price" issued by the gay High Commander (although I did hear him tell one gentleman not to sit on a lady's knee. Hush!! No! he did NOT whisper that order), but some gentleman must have received the message strictly in confidence. He foraged the larder at frequent intervals and despatched the 'same in a doorway whilst watching the dancers.. There's fascination,for you Couldn't leave the ladies—l mean dainties—at any price. Someone., ventured the suggestion- that the ladies would be in the front rank of . fashion as regards " feathers " for so.me time to come! Really! What a relief! For what a lot shall we have to thank the Liberal Ball! It will be a welcome sight to see Paris on a visit to Warrington in our all too sombre streets, and I for one will say 'an audible "I thank you!" te the fertile ingenuity of the brain behind the scenes. Some outside critics have ventured to air the opinion that Warrington women do not dress well, and I believe • this profanity has even appeared in print. I boldly invite the generous critics to view our town for the next few days. [Do you think I've given the ladies time to ornamor</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>SOME OF THE CHARACTERS.</head>
        <p>SOME OF THE CHARACTERS.</p>
        <p>Amongst those present were : Mr.. A. 11. Crosfield, huntsman; Mrs. A. 11. Crosfield, Boadicea; Mr. and Mrs. Elliadi; Mies Elliadi, Quaker girl; Miss Helena Schilizzi, Car- men; Mrs. Peacock, Cleopatra; Mr. Peacock; Mr. and Mrs. Osborne; Mr. Sanders, naval officer; Mrs. Sanders, ranch girl; Mr. H. M. Ashton, Dragoon Guard officer; Mr. W. Smith, San Toy; Mrs. Lowe, Welsh woman; Miss E. Earl, Winter; Miss Dora Pemberton, ragtimer ; Miss E.'Booth, Mercia; Miss E. Dearden, Japan- ese lady; Mr. P. Delaney; Councillor T. Locker and Mrs. Locker, Russian peasant; Mrs. Ziba Armitage; Mr. Thorold Lee, Harlequin; Mrs. Gordon, fortune teller; Councillor Lindley; Mr. H. S. Page; Mr. and Mrs. O'Brien; Mr. and Mrs. Dutton; Councillor and Mrs. Halliwell; Mr. and Mrs. Lloyd Jones; Mr. and Mrs. D. S. Pendlebury ; Councillor and Mrs. Wilkinson; Mr. Joseph Wilkinson; Mr. R. Delaney, Hullo Ragtime; Miss G. Delaney, North-West Indian; Miss M. Delaney, schoolgirl; Mrs. 11. Boyle, Duchess of Marlborough; Mr. 11. Boyle, Happy Fanny Fields; Mr. J. H. West, Mikado; Miss B. Booth Jessica; Miss Short,Cherry Ripe; Mrs. Freeman, a friend in need; Mrs. Pendlebury, Christmas cracker; Mr. Alcock, toreador; Mrs. Bissell, sunflower; Mr. Williams, chef; Mr. Armitage; Miss Page, Folly; Miss Massey, Shamrock; Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Hill, and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crosfield presented the prizes to the successful competitors as. follows : Floral Lady—Mrs. Bissell. Industry—Mr. Williams. Oriental Lady—Mrs. Peacock. Oriental Gentleman—Mr. J. 11. West. Historical Lady—Miss Booth. Historical Gentleman—Mr. Alcock. Comical Lady—Miss Page. Comical Gentleman—Mr: H. Delaney. Paper Lady—Miss Massey. Most Original Costume—Mrs. Freeman. Committee—Mrs. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Mrs. 'Crosfield, however, preferred to forego her prize, which was presented to Mrs. T. Locker, the runner-up.</p>
        <p>"THE FAIRY GODMOTHER.!"</p>
        <p>Miss Schilizzi Gives Prizes and Promises a . Piano for the Club.</p>
        <p>During an interval in the programme, Mr. CROSFIELD said: Miss Schilizzi, ladies and gentlemen, I shall not detain you more than two or three minutes, but I am particularly glad to have this opportunity to express, on your behalf, as well as on my own account, our united, sincere and very hearty thanks to the Fairy Godmother—(prolonged applause)—who has, in such a delightful way, added to the en- joyment of all of us this evening. (More cheers.) Miss Schilizzi is an old- friend of my wife, and of my wife's family, and I have the privilege to Claim her myself as a friend of many years' standing. She has taken a keen interest' in the political fortunes of our party in Warrington, and it was entirely on her own initiative, and thanks to her generosity, that this delightful idea of a cotillon has been in- cluded in ,our proceedings, and made such a success to-night. Not content with so kindly and‘generously helping•us on this occasion, Miss ,Schilizzi has also just Promised that when the Memorial Hall, which Mrs. Crosfield is building , in Bewseyi-street, is completed, she will present us with a Grand piano for it. (Loud and con- tinued applause.) I am sure, ladies and gentle- men, that the heartiness of your applause will be the best proof to Miss Schilizzi of the way in which her most liberal and kindly assistance is appreciated. (Cheers.) Miss SCRILIZZI, in reply, said: ladies and gentlemen, it gives me very great pleasure in- deed to be amongst you this evening, and I hope it will not be long before we all meet again. (Loud and continued applause.) I also hope it won't be long before you again return Mr. Arthur Crosfield as your member. (Renewed applause.) . At the close of the programme Mr. Sanders called for three hearty cheers to Miss Schilizzi, for her many kindnesses. and this was re- sponded to by the large gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>Stewards: Messrs. Nurse, IL oShorne, G. Smith, Unsworth, Massey, Cox, O'Neil, Lay- land and Hallows, assisted by six members of</p>
        <p>(Continued in next co :torn )</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>PUBLIC AfAUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AfAUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE</p>
        <p>" MONTE CRISTO." Large houses have assembled at the Royal- Court Theatre to witness an excellent presenta- tion a-Alexandre Duma's masterpiece, "Mont, Cristo." The adventures of Dantes, from the time he is arrested as the sailor to his Emcees-. sion to the estates of Monte Cristo, are in- tensely exciting. A strong love interest rune through the story, and the hea*ier aspects are counterbalanced by a goodly supply of humour. Mr. Wilson Coleman makes Duma's Dante Miss Mary Pemberton is well placed as Men, cedes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>"The Pirate Ship" will be preserrtod by .Mr. C. Watson Mills' London company xrext week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Hanlon Bros. and Co. have scored a decide&amp; success at the Hippodrome this week in a far- cical pantomimic absurdity, "The Hauntect Hotel." The " mirror " illusion was very good. Wright Aitken and Co. make an appeal with the domestic episode, "The Battlq of Life," in which the strongest feature ieto itus- trate the "class" war. James Rose and Grace create much laughter in "The Insurance Agent and Hints," their gags being up to date. Auber gives a clever musical turn, and the others are all good. , NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>At the Hippodrome next week Capt. H. Shaw. will present "All Square," a Western play in one act. Other turns will be: Noblett, in "Masks and Faces"; Will, Hay, schoolmaster comedian; Jimmy Godden, Nature's cornedian;. Carpati Bros., bar gymnasts; Les Andres, dancing speciality; Eddie Astor, dancing jug- gler; Cleo Peel, comedienne and impressionist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL,</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL,</p>
        <p>A grand re-opening will be made at the popu- lar Scotland-road house on Monday, when .the "Royal" Stock Co. will perform "The Shaughraun." Mr. H. Val Vousden, always favourite here, will appear as Con, and Miss Leah Corenters will join the company shortly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>"The Tiger Lily," described as the greatest animal drama of the year, will provide the principal attraction here for the first three nights next week, whilst at the remaining performances a Western war drama, " Bed- ford's Hope," should attract large houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>UNIQUE EVENT.</head>
        <p>A UNIQUE EVENT.</p>
        <p>Johannes Wolff's Great Farewell Warrinftos</p>
        <p>Concert.</p>
        <p>Next Thursday evening Mr. Johtinnes Wolil comes to Part' Hall, Warrington, and will give his farewell ,concert.</p>
        <p>The world-famous violin virtuoso, though in the prime of life, has decided to retire from the concert „platform, and thus leave true lovers of music so much the poorer. Recently, at Black- pool and Bournemouth, vety large audiences assembled to enjoy, for the last time, the un- bounded pleasure Mr. Johannes Wolff unfail- ingly gives his hearers. A virtuoso of rare finish, with a magnificent technique, capable of expressing every grade of human emotion, he has won the hearts of his hearers entirely by his temperamental playing and the magnetism of his personality. A great favourite with the Royal Family, Mr. Wolff is the proud owner of many valuable gifts from the late Queen Victoria, as marks of her appre—</p>
        <p>ciation of his art. He—holds the distinguished appointment of Court violinist to the Queen of Holland, and is Knight of the Order of Orange Nassau of Holland, of Saint Stanislas of Russia', Louis of Hesse, of Portugal, and Officer of the Legion d'Honneur, and Officer of the Instruc- tion Publique de France, N1.V.0., &amp;c. In addi- tion to being welcome at all the Courts of Europe Mr. Wolff is the personal friend of Saint-Saens, and has been largely instrumental in making the public of all countries familiar wit II the works of this great composer, together with Francis Thorne, Edward Grieg, and many others.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress are taking a great interest in this " farewell,": and have promised to be present. the programme .will be a very tine one. Miss Alys Bateman, the famous English singer, is specially engaged, and other London artistes of first rank' will appear, in- cluding Miss Dorothy Bottomley, the famous contralto from Yorkshire; Harold Wilde, the great tenor, and 'a 'Warrington favourite; George Baker; and Parlowitz, the wonderful Polish pianist. Mr. H. H. Pearson; Bridge- street, has charge of the local arrangements, and a large audience is expected:</p>
        <p>Perfectly Good Answer. The comment by a candidate in an examina- tion paper that "to have to say . something" was a phrase that expressed' the predicament of some of our poet laureates is described as a "howler," but it seems to us a perfectly good answer.</p>
        <p>The Council Rose.</p>
        <p>A friend in, Buckingham sends us a paper, which contains, among many other good things, a report of the Aylesbury Urban Dis- trict Council, which concludes: "The Council rose at 9.15." Why go into such detail? Some wives of councillors believe all councils last till eleven. Why, we again ask, go into such detail?</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOOIATION FANCY DRESS BALL—Continued.</p>
        <p>the junior Liberal Association. Judges: Messrs. 11. M. Ashton and T. Critch-</p>
        <p>.M.C.'s.—Messrs. T. Locker, T. Bunch, P. Delaney, J. Booth and Sanders. The hall had been decorated in tasteful fashion by a committee, under the direction of Mrs. Osborne, and consisting of Miss Booth, Miss Short, Mrs. G. Pendlebury, Mrs. Hill and Mrs. Sanders.</p>
        <p>The best thanks of all are due to ,Mrs. Osborne, who acted as secretary, and upon whom most of the work devolved. The catering was in .the capable bands of Atkinson's Cafe.</p>
        <p>DON'T LOOK OLD—Keep the MeE, Of time at bay by darkenin g the grcy streaks with LOCRYER'SSULPHIIR HAIR RnsTour.a,which deepens to a naturaleolonrin a few days. Lockyer's mainte,ins a youthful appearance. Large botB. everywhere. Post free 1/6.Peeford Laboratory,London.S.</p>
