      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>WOMAN ESCAPES MUM</head>
        <p>WOMAN ESCAPES MUM</p>
        <p>OPERATION</p>
        <p>How She Was Saved From Surgeon's Knife by Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound.</p>
        <p>"The first two years was married .1 suffered so much from female troubles and bearing down pains that I could not stand .on my feet long enough to do 'my work. The doctor said 1 would have to undergo an opera- tion, but my husband wanted me to *try Lydia E. Fsinkham's Vegetable Compound</p>
        <p>-; a-e•-• QA. ...;?;K:i • •• • • ..„. .....•••.• • F.,. 7, Ai</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>first.</head>
        <p>first.</p>
        <p>I took three</p>
        <p>bottles and it made Tile well and strong and I avoided a dreadful operation. I now have two fine healthy. children, and I cannot say too much about what Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound has done for me." (Name and address ,on request.) Why will women take the risk of an operation or drag , out a sickly, half- hearted existence, missing three-fourths of the joy of living, when they can find health in Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound? There is scarcely a civilized country where Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound is not to be had. In this country any chemist will supply it at 2s. 9d. per bottle, or 4s. 6d. for a very large bottle. Any woman who will send a postcard to The Lady Manager, c/o Thr. Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., '42a Hampstead Road, London, N.W., will receive a free private book to women only. She will also supply the medicine, if de- sired, post free at the prices named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>MOTORISTS FINED.</p>
        <p>Result of the Police Watch.</p>
        <p>The local police are keeping a strict lookout for motorists who break the Motor " Car Act regulations, and at the Borough Court on Tues- day, before Councillor 11. Roberts (in the chair) and other magistrates), William Edmundson, 8; Riddlcton-street, Preston, a "heavy motor" driver, in the employ of M4srs. George and :Richard Dowhurst, of Preston, was summoped for exceeding the speed limit of 12 .miles per hour in Manchester-road on October 2nd.—Mr. Grace, of Manchester, represented the defendant. Inspector Foster said that on the Clay in ques- tion .he was timing heavy motor cars in Man- chester-road, with the assistance of Detective. sergeant, Reddington and Detective Summers. Shortly after 11 a.m. defendant came along, driving a vehicle- in the. direction of Liverpool. He travelled over the measured distance of 440 yards in 57 seconds—a speed of over 15 'miles an hour. There had been a good deal •of talk re: cently about motor waggons, arid the police had received many complaints. Detective-Sergeant Reddington gave corrobor- ative evidence,</p>
        <p>Detective Summers said when he stopped defendamt the 'latter said his speedometer 'regis- tered 11 miles an hour. -. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Grace said defendant had been driving motor waggons for close on six years, :and had never previously had,a charge against him. lie asked the magistrates to regard the offence as a purely technical .one. The man worked long</p>
        <p>hours, and on the day in question there was the trouble at the Manchester Docks and a lot of motors would pass through the town. • A fine of £3 3s. and costs was imposed. Arthur Holmes, 12, White Lion-street, Man- chester, was charged with a:iimilar offence in Manchester-Toad on the same date.—He pleaded guilty. Inspector Fester said the speed of the waggon was over 15 Miles an hour. . Defendant was fined £3 3s. and costs. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Mr. A. J. Jolley, and Mr. T: Pemberton. _</p>
        <p>Tam VICAR'S -CYCLE.—Ernest Albert Victor Gleave (15), 41, Willis-street, was charged with stealing a bicycle, value £l, the property of the Rev. F.. E. Evans, vicar of Appleton Thorn. —The Chief Constable said the previous day' Mr. Evans left the bicycle outside the Museum, and when he came out he missed it. Detective summers made inquiries, with the result that the, boy was arrested. • He had taken the mach- ine to Mr. D. Webster, cycle dealer, Church- street, and asked for £1 for it, saying it was his father's, and that his mother was in arrears of rent. Mr. Webster kept the bicycle and told the boy to bring his mother 'along, and if the story was true he would give him 15s: for it. The, boy came back and was handed over to the police/ He still stuck to his story that the bicycle was his father's, but afterwards admitted the offence. The boy, said the Chief Constable, appeared to be a serious source of trouble to ; his parents. Employment was found him, ,but he would not keep to it. Perhaps he might be sent to an industrial school.—The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.. Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst Bolton and James Evans, Messrs. S. Wright, G. Pilling, W. Furness and T. Locker</p>
        <p>A "RUMMY " STORY. Thomas Cannon, labourer, Dutton, was fined 55., including costs, lei- having been drunk and disorderly.—lt was stated that prisoner had three bottles; each half filled with rum, in his possession when arrested. —ln answer to a magistrate, the Chief Con- stable said, "We have that, but it will be re- turned to him. We should.like to dilute it with ,eoffee, but I am afraid we cannot."—ln fining the prisoner, the Mayor said, "Take great care of that rum."</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair),</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. T. Locker—.</p>
        <p>DOWNRIGHT LAZINESS."—James and Hannah Mather, 11, Rodney-street, again appeared in answer to the summons for neglect of children. —Mr: Browne, on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said that the case had been adjourned for four weeks, to see how defendants went on. The Bench bad intimated that if a substantial improve- ment was not manifested at the end of that time a severe, penalty would be inflicted. The object of the summons was to make the husband recognise his responsibilities. The fault of the wife was pure and wilful laziness.—The male defendant said he had bought clothing for the •</p>
        <p>WE ARI-3 EXPERTS IN Ladies' Tailoring. A Trial Order will prove this. Call and inspect our New Ranges of Choice Goods for this season.</p>
        <p>GEORGE SUTTON</p>
        <p>(LATE U. S. PAGE S.: CO.) 74, SANKEY ST. Warrington. TEL 553. LECH ST. CORNER.</p>
        <p>aildres.' and semerything hat' *gone to the pawn- shap.—The Chairman: It is 'a case of downright lazinass.—The female deltndant was sent to prison .for two months, and the ease against Mather was adjourned for eight weeks. "FED W."—Martin Mannion, 1, Winifred- street,. was summoned by his .wife, May Ann Mannion, 1, Platt-street, for neglecting her and her child.—Mannion pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Browns, who appeared for the complainant, said the parties were married hist January. The husband was in an excellent position at the Union Tanneries, Howley, and his wages were 365.. a. week. He would not give his wife sufficient money, and she had had to be assisted by her brother, and had been compelled to leave her husband. The defendant had told both the brother and Inspector Wainwright that he wanted a separation.—Complainant stated that her husband said he was "fed up," and the only thing she could get from him was "separation.' —Defendant said on September 19th he gave his wife 18s., and only drew 21s. 4d. She had left him for days at a time.—The case was adjourned for four weeks.</p>
        <p>THUIPDAY. Before Messrs. 3... Rymer Young (in the chair), A. J. Jolley, W. Roberts, J. I) pson, and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>HAVING A SLEEP.—John Jones, a stranger to the town, with a bad record, was charged with being on enclosed premises.—Prisoner explained when charged that he had gone there for a sleep.—He was committed to prison for three months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>AN INNOCENT BOUND OVER--Joseph Bibby, Lythgoes-lane, was charged with using threats towards John Dacey, also of Lythgoes-lane.— Complainant stated that on Tuesday, when he wont home, defendant commenced a quarrel and threw a brick, which just grazed complainallt's wife. • "I have always been afraid of that fel- low," he remarked.—Defendant said that he was us innocent as a.child, but was bound over for six months. ACTED DEAF AND DOM—John Crawford, of no settled abode, was sent to prison for 14 days for begging.—The officer who proved the case said Crawford acted as though he was deaf and dumb.</p>
        <p>FOUND A GOLD MEDAL.—Andrew Higgins, of no settled abode, was charged with stealing a medal, the property of some person unknown.— Evidence 4was given showing that he attempted to Pawn it; when ho said it was his property.— Vrisoner now said he found tho medal half a mile away from Hale,.and he was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>TACKLED THE CoNsTAßLE.—George Knight, of West-street, Warrington, was charged with keeping a ferocious dog, and also With having no licence for. the ,dog, which, it was 'slated in evidence; bit a. constable and tore his trousers.-- pefendp.nt- ,was ifined 20s. and costs ii the first case, and 10s. and 'costs in the second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNiNG.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNiNG.</p>
        <p>Ideal weather prevailed on Saturday,- when 26 of Warrington ran over nearly five miles of good country in the vicinity of.Padgate, Fearn- head and Orford; so that a change of. .country could be, enjoyed. .Mr: Harry Ashton provided splendid 'dressing accommodation at the Orford Hotel, from where the start took place. The captain, J.. Parsonage, had the assistance of H. IC. Jepson, T. RowlinSon, E. Scott and H. Woods to pace and whip a pack which Main- tained a splendid pace throughout, and a "jolly good run" was the opinion of the participants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>To-day's Vixture.</head>
        <p>To-day's Vixture.</p>
        <p>Meet at the Pavilion, Fletcher-street,'at.three p.m. for a quick turn out, and start at 3.15 prompt. In view of :the fact that. the. novice and •intermediate points Competitions begin next Saturday, all the eligibles are particularly requested to attend to-day. For evening exercise meet at •headquarters at 7.30 p.m. on Tuesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>Runner as Footballer.</head>
        <p>Runner as Footballer.</p>
        <p>The James E. Cartwright selected by Man- chester City to play against Everton to-day, is Warrington's ." Shino," although described by the sitriOui daily papers as of Northwich; this is the sturdy sprinter who won the _local club's 100 yards handicap from scratch on the day of the King's Visit, and local sportsmen wish him success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>IMPRISONMENT FOR DEBT.</head>
        <p>IMPRISONMENT FOR DEBT.</p>
        <p>" Old Journalist " writes : The case of the lapsed commitment order men- tioned in Warrington County court recently, once more gives rise to this question. Up- wards of 11,000 debtors were imprisoned in the year 1904, under the Debtors Act of 1869. Since then the fact has been taken as the text of an open letter by Mr. Joseph CoHinson, of the Humanitarian League, ti the Home Secretary, published in the "Humane lieview." The case for the need of reforming debtor's law seems to be made out. One i reminded that the 'Debtors Act had as its great object' the abolish- ment of imprisonment for debt;• but unfortu- nately it conferred a new bower of imprison- ment on the judges, which has never been fully consideried by Parliament: Sinai '1869-a man may be imprisoned for a judgment-debt of one shilling, and, in fact, debtors are often imprisoned for very small amounts; and. the number of committals. for debt has no reached' a figure which, we believe, was never equalled in the worst times, though there are limits to its duration that did not then exist. The object or the Statute is not to punish debtors, but to coned the debt. This object imprisonment naturally defeats, except when money is borrowed to pay the debt, which leaves the debtor in the same or in a worse condition than before. Thus says the writer, and he proceeds to point out that the work done by debtors while in prison ,does not pay the expense* of maintaining them there, and that the deficiency' is made good by the ratepayers, who derive no benefit from the imprisonment; that during such imprisonment the wives and families of the imprisoned debtors are usually also supported by the rates or by, private char- ity, or are driven to dishonest courses, which equally involve a loss to the public; that the debtor often loses his employment in conse- quence of his imprisonment, and becomes chargeable on the ratepayers, or else incurs fresh liabilities of increased amount; and that the number of these judgment stimmonses to be heard at the County ,Courts adds to the expense of maintaining these Courts, interferes with, the speedy transaction of their general business,., and sometimes leads, to sentences of imprisonment being passed after a very hur- ried and perfunctory hearing. I could from personal experience Multiply instances in sup- port of these points, if ne-cessary. By rules made under the Prisons Act, 1898, and which came into Operation on the Ist of May, 1899, the treatment of debtors in prison was completely changed. Prior to that date debtors were allowed to idle in association in a "common room," and could have their food from outside. But under the new rules they are compelled to work, and, in fact, though they are kept' as a class apart, their treatment does not differ materially from the treatment of criminal prisoners of the second division. It was hoped to reduce the number of debtors imprisoned by making. their condition in prison more deter- rent. But, by a curious irony, it is' precisely since the new rules came into operation that the number of debtor prisoners has steadily increased. No stronger proof could well he given that this annual quota of thousands of debtors are imprisoned not because -they will not, but because they cannot, pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Free Church.--A sale of the fruit given for harvest thanksgiving services was held in the Free Church on Monday night. A musical pro- gramme had been. arranged. Miss D. Lawton sang " Absent " and " Until "; Miss Watts, "Come, sing to me"; Miss Ormiston and Mr. E. Smethurst, "Star of Bethlehem" and "By the fountain"; and Mr. F. Richmond,'" Asleep in the dew." Mr. Richmond also played a violin solo, and Master C. Wilson gave a bell solo. The humorous element was supplied by Messrs. R. Barlow and &amp;Tilton. Mr. R. Wilson was the accompanist.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE GOSPEL ABROAD.</p>
        <p>S.P.G. Speaker Says Materialism Bars Progress of Christianity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>APPROACHING EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>APPROACHING EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>A more generous use Of the pulpit in regard to missionary societies was pleaded for by Major Storr (lay secretary of the C.E.M.S.), at the annual meeting of the 'Society for the Pro- pagation of the Gospel in Foreign Lands, in the Parr Hall on Tuesday. The Major said that one sermon in 52 weeks was not sufficient. • The 80% E. H. Day (local organising secretary of the S.P.G.), a 'missionary, late of Burmali, also gave an interesting address. The hall was crowded. no Hey. the Hon. C. F. Cross pre- sided. There were also present representa- tives •of • the -varioug: 'churches and 'church or- ganisations in the town.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>VENERABLE, • BUT NOT ANTIQUATED.</head>
        <p>VENERABLE, • BUT NOT ANTIQUATED.</p>
        <p>, ir The CHAIRMAN said he was glad to notice tha( tahriedi:llitoerr6es. That t in the whesis a society was growing more matter for which they had to thank God. The S.P.G. had sometimes been called venerable, and when they thought about a venerable 'society such as that, they were to use the word in the right spirit— society to be trusted, valued and used-. The S.P.G. was not an antiquated, out-of-date society. They had. 212 years of life behind it. They had schools, many of ,which were vener- able Institutions, in the country, but if ,they visited thein they would show the youth of the nation—boys and girls with all the promise of life—in the courts) quadrangles; lecture-rooms and playgrounds, where others had been IOnL years before. That society, which had a great history in the past, was full of life and vigour at the present time. It was decidedlk inter- esting to see the headquarters of the society in Tufton-street, London. They owed a deep debt ,of gratitude to the Junior Clergy Missionary Association, of which the Vicar of Budworth was head, for the way they had persevered in the annual arrangements for 'that meetlng. That' meeting was a .sort of introduction and usher to that greater and more important cele- bration in November. (Applause.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>APATHY IN MISSIONARY WORK.</head>
        <p>APATHY IN MISSIONARY WORK.</p>
        <p>• Major STOIIII Said they wanted a more gen- erous use of the pulpit in regard to missions. :here were still too many parishes where they had only one missionary sermon in a year, and where practically' rjo other mention of "he society was ever made frOm the pulpit. They were told over and over again that missions tirgelabi , Joust occupy it central place in the life of th Thurch. Was one sunday in 52 giving mis- sionsthe central place? ' literature making of theYialeditin i ‘nvoe-s,l‘.e' ptil•loccYlueed by the various missionary socie- ties? People used to say—and perhaps with some justice—that missionary periodicals and magazines were dull arid Uninteresting, and badly got up, &amp;T. There was; however, no pos- sible excuse for Saying that nowadays, for the and other mis- siOnaty: societies were perfectly'. admirable, publications issued by the S.P.G. which Were "not .450 widely Used as they could. he. Were they making any effort in their par- ishes to run a u4Sionary lending library, ,thich was perfe(Aly possible in all parishes? They should do all in their power to increase their knowledge on the subject of missions. MiSgions were contributed to by one-fifth of the members of the Church. there were•four- tifths who contributed nothing, but there were magnificent instances of devotion in the *one-. lifth who did contribute, but they must broaden the basis.</p>
        <p>What was to be the result of the forthcOming exhibition? Were they going to have many thousands of people going through the halls and courts and saying, "Oh, yesOt is very in- teresting"? If it. was going to remain there, iley had better have no exhibition at all. They should follow the inatter up carefully, Method- ically, and to the •best of their ability, and prayer must be the mainspring of all their efforts in that an all directions. Materialism WaS making such headway that it seemed hard for Christianity to make any headway at all in many places. Where they were making ,headway,it was only a drop in the ocean. It was 19 centuries since Christ f•ame, and there were still two-thirds of the world unChristian. They wanted their •faith stirring up, and they would only get that by prayer. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>WHY GO TO BURMAII WHEN THERE IS</head>
        <p>WHY GO TO BURMAII WHEN THERE IS</p>
        <p>HEATHENISM AT HOME?</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. H. DAY said they owed-a debt of gratitude to the Church of England Men's Society for organising that meeting. The. lay- men had taken 'the 91'ganisation in their own. hands, and were going to do a great deal at the exhibition, where they would have the Colonial- Court. Speaking' of Burmah, - where •le had laboured for a number of years, he said it was a country witli great possibilities. We had sent • out soldiers to guard our -interests, officials to govern, and merchants to trade'with the people, but what were we doing in order to help those people? Were we a hindrance or were we a help? If they knew anyone going to Burmah or India, they should impress on them the importance of remembering the ,faith of the old country. In Burmah they were sur- rounded by heathenism and temptations ten times. greater. than in England. .In regard to Buddhism, he..said, they were asked, " Why do you go to a. people with a religion older than your own? Why not leave them alone?" When he first‘ went to Utirmah in 1896 an Englishman had said, " You have enough heathen in Eng- land,' why do you come here?" and as he looked round on the natives in their silks, and appar- ently having no trouble he thought there was some truth in what the man said. But as he ,got to know the people and their religion more he saw the reason why missionaries should be sent there. Christianity was a positive religion. They believed in God, a Saviour, souls and heaven, but the Buddhist said there were none of those things, and yet there were many more people in Burmah following Buddhism' than t.:hristianity. A hearty vote of thanks to the speakers and chairman was carried unanimously;</p>
        <p>The C.E.M.S. are working strenuously for the forthcoming exhibition, and handbills and advertisement tickets have been issued by them to make the exhibition more publicly known. ;Mr. W. B. Randall, organist of Holy Trinity presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>Your Vision Needs Testing if you have any reason what- ever to suspect that it is imperfect, but especially if after reading your eyes ache.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Johnston Watson</head>
        <p>Johnston Watson</p>
        <p>SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>STEEL SPECTACLES FROM 2/6. ROLLED GOLD FROM 8/6. PLEASE NOTE.—MR. WATSON attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-30; Thurs- day 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30. Hours of Business 9-0 to 7-30. Saturday; 9-0 to 9-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>.. ~,. -- . _ :... ,-0 .-% .. :„„.i...J., 11.., '-,.. ~, ,;,.. ...4, 1 b 4....„:„. ......_...,—. I . .-...:.- ...__ , ~, .. _• ..,....-..:-..,. ~..„ 4..._•:--- ...,.....,,,,,, ~,...A..„.'. if.'</head>
        <p>.. ~,. -- . _ :... ,-0 .-% .. :„„.i...J., 11.., '-,.. ~, ,;,.. ...4, 1 b 4....„:„. ......_...,—. I . .-...:.- ...__ , ~, .. _• ..,....-..:-..,. ~..„ 4..._•:--- ...,.....,,,,,, ~,...A..„.'. if.'</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>Heathens have propounded riddles. " Where. have toe mice gone?" asks one. Ile continues in this form : " I have three cats in my house, I maintain them to get mice, "And I cannot See any mice about." Truly we sympathise with him. But as to the mice—well, Ile might, perhapsoisk the cats. • the early bird catches the first worm. . The cats evidently Catch the mice first. Our correspondent has no cause to grumble. Another Heathen writes in this fashiOn.- " What did the four ladies do with the fruit, "After Independent Methodist harvest festi- val?" . That beats all our wit—really. ' PerimPs we might ask him another. What did Mother Eve do with the apple? .But let us try to answer his query. Probably they made it into fruit pies. Beyond that we dare not go further. Some folk wont Whitefield-road Paved with flags on both sides of the way. The property owners don't want that. It would cost heaps ofphard-earned money. Possibly the One who wants does not Own property in the Whitefield-road. Why should the owners be put to expense In order to pave the footway there F.a. the convenience of people who occasionally pass along that road? Now that is a conundrum for you! The Wesleyans are to ." anniversary " to- morrow: They are to have a great day in order to (clebrzite time eighth year of the founding Of their pretty church at Heath-street... - Wilderspool Brewery has been crowned. It has got a tower and a weather vane., - This is rather reminiscent of the Ulster guns. • The. new lower is chiefly made of ,wood. "East is east, and west is west, and---," Al least Kipling doth tell us so. A heathen swears that the sun actually the other night in the east:</p>
        <p>l'orhaps it was moonrise that he did see</p>
        <p>• Tuberculcsis.—lt was intimated at a ,meeting of the Rullcorn ,Rural District Council on Tues- day that three Oases of pulmonary tuberculosis had occurred within the past fortnight in Stock- ton Heath. These patients will probably be treated at the Sanatorium..</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church.—The eighth . •anniversary of the dedication ,of the Wesleyan Church will be celebrated to-morrow. On Sunday specia! sermons were preached, when the Rev. Raine Sunter, of . DidsburY College, Manchester, was eagerly listened to .by good congregations. Gounod's "Send out Thy Light" was admirably rendered by the choir, 'under the conductorship of Mr. F. . renketh, at the evening service. Mr. J. White•was the organist. St. Thomas' Band of Hope.—There was a large attendance ht St. ' Thomas' • Band of Hope - -on Thursday evening, when Mr: Hatton, one of the committee, who, With his sister, is . sailing on Wednesday for • Canada,. was presented with a Teacher's Bible and Church Service. The Vicar, who made the presentation on behalf of the committee and workers, complimented Mr. Hatton on his efforts for the Band of Hone, and wished Min every success in the land to which he was going.. The Superintendent, Mr.' Hal- lows, Mr. Stannard and Mr. Whiteman all spoke of the 'good work done by. Mr. Hatton on behalf of the society. Mr. Hatton 'suitably responded_ and thanked them for their kind appreciation of what little he had been able to do; lie wished it could have been more.—The closing ceremony, taken in a very feeling, manner by Mr. Hatton, brought the meeting to a close. Band of Hope.-.-The winter session of the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope was commenced on Monday in the Hill Cliff e Schoolroom. • Mr. R. Billington presided, and supporting him- were the Superintendent, Mr. J. Moss, and the Honorary Secretary, Mr. G. Whitfield. The members and friends sub- mitted -1 good programme, which consisted of pianoforte soles by Miss Evelyn Fraser and Reggie MosS, recitations. by Daisy Summer, M. Rencassell, Hilda Taylor, Ada Ramsbottom, Lily Hamblett, Emily Williams, Winnie Stringer and Hilda Adamson: songs by Bertha Stringer, Sarah Gamble,. Lizzie Brooks, Isabella Clarkc and Miss Webb. An enjoyable contribution was that of Miss Sophie and Miss May Knight, twc young vocalists, who merited their encore. Miss Emily ustage gave -an auto-harp selection. whilst a reading was. rendered by Mr. Harold Stringer. A dialogue, "Our Band of Hope," was Supplied by Bessie Clarke, Eva Mather and Lizzie Brookes. An excellent interpretation of the. recitation "The Fireman's Wedding" was given by Mr. :T. Whitfield. Miss ,Challoner and Mr. G. A. Whitfield presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>No Amalgamation.—"l • notice," writes a Stockton Heath correspondent, "Ajax," to "The Examiner," "that at the Parish Meeting there was a kit of vapouring about amalgamation with Warrington. I put it point blank to those speakers—are they prepared to face at the very least a rise of 2s. 4d. in the X straight away? That is what amalgamation would mean.. Do those folk imagine that Warrington would give Stockton Heath anything without making them pay through the nose? Warrington are not the heaven-sent philanthropists these parishioners would have us believe. I knock about the country a good bit and I have learned first- hand what has happened to similar districts to Stockton Heath when taken into towns. In the suburbs taken in within recent years, despite spacious promises of what differentiation in rating would do, by Leeds, Sheffield, Bir- mingham, and GlaSgow, the rates of the annexed suburbs have increased at an alarming rate, and the districts have got nothing they, had not formerly, except a Lord Mayor and a few swell-headed aldermen. Let Warrington take over Stockton Heath, and the same would happen, except that we could not possibly have a Lord Mayor. ,The promoters of the scheme would be the first to grumble if the rates in; creased, but from what I can gather they are comparatively insignificant ratepayers. Cer- tainly they have no tight to pose as the.mouth- pieces of the general body of ratepayers. Let there be no costly amalgamation, it is a sense- less game."</p>
        <p>"THE CRY OF BURMA." An Interesting Local Pageant.</p>
        <p>With the object of setting aglow the mission- ary spirit, the younger members of the St. Thomas' congregation, Stockton Heath, pre- sented a pageant entitled "The Cry of Burma" to a well-filled auditorium ,at the Stockton Heath Parish Room on Thursday evening. The performance was illustrative of the work of the Church in Burma amongst the various races, who are asking for help. The pageant was staged amidst Eastern surroundings, and the appropriate costumes carried one in imag-, illation to a far warmer clime than our own. . .</p>
        <p>Previous to the coittmenceMe-n-C,-the Rev. E. 11. Day, the organising secretary for this dis- trict of the, S.P.G., made a few remarks, in which Jie said that in Burma there were 47 different races, speaking 47 different languages, hut, quite naturally, they could only bring before them representations of the principal races, namely, Burmese, Earens, Tamils and Chinese. He hoped that everybody in Stockton Heath would listen to the cry, ,as each race came up asking for . help. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Cast of the Pageant.</head>
        <p>Cast of the Pageant.</p>
        <p>The following is the east, with the names of the principals taking part, as representing the various tribes:—</p>
        <p>Burmese—Women: Mrs. Worrall, the Misses Hallows, L. Barlow, A. .Astall and M. Shepherd. Men: Messrs. E. T. Taylor, IL Battersby and J. IL Caldwell.</p>
        <p>Monk—Rev. E. W. Clarke. Attendant. boys: Eric Rose and Frank Caldwell. , Karens—The Misses Baguley, Astall, A. Thompson and G. Thatcher, Messrs. A. Moores (Igan), A. Thompson ,(Paku) and F. Caldwell (Bruay). Tamils—The Misses 0. ,Gleave, Thatcher and E. Gleave, Messrs. B. Isherwood and T.'ltaguley.</p>
        <p>China—The Misses Moores and M. Caines, and Mr. J. Proberts.</p>
        <p>Chinese—The Misses E. Hayward and T. McDermid, and Mr. H. Caldwell. English Girls, &amp;T.—The Misses C. Hayward, L. Howes, M. Hallows, E. Shaw, J. Twigs, P. Downs, E. Hughes, C. Coppax, T. Bailey, T. Smith, L. Rathbone, G. Williams, D. Taylor, B. Owen, D. Probyn, M. Barrington and E. Bagu- ley. Cross bearer: D. llallows. Choir boys: F. Williams., E. Haslam, G. Hughes and E. Rose. Great credit was due to Mrs. Wood an Mrs. Hankey, who undertook the responsibility of training the performers, and the performance reflected admirably upon them. The Vicar, the Rev. James Collins, had also worked hard for the success of the venture, along with the Rev. E. H. Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>TR kGIC DEATH OF MR. W. CRAMPTON.</head>
        <p>TR kGIC DEATH OF MR. W. CRAMPTON.</p>
        <p>Passes .ftwa: in a Tramcar When En Route to Warrington.</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING CAREER. It was with profound . regret that Stockton Heath people learned on Tuesday of the suilden demise of Mr. William Crampton, a well-known and highly respected member of the community. The deceased was a builders' merchant, and resided in Whitefield-road. He was proceeding to to4n, apparently in his usual health, and when near the Bridge he collaPsed, A doctor was summoned, but by then life was extinct. Heart, failure was • the cause of death. The late Mr. William Crampon who was 59 years of age, was the 'eldest son of the late Mr. William Crampton, a well-known' farmer at Glazebury. He came to Warrington about the year 1870, when he entered the offices of Messrs. Nunnerley and Co., builders' merchants, and became their' business representative, in 'whose employ he remained 'until All. Nunnerley's retirement, and had then served about a dozen years with the firm. He then went into business on his own account, and during the course of the last 30 years he succeeded in building up a Very prosperous business in South-West. Lancashire, Cheshire and North Wales.. In his business life Mr. Crampton was very methodical. and industrious. His relations with his clients were always char- acterised by the strictest integrity, and,. as a consequenee,, his business friends were numer- -sns.. Deceased was of a very retiring 'nature. He did not court Is quiet,Pu-licitY. After his business hours he sought the quietude of his home, and here his friends were always of to share his hospitality. •</p>
        <p>Treasurer of Legh-street Church. Like his father, he was. a strong supporter of the Primitive 'Methodist Church, but here again his unassuming disposition made itself evident,. for it was with the utmost difficulty that ..he could be Persuaded to take upon himself any Public work in the Church. Nevertheless his interest in the. church in Legh-street was real and deep, and -he- consistently attended public worship there right up te the time of his death. His father was for 50, years a local preacher at Glazebury and, Legh-street. Chapels. It •is within the knowledge of one of deceased's friends that, especially during the last few months, the ministfy at Legh-street had touched deep into his life, and only quite re- cently he stated ho* helpful this ministiy had been to him. Deceased was for many years an official in the church, 'formerly being secretary to the trustees, .and latterly the treasurer. His official .duties as church steward were regarded by him as a,trust and were discharged as faith- fully as were the duties in his own business. Mr. Crampton, an 'Examiner" representative was informed, made a practice of settling every bill he received within 24 hours. In January, 1907, Mr. Crampton . married Hester Hilda, younger daughter of the late Mr. John William Ainsworth, of Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath; who for a number of years was a well-known provision merchant in Bridge- street, Warrington. ,He leaves no family. n account of the funeral will be found under Yesterday's District News," on another page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>LY3IIII.</head>
        <p>LY3IIII.</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>The bazaar proved a huge success. The .money raised exceeded expectations. Chief rent will now cease to trouble , Tho members of the Congregational Church. And the "fly caps'" helped considerably. Congratulations to the Postmaster Upon his ,new appointment at Wallasey. His genial presence Will be missed in Lymm. Can you make a dress, dear ladies? - If .not, you should have attended the lecture. There you would have learned all about the art.</p>
        <p>Cruelty to a Horse.—Georc,be Hazlehurst, greengrocer, of Lymni Bridge, Lymm, was fined 10s., including costs, at the Warrington Borough Court on Thursday, for ill-treating a horse by working it while unfit.</p>
        <p>Cult of Dressmaking.—With the object of com- mencing a series of dressmaking classes, a free lesson to ladies on blouse, skirt and coat making, was given by Mrs. Ray in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club on Wednesday night. There was also an exhibition of pupils' work.</p>
        <p>The Missionary Cause.—Mr. H. S. Brown pre- sided over a very interesting young people's missionary meeting at the Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening, when the Rev. T. Maltby Kerruish, formerly of India, gave an excellent address on the child life of that country. At the missionm 'Y meeting for adults Mr. Kerruish spoke on the subject of "Where Four Kingdoms Meet." The exhibition of diagrams and curios,. together with native costumes worn by several children, rendered the visit most ,attractive. The Rev. J. H. Pawlyn presided over the latter gathering. The collections were for the Wes 4 leyan Foreign Missionary Society.</p>
        <p>Promotion for the Postmaster.—Whilst regret- ting his severance with Lymm, it is with great pleasure that we announce the promotion of Mr. C. H. Madeley, the popular Lymm post- master, to the position of chief at the new and. commodious Post Office at Wallasey, Birken.. head. Though he has only been in Lymm three and a half years, his courteous attention has obtained .for him a wide circle of friends. It is hoped that the breezes of the Wirral,Penin- sular will benefit his health.</p>
        <p>Women Liberals.—The Lymm and District Women's Liberal Association held • a very suc- cessful meeting in the Eagle Brow Schoolroom on Thursday night, when a lecture on "Women's Suffrage" by Mrs. Stewart-Brown, of Liverpool, attracted a large audience, which included a number of members of the Women's Suffrage Society. Mrs. Dodds, of Penketh, the President of the Lymm Wo—men's Liberal Association, was in the chair.</p>
        <p>The, Congregational Bazaar.—The merited success which attended the Congregational bazaar -on Wednesday and Thursday last week was again in evidence on Saturday, when a fur- ther chance of participating in a popular event was afforded the locality, an opportunity which was readily seized. Though the proceedings were of a more _mirthful character than on the previous days—an extra number of competi- tions, &amp;e., which were readily patronised, being requisitioned—a touch of formality was afforded by the opening remarks of Miss Jessie Holt, under the presidency of Miss Rushton. The raising of the total to £233 lls. 11d, now enables the deacons ,to celebrate the jubilee of their church in a practical form by completing the fund for the purchase of the chief rent, and the execution of sundry repairs to the church and school fabric. The results of various, competi- tions were declared as follows: Weight of cake, Mrs. Leather; cost of doll's cot, Miss Welford; cost of dressing doll, Miss E. M. Tonge; hat- trimming, Mr. G. Willan ; number of peas, Miss Elsie Hesketh; sponge cake, Messrs. G. Davies and L. Thomas.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mrs. Norris.—The funeral of the late Mrs. T. P. Norris, whose death, at the age of 69, was announced in our last issue, took place on Saturday afternoon. Prior to the interment at the Parish Churchyard, a choral service, conducked by the organist, Mr. W. Cooke, was held in the church, at which a largo number of</p>
        <p>person, including the High Sheriff of Chester (Mr. .N. Watkin), the lion. J. E. Cross, and Lieutenant-Colonel J. E. G. Groves; assembled' to pay their last respects to one they held in' high regard. The Revs. G. B. Thurston (rector), F. C. Bozman (of Broadheath, late of Lymm), J. Edwardes-Evans (of Ringway, formerly head- master Of Lymm Grammar School), and J. Pigot (Grappenhall) were the clergymen present. The principal mourners were Messrs. T. H. Morris, IL F. Norris and C. K. Norris (sons), the Misses E. M. Norris, p., W. Norris, W. A.. Norris, and ',Mrs., H. Egan lull "(daughters). The !numerous floral tributes included some strikingly beauti- fgl designs, and amongst the 'donors were the family, the District Nurse's Fund •Committee, members of the Lymm Habitation of the Prim- rose League, and the Conservative Association. The ringing of a muffled peal at the church on Sunday evening was a further acknowledgment of esteem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>GRAPPEN H ALL: 61,1: st.NINGS FROM GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPEN H ALL: 61,1: st.NINGS FROM GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>The sewage scheme is Still the topic, Mr. Lister-Kaye mentioned it again At the [bird District Council meeting.</p>
        <p>It was an intercsling function that, Marked the presentation to Mr. A. F: 1 Smith. His departure means a loss to the village. He Was always active in its 'welfare. And was one of the foundersf the band. Autumn is creeping on apace. All the folk are bulb planting now. That is a.sure sign of " the fall."</p>
        <p>Drowned in the Fog.—The body of John Antro- bus was recovered from the Latchford Locks on Thursday morning. Deceased left his home. in Bradshaw's4ane .to go. to his work at the hydraulic shed on the Ship Canal, by way of the locks, and, owing' to. the dense fog which prevailed, it is thought he must have miTsed his Way. It was not till two hours had elapsed that his body was recovered. He was 35 years of age and leaves a widow and three children.—A re- port of the inquest will be found under the heading of "Yesterday's District News,7</p>
        <p>A " F earf uI " Road.—The• making of York- drive arid other streets in Grappenhall will not be commenced for six weeks. This fact was made known at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday.—Councillor Lister-Kaye: That will mean it is not 'done this year.—Councillor Pilling: Grove-drive ig in a most fearful condition; it is not fit for anyone to walk up. Something ought to be done before six weeks elapse. I never saw a worse road.— Councillor Lister Kaye: A couple of loads of cinders would do for the present.—lt was decided that the Surveyor see to the cindering of the path.</p>
        <p>Presentation to a Church Worker.—The Rector presided over a large gathering of Church workers and. friends in the large room on Monday night to present Mr. A. F. E. Smith with a handsome cigarette box and suitably inscribed address. The Rector, in making the presentation, said they all regretted Mr. Smith's departure for Pershore, in Worcester, and he thought it would be a fitting opportuntiy to recognise the valuable services he had rendered in his capacity as sidesman, bellringer and Sunday school teacher, and last, but not least, the 'arduous task he had in the raising of sub- scriptions for the formation of the Grappun-. hall Band. The Vicar added that Mr. Smith had won the love and affection of all. His ser- vices Were cheerfully rendered, and his loss would be keenly felt.—Mr. Smith, who was greeted with applause, , said be felt unwerth:,- of the honour which had been bestowed upon bim ; he had always done his best.—The Grappenhall Band was in attendance, and played a selection of music.</p>
        <p>THAT SEWERAGE SCHEME. Again Mentioned at Council Meeting. Coun. Lister-Kaye is keen on the Grappenhall sewerage scheme, and at Tuesday's meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council said that a notice had been put in the papers to the effect that the committee were going in for a provi- sional order for land in Thelwall.</p>
        <p>llis idea was not to get a provisional order until they had approached the Local Govern- ment Board and tried to get Lord Barrymore's land. What was the use of taking land up now in Thelwall?</p>
        <p>Councillor Pilling: If the Local Government Board consent the compulsory order will be withdrawn. We have to wait until the Local Government Board say they will receive us. Councillor Lister-Kaye: We are going to a great deal of expense already about the provi- sional order.</p>
        <p>Councillor Britch : I think it was made per- fectly clear that, pending further steps, we were bound to make application for compu:(my powere, otherwise the whole thing would have to lie in abeyance for twelve months. The Assistant Clerk: The provisional orders have to be in by a certain date in October. • Councillor Lister-Kaye : • I did not think our deputation thought much of it if they were going on with the scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Teachers' Whist Drive.—The teachers held a whist drive and dance in the Council Schools, Stocks-lane. Whist was followed by dancing, and an enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
        <p>Labour Club.—A friendly billiard match took place on Saturday between the .Warrington Junior Liberals and the Penketh Labour Club. The visitors proved too much for the Penketh- ites, the scores being: Junior Liberals, 921; Penketh Labour Club, 854.</p>
