      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Last week's paragraph -headed The Will of Allah." reminded me of the Turk whose chair Was snatched away by a joker as he was about LO sit, and who, falling heavily, calmly accepted it all as inevitable. I imagine that, whether he Gave expression to them or not, 'Sir lierewaid Wake had very different feelings when, after a 4lieech at a farmers' dinner on Saturday, a Bimilar mishap befel him, someone. having tuoved his chair while he was holding forth on the 'subject of Mr. Lloyd George's pheasants. fancy I have previously told how the late M. Keighley Walton, when. Town Clerk-of flail- played a similar trick upon a fellow guest !It a public dinner, and how his victim re- torted by scalping him. Reirig quite bald, Mr. Walton wore a wig, but he was. very sensitive ''l! the subject, so when his covering of artifi- ljal hair was suddenly snatched from lii head Ond thrown across the room, the biter was .-hitten with a vengeance.</p>
        <p>A Alarvel. • On a train journey the other day I fell into e.9nversation with .an old Irishman, appar- ently in comfortable circumstances - so far as .LI OS world's goods are concerned, if not exactly to be envied in a physical sense. Ile confided to me that he was on his way to London for a 81Irgica1 operation, and when I ventured to Press him.for.more.precise information .upon tile point, he said, "Sure, an' Win goan to. have tile shtomach renovated r, "Dear, me." I ex- "filmed, with genuine Interest, "I- should have • - 'considered ;it rather risky at your age." "An' d'ye guess me age at?" he pursued., • . "Oh," I replied, "you must be well over three :'s-core; in. fact, I should say you'll never see 65 'n,gain." "That Oi niVver said he. nor loike of it, for O'im 84." "Well." said I, "a, 'flan who at 84 has a stomach that will stand renovating is a marvel." Irish .Logic.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine who almost invariably goes to Ireland for his summer holidays, and last season tramped over Galway with his camera, told he of a jaunting car driver whom he occa- sionally patronised at one place at which be so/ourned. One hot day, as they 'neared a pub- -house, the " fare " said to the driver, "Are .):011 dry, Nlicky?" "Dhry, is it?" burst out -.lick with emphasis, "faith, an: if ye slitroike 111 Q on the back yeli see the dust iloy out ov me I"</p>
        <p>Ahiscular Christianity. As a youngster my reading was for some- almost exclusively confined to Runyan's "Pilgrim's Progress" and Mrs. Stowe's "Uncle Tom's Cabin," and I think I could pass an ex lnaination in either without special preparation notice. When, therefore, .I read in the laSt, IssUe of this paper what the Rev. T. T. James llad to say at, the Wycliffe. Memorial Hall on /the previous Wednesday night, I immediately IliOught of the Quaker,.Phineas. Fletcher, who, When the foremost of the pursuing pally WilS ‘411)011t to leap into the midst of the little corn- fly that included the escaping slaves, pushed hint into the chasm with his long arms, gently 'Lying "Friend, thee isn't Wanted here." I lutve a dim recollection of another instance in Which a bully struck an inoffensive Illall with- any sort of provocation or excuse, saying NOW turn the other cheek. as your religion "Ilfilches." The assailed org! meekly complied, U, having received a similar blow, and thus ly obeyed the Scriptural injunction, sailed in and wiped the ttOor" with the bully, 14iving him the sound thrashing he .so richly 'Aeserved.</p>
        <p>'too "Previous." , a meeting Of the Stockton Heath authority "IA week it was formally resolved that the pro- "'used erection of a wooden structure in ,bon-lane be granted, whereupon Mr. Whitley I'ePorted front personal observation that the iluilding in question was -already there." The ~•ommittee seem to have taken it all lying down, -11 w :only comment being. Its preniatini,." I ,have been a plileeS where prompt removid of the building would have been insisted upon. NIA the action of the owner was sintilar to that of municipal corporations that apply for auction of a loan for public works already . PI ogress and even well advanced. However, „Local ,Government Board inspectors are at last si),eginning very strongly to resent- that sort of as it is calculated to reduce the public „pi (Miry to a farce, and in future offenders may lid themselves in a quandary.</p>
        <p>.41 Spoilt Nlotorist. II Warning signs are nowadays set, up all along hie highways with We object of preventing the ruPered motorist from breaking his precious tr,ek; an local authorities have even had to go .I(.` length of posting conspicuous notices in- Ining hini of the proximity of it school. tater', wholly disregarding these—as he liabitu- ,,elY does L-the selfish and reckless pleasure- 'theker is convicted of driving to the danger of Phblic, a fellow feeling makes the inagis- li thies wondrous kind, and, as if suspecting may next be the turn of one of their own Cwber, they merely order- the gross offender , PaY the costs, or at most impose a nominal Ibtqlaity.</p>
        <p>• `l,r, SPraying. on account of , the road hogs that addi- „``)flal constables have to be, englaged for point and extra expense has to be incurred in ''e• regular tar-spraying of highways. More- .r, the latter is not only a question of more neY from the public purse ; there is good . to suppose that it is ,a matter affecting the health of COMlTlOnities. A peculiar ,It'oat trouble; which for a., while 4 baffled the .7ecialists, has been finally traced to this source. Ti Minor comptaint comes from bee-keepers. v.leir hitherto profitable winged charges were nt to go steadily along their legitimate busi- ic,ess, but, the ,wholesale use of tar has had a oralising effect upon some of the oceupants he hives. There is something in the tar that. 1„.1)eu15 to the weaker side of bee nature. The 14tUated insects find it irresistible, and gel, in- . ,i':ieated upon it, with the result that, many ' 1710Pelessly lost. The situation has its funny eourse—though the comparatively small •1;"°/1 who happen to be financially concerned - be Pardoned their inability to see just the laugh comes in. 14tuti. 11t.ris es ' Stipends. r iriovllaYe a good deal Of sympathy with the 11-i(biePellt in behalf of the Baptist, Sustenta- (ill; 'kind. It is pitiable to see one afiei• an - 41r of the devoted uteri who have made saeri- by to qualify for the Ministerial life. Obliged andlacii of means to abandon poor churches return to Wade and commerce because their liQedl,and, on the whole, wealthy denomina- -1.4.ah- ,mis not seen its way to make adequate 111 aid. lam personally acquainted with ieneleast three former Baptist ministers whose ,c!st'et, Promising professional careers and fond- t„°Pes have been thus blighted; one became 'titi,,i-aveller, another a bookkeeper, and the litt'utrik a printer's reader. The latter served a .1)14.,u,er of village churches grouped for the Sc, but the people were Poor, and could '441 Maintain a Minister without more substan, tist, cassistance than was forthcoming. Bap- 'tan ,h eh within a short distance' of arring- ‘,lllir W 4(1 to dispense with their minister, and tne to which he went—and he has married g44,eue meantime—took the precaution to ell- 'the chilli for three years 0n1y... However, is in N'lts, °se of the Congregationalists,: the Baptist 14) _Lrentation •Fund may confidently be expected vii gull,' e the necessary help to many a strug- cause.</p>
        <p>A STRIKING EXAMPLE of genuine value is afforded by the fact that Steedman's Powders have had a steadily increasing sale fOX' nearly 100 years, and still stand unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no Poison Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
