      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <p>Reminiscences and Relics of Daniel Lambert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>HOMAGE OF THE SMALLEST EMI TO THE</head>
        <p>THE HOMAGE OF THE SMALLEST EMI TO THE</p>
        <p>GREATEST.</p>
        <p>Nothing is • certain in this world except change—and romance. And romance is sprinkled so evenly through the human family, that it is certain to appear, in the long run, everywhere, even in places that present the least ordinary likelihood. In illustration let the reader peruse the follow- ing clipping from the "Daily Chronicle" of last Saturday :--</p>
        <p>40-STONE BRIDE.</p>
        <p>ROMANCE OF A DESCENDANT OF DANIEL LAMBERT.</p>
        <p>A touch of the village fair has been given th? bustling Strand by "Nellie "—" the fattest lady in the world:" as she herself will tell you, when you have paid your penny. But there is some- thing in Nellie 'apart from constituting a fat .woman show, for she is the embodiment of a delicate romance; in Deceniber, Mr. Ensell, weight-lifter, of. Birmingham, will lead a 40- stone bride to the altar, and the wedding bells will ring for Miss Nellie Lambert, great grand- daughter of the famous Daniel Lambert. Miss Lambert's manager will speak of the betrothal in the terms of cold materialism. He will tell you that the bridegroom-to-be con- templates making his debut on the music hall stage, and, unlike Sandow and Hercules, who lifted horses, he will lift his mammoth bride by his teeth right up on to a silver trapeze. But the corpulent lady herself speaks of her</p>
        <p>, - 'V* '144-N1</p>
        <p>DANIEL LAMBERT,</p>
        <p>and the immense substance of his legs necessi- tated it to be made in the form of a square case. It was built upon two axle trees, and four clog whet's, and upon these the remains of the great man were rolled into his grave in St. Martin's Churchyard. The ordinary methods of lowering a body into the grave were out of the question. The method employed was that of cutting a long and regular descent to the grave through the earth along which the coffin was rolled on its special carriage.</p>
        <p>The apartments which he occupied were on the ground floor. This was from necessity, for he had long been incapable of ascending a stair- case. It was a fortunate thing for • those who had to deal with the body that his death occurred on the ground floor. As it was, the window and part of the wall of the room in which he died had to be taken down to allow pf. the:passage of the coffin. A vast multitude, we are told, looked on in wonder, and followed .the coffin to the grave.</p>
        <p>A</p>
