      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <p>betrothal with shining eyes. "How did you meet the gentleman?" she was asked by a "Daily Chronicle" representative yesterday.</p>
        <p>"Just the same as you met your wife," re- sponded Miss Lambert, promptly, as though there was nothing in being of excessive size to render doing things "just the same" as other people impossible. "I was walking along the street, and came across a young gentleman I knew, and he was with another young gentle- man. He asked me to go and have a drink— and there you are," -continued the bride-elect. Considering that, by all accounts, the lady with 27-inch biceps can scrub and cook without the slightest inconvenience, and that she makes all her own clothes; Mr. Ensell may, perhaps, be congratulated upon gaining a bride "among ten thousand."</p>
        <p>If Miss Lambert be a bride among ten thousand she can boast a (treat grandfather who was a num among a hundred millions.</p>
        <p>Did not Daniel Lambert boast himself the " greatest " man who ever lived? And does not authentic evidence bear out his claim? Let us briefly set forth the facts. Daniel Lambert was the son of a Daniel Lam bort, of Leicester, who was formerly a hunting master, but who became keeper of Leicester gaol. Daniel, jun., was born March 13th 1770, and after an apprenticeship served in. the en- graved button, trade succeeded his father as keeper of the gaol in 1791.</p>
        <p>It was not until this period of his life that he began to show signs of corpulency. In his youth he had been famed as a swimmer and walker, and athletics had been his hobby. lie began now to develop enormously in his 'body and lower limbs, and rapidly became an object of wonder. • Unfitted for an active life, it seems to have occurred to him to turn his physical develop- inent to profit. He designed .and had built for himself a special carriagt,- and journeyed in this to London, taking apartments in Picca- dilly, where he "received visitors" from eleven to five O'clock. The public imagination was touched, and crowds flocked to see the human curiosity, whose wit made him a very enter- taining host. • We have been fortunate enough to discover one of Mr. Lambert's advertisements, issued during this. visit to London in 1806.</p>
        <p>EXHIBITION.—Mr. Daniel Lambert, of Leicester, the heaviest man that ever lived, who, at the age of thirty-six years, weighs upwards of fifty stone (fourteen pounds to the stone), or eighty-seven stones four pounds, London weight, which is ninety-one pounds more than the great Mr. Bright weighed. Mr. Lambert will see company at his house, No. 53. Piccadilly, next Albany, nearly opposite St. James' Church, from eleven to five o'clock. Tickets of ad- mission, one shilling each.</p>
        <p>One of his guests wrote a description of his appearance which says:</p>
        <p>When sitting he appears to be a stupendous mass' of flesh, for his thighs are so covered by his belly that nothing but his knees are to be seen, while the flesh of,his legs, which resemble pillows, projects in such a manner as to nearly bury his feet.</p>
        <p>Yet the limbs are well proportioned, and his face is described as " manly and intelligent."</p>
        <p>Again in 1807 Mr. Lambert "received com- pany" in London at the same modest fee as before, and afterwards travelled through some provincial towns, sojourning at inns, and giv- ing local society food for their -wonder and a new topic for discussion. But he did not long follow the course he had marked out for himself. At Stamford he died suddenly at the Waggon and Horses Inn in June, 1809, and was there buried. A scribe of the period, endeavouring perhaps to fit his style to the massive subject he had in hand, wrote in a local newspaper:</p>
        <p>Nature had et,ldured all the trespass she could admit; the poor man's corpulency had con- stantly increased, until, at the time we have mentioned, the clogged machinery of life stood still, and this prodigy of mammon was num- bered with the dead.</p>
        <p>Another does the more useful service of giv- ing the facts and figures relating to his uncom- mon funeral. Here they arc:</p>
        <p>liis coffin contained 112 superficial feet of elm, and was six feet four inches long, four feet four inches wide, and two feet four inches deep;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>MR. JOHN NAYLOR'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN NAYLOR'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Lambert's epitaph is something of a curiosity, and we had better quote it:</p>
        <p>In remembrance of that prodigy in nature, Daniel Lambert 'a native of Leicester, who was possessed of an excellent and convivial mind, and in personal greatness he had no competitor. He measured three feet one inch round the leg; nine feet four inches round the body; and weighed 52 stone 111 h. (141 b. to the stone). He departed this life on the 21st of June, 1809, aged 39 years. As ,4 testimony of respect this • stone is erected by his friends at Leicester.</p>
        <p>\V*, have not had. the advantage of a visit to burial-place of Daniel Lambert, but, in re- spons to a, communication to Mr. John Nay- lor, have received a very .interesting letter re- lating to the giant and his clothes.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Mounfield,— My brother .and myself walked home from London by a 'rather circuitous route in 1870. We occupied two weeks and, Sundays excepted, we walked 306 miles in the twelve days we were on the road. Amongst other places visited we came to Stamford, which we were told was 'in three counties, but chiefly in Lincolnshire, and we heard of the great Daniel Lambert, Who had died and been buried there, and whose clothes were to be seen at the London Inn. We went there, and each of us passed thrOugh the leg of o. pair of his breeches. He was a native of Leicester, and -travelled through the country, exhibiting himself, and died, at Stamford dur- ing one of his tours. The great difficulty was to bury himoand it is said that the corpse and .the coffin approached a ton in weight. It was impossible .to lower him in the grave in the usual way, so something in the nature of an inclined plane was made down to his last rest- ing-place, a new cemetery or an addition to the churchyard making this possible. I was in Stamford in 1912 and called on a friend of mine, who has charge of a small fac- tory there, and whO had heard of Daniel, but did not know his clothes were in the town, so inquiries were made, and we found that they were in the institute, and we went to see them. A. charge of one shilling is now made to visitors, and we inspected the clothes, as well as a suit. which belonged to the smallest man, General Tom Thumb,' who must have visited Stamford after our visit in 1870. The General managed to get through one of Daniel's coat sleeves,, but it had to be slit about four inches at the wrist to enable him to get through, and he left a suit of his own clothes in memory of his visit. You will see that Lambert's weight was chiefly in the lower portion of his body, and in his legs, and that he did not carry his weight propor- tionately in his figure, which is rather a "monstrosity." His hat is also to be seen, which appears to have been a beaver, and would fit any ordinary, head, but the pile has been worn off the' surface.</p>
        <p>William Ball, the Shropshire giant, carried his weight much better than Lambert, but he was not so heavy. enclose you his card, and have seen the crooked stick and his chair at Horsehay, and the manager oTF the iron works there tqld me that when Ball was alive there was a great pnicession, and they wished Ball to walk at the head of it, to ithow what kind of man they had at Horsehay, but his legs were too short, and his body too heavy to keep up with the band, so they gpt the biggest horse they could find, but Ball could not get on it, nor could they lift him on it, as he was scwt. or more in weight, so they brought him in the iron works and hoisted him on the horse's back with a crane, but the horse had not gone more than 30 yards, when its ,back broke and Ball came down and the horse as well. 'Colonel Paget, the director of the Shropshire Iron Works, told me he could just remember Ball, and saw him drink a quart of ale from a jug without taking breath. -</p>
        <p>A few lines may, perhaps, be devoted to an- other prodigy of flesh, who came within reach of being d rival of the incomparable Daniel. This was the " great Mr. Bright," who is re- ferred to in Lambert's advertisement, and who is there surveyed from the lofty standpoint which ninety-one pounds of extra flesh gave to the advertiser.</p>
        <p>Mr.- Bright .(we cannot discover his Christian name) was a grocer of Ntaldon, in, Essex, and came of a family with a notoriety for appe- tite and bulk. Bright.was born to 'greatness in the most liberal sense, and from earliest youth was of -Unwieldy bulk. lie married at the age of #, and had five children. Cheerful in every relationship and event of life, he bocame popu- lar and beloved by his family and TOghboars. He was in repute as a man of honesty and was known through all the countryside. It was a sorrow of no common kind when death seized him a.t‘the age of thirty. Yet those who lamented him most could but regard his death as a release to him- '</p>
        <p>His weight was 616 pounds, and life had becoine so much of a burden that he had often, in spite of his cheery acquiescence in all that Providence sent to him, expressed a wish to be released from his burden. His cotlin was three feet six inches •broad at the shoulders, and more than three feet in depth. To remove this huge box from the house a way had to be cut through the wall and staircase, and the coffin lowered by mechanical means to Ms shop. The funeral was so remarkable that crowds of curious onlookers gatheied from far and near to witness it. The coffin WI'S drawn to church upon a wheeled carriage, drawn by twelve men; an engine was fixed upon the church, and by means of this the eollin was raised from the carriage and lowered into the grave. Al t&amp; his death a wager was offered that five men, each twenty-one years of age, could be placed inside his waistcoat and buttoned in., ['his seemed ,an incredible thing, but the bet having been made, a test was arranged at the Black Bull Inn, at his native Mahlon. • The .person who had offered the bet -secured the presence of a string of slim youths of twenty-One and upwards, and the test began. Of these young men, seven were easily but- toned up in the waistcoat without a stitch or button being strained. England-seemS to have, been enillent with her past efforts at producing great men. In recent</p>
        <p>(Cont. inued in next column.)</p>
        <p>generations there have certainly been none to challenge comparison with Bright and ,Lam- bert. Nor, indeed, as far as our knowledge goes, have former generations had anything so massive to show.</p>
        <p>Lambert's immense superiority in size over one of the largest of ordinary large men is re- vealed In a story which is told of a Mr. Palmer, a public-house keeper, of Brompton. This gentleman weighed 245 stone, and was so bulky that five ordinary people could get inside his coat. Bather proud of his size, he refused to believe that Lambert could be a larger man, and made a visit to him ,at London. But the publican looked a mere pigmy when beside the gigantic Daniel. The disappointment affected him greatly, and he died three weeks later—of a 'broken heart, his friends declared.</p>
        <p>Whi(th shows that human vanity can take curious turns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
