      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>TA BL E TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of interesiing Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>A black tulip would be worth perhaps £lOO, said Mr. H. Essenhigh Corks, F.8.H.5., in a re- cent lecture on Hower photography. Black was the only shade which tulip growers had found it impossible to produce by cross fertilisation. Underground Garage for Chicago.</p>
        <p>For relieving the down-town streets of Chicago of the congestion caused by the 'park- ing of hundreds of automobiles along the kerb- ing, it .is proposed to build an underground garage, 13 blocks long. It is estimated that such a garage would take care of more than 2,000 cars daily. If built as planned, this will be the largest and most novel garage in the world, and will cost over 1,250,000 dollars.</p>
        <p>Strychnine in Stolen Apples.</p>
        <p>The charming village in Upper Wharfedale, to which Captain Kettle retired after his amaz- ing adventures, has been chuckling over a notice in the window of the largest shop, which is also the post office:. It reads: "May I sug- gest to the individual who stole my apples that it is inadvisable to eat' the specimens .that have been dosed with strychnine for the birds?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Wild" Sheep from Sardinia.</head>
        <p>Wild" Sheep from Sardinia.</p>
        <p>By the •generositi of the Duke of Bedford, President of the ZOological Society, a little flock of the mouflon (ovis inusimon) the wild sheep of. Corsica and. Sardinia, has bOn obtained for the collection in Regent's Park. Unfortunately, says "The Times," this relic of the primitive European fauna is on the verge of extinction. It has long disappeared from Spain, has been extinct in Sicily fel' half a century, and even in Corsica and Sardinia there are few survivors of the genuine wild stock. Some of these are being disposed of by their owner, and were pur- chased by Hagenbeck, of Hamburg, to the order of the Mice of Bedford..</p>
        <p>A Well-paid Player. The four-figure transfer fees paid in English _football sink into significance .beside an item which comes from New York. The record surn £20,000 has been offered to the Detroit Base= ball Club for the sale of Mr. Tyrus Cobb, America's most spectacular player, to the Niashington Baseball Club. The offer has Teated .much talk- in the baseball world. Mr. Cobb is probably the fastest baseball player living. As base runner his performances are unique, and hove for several years past proved so popular with the public and.so profitable to •his club that at the beginning of this season his salary was raised from zel,Boo to £3,100.</p>
        <p>Signposts to Welcome Travellers.</p>
        <p>Motorists are received in a very courteous manner by the city of Aurora, Illinois. Signs stationed like outposts on all the roads form a reception cormnittee to welcome the tourist and put hill' in a pleasant humour before he crosses [lie city limits. Instead of 'frightening ,him aw4y with a warning of a fine for exceeding the speed *limit, the 'signs merely, give him a kindly hint that excessive speed is not desir- able. To a motorist. approaching the city, the signs read, "Aurora. Welconie to Our city; Watch Out for Cops!" and as he leaves the city, the other side of each sign says, "So Long. Come often and Stay Longer."</p>
        <p>Grain Elevator Destroyed by Dust •Explosion. An example of the destructive -effect of a grain-dust explosion was 'given in Buffalo, New York, -recently, when an explosion with conse- quent tire destroyed the greater part of one of the largest grain-elevator plans in the country. About 170 men were working in the plant. at the time of the explosion, and of these over 30 were killed and a larger, number injured. The plant occupied an area 500 feet long and 170 feet wide, and comprised an elevator with ad- jacent steel storage bins, and a number of con- a.ete grain bins, as well as .the usual engine and boiler rooms and service buildings.</p>
        <p>Piano Music ,Sent by Telephone. By connecting a player piano to the county telephone system a piano company in Indiana is in position to supply music to the homes of all the telephone subscribers, and at the same thne advertise their business in an effective manner. At first the scheme attracted only a curious crowd, but later the company began to receive hundreds of telephone calls from persons anxious to hear their favourite selec- tions. The music as ,it comes .over the line is of good tone, and where party lines are in operation, as many as 12 families frequently hear the concert at one time.</p>
        <p>'White Heather.</p>
        <p>A profitable 'market having been discovered for white heather. in London, the bloom has evidently been carefully cultivated, with the re- sult that it is unusually abundant. this summer. Street sellers crying "Lucky white heather," do a ready trade a penny a sprig, and in the shops it is twisted into, horseshoe shape to en- hance the:4;ol. It has become a favourite feature of bridgl bouquet's. Some people think that the white heather for sale in the streets is the commoner variety bleached for the purpose .of greater' profit, but. as a matter of fact the sprays now on sale are genuine and in the natural state. Most of the supplies at Covent Garden' come from Scotland. thougli the output is growing from Yorkshire and Cornwall.</p>
        <p>Criminal's Dry Humour. Perhaps the. most noteworthy ,of Deteetive- Sergeant Tupper's cases at Tottenham, from thence he is now retiring, and one illustrating the somewhat dry humour of some of the criminals, was that in which a man who styled himself on a board over his premipes as having been " in Government service for 16 years " was sentenced to seven years, and is still in prison. This man called himself a builder and contrac- tor, and he induced the owner of a large estate of houses at Tottenham .to allow 'him to look after the property and take the rents. The sequel was heard at the Old Bailey. where it was related that he had wrecked nearly all the houses, actually tearing away the gas and water pipes and selling them. It was then found that his claim to have been' In the Uov- ernment service for 16 years was reallya fact— for he had already been 16 years in prison!</p>
        <p>At Southport on Saturday Mary Lady Gerard presented a life-saving medal to I'atrol Eric Boston, aged 18, of the Southport Troop of Boy Scouts, for rescuing -a woman in a rough sea while swimming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <p>THE " GREATEST " MAN WHO EVER LlVED—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS</head>
        <p>WORLD OF LETTERS</p>
        <p>LIVING WAGE AND COST OF LIVING</p>
        <p>Notes oil Useful Books.</p>
        <p>The two questions of the living_ wage and the cost of living have a very practicable interest just nbw for employers, shareholders, wage- earners, and the great consuming public. They need to be studied carefully in an atmosphere free from partisanship. Thetuild of Help has formed a Study Circle, to which alt are wel- come. The reading is done at home, but dis- cussions are 'held on the third Monday in each month. The following notes will give to a wider public some idea Of the contents of books written on the subject. An expendiiture of, say, 2s. 6d. or less, will suffice to give an insight into the -problem.</p>
        <p>I Living Vage. Macmillan, 2s. net. Written by Professor Ryan, a Roman Catho- lic . priest, who lectures on Ethics and Economics at an American seminary. It deals with the history of the question; the forces that regulate prices, e.g., rent, profits and interest, and. wages; and the obligations corresponding to the right to a living wage. A careful and exact piece of work.</p>
        <p>The Living Wage. !fodder and Stoughton, is. - net.. By Alt.. Philip Snowden. Directly bears Upon the problem in the United Kingdom. Pt shows the cost of poverty to the State, .the cOst and futility of strikes, deals with conciliation, and mentions experimdilts in the Colonies and under the Trade, Boards Act, and faces objec- tions and. difficulties. The appendices contain the text of three recent Acts of Parliament. The ' book is written for the general public. Wages. Mowbray, is. 6d. net:</p>
        <p>One of the handbooks of the Christian Social Union, edited by Canon Scott Holland. The author is Dr. A. J. Carlyle, lecturer at Univer- sity College, Oxford. This book deals 'almost exclusively with economic theory (Adam Smith, lalthus, Ricardo, Mill and others). It discusses various methods of fixing and adjusting wages. An excellent book for students.</p>
        <p>Sweated Labour and the Trade Boards Art. Catholic Truth Society, 6d. net. By various authors. The title well expresses the contents. The second edition brings it up to date.</p>
        <p>Ileadley Bros., is. net. By Cadbury and Shann. A useful and lucid statement of conditions previous to the passing of the Trade Boards Act of 1909.</p>
        <p>Poverty: A Study of Town Life. Macmillan, Is. net. A cheap re-issue of Mr. flowntree's book, which became famous by drawing "the poverty line."</p>
        <p>How the Labourer Lives. Nelson, 2s. net. A recent book by the same author—equally dispassionate and accurate. It consists chiefly of a series of family budgets of Tura] labourers. Despite many statistics, it is vital with human interest.</p>
        <p>U the Works. Nelson, Is. net. A cheap reprint of Lady Bell's interesting survey --of conditions in Middlesbrough, with special reference to the lives of ironworkers. ,-I.bout 50 pp. devoted to family budgets. Other useful pamphlets are.—" State Arbitra- tion,"," flOme Work and Sweating," 'The Case for a Legal Minimum 'Wage," 'Food Prices in London," "Leo XI I L's Encyclical, Berm Novarum," "Family Life On £1 per week," and "The State and the Wage Earner." Government- Publications:—</p>
        <p>6955. Price 4s. 11d. "Cost of Living of Working Classes." This Volume is crammed with useful facts, comparibg in detail prices Ind rents in 1905 with those of 1912. If every Chamber of Commerce had a Standing Com- mittee on Blue Books, this is one that would be digested.</p>
        <p>6496. Price 9d. - " Profit-sharing and Labour Co-partnership," showing the rise and fall Of profit-sharing'from 1870 to middle of 1912. 5963. Price sd. "Expenditure of Wage- earning Women and Girls." An interesting collection of household budgets, which ought to be read by all who want to study the wage question, as it Specially touches female labour. 7080. Price 111 d, "Changes in Wages and Flours in 1912, with comparative _statistics. 4167. Price Is. 10d. "Wages' Boards, &amp;c., in Nustralia and New Zealand, 1908."</p>
        <p>Note: All the above will be found at the Public Library. with the exception of "Sweated Labour," and Nos. 5,963 and 4,167.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>DO YOU KNOW TIIAT</p>
        <p>" Blue-stocking " originates from a Mr. Stil- linglleet, who was a .favourite member of a literary gathering in the time of Dr. Johnson, and who always wore blue stockings. If he happened to be absent all asked, "Where is our blue-stocking?" and from this the term for ladies of literary pretensions was derived.</p>
        <p>Husband moans house-bound; wife, weaving one; son is the cleaner; daughter, is the milker; spinster is the unmarped sister of husband or wife, who is the spinner. If spiders in spinning their webs make the terminating filaments long it may be concluded that the weather will continue serene for ten or twelve days, according to the length of the filaments.</p>
        <p>Pocket handkerchief is the queerest word in the language. At first it was kerchief (couvre- chef), a covering for the head; then it became handkerchief, a covering 'for the head carried in the hand, and at last pocket handerkchief, covering for the head, held in the hand and kept in the pocket. Hydropathy, or the applicatiOn of water to the cure of disease, was known as early as 1855, when a Doctor Heberden, of London, placed his patients in a damp house and made them sleep between damp sheets. It astonished other physicians that sheets were dipped into a spring, then wrapped around the patients, who were put to bed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>RAILWAY WAR ON DISEASE.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY WAR ON DISEASE.</p>
        <p>The Pennsylvania Railroad has established a rule-which shall debar from employment in its dining car and restaurant department any per- son with even a tendency toward a communi- cable disease. There is to be .a quarterly physi- cal examination of every employe who has any- thing whatsoever to do with the preparation or serving of food. Dishwashers, kitchen helpers, cooks and waiters, both white and coloured, ,must undergo a rigid .examination every three months, and only a 100 per cent. report on their physical • condition will permit their remaining in the service. While those suffering from tuberculosis, diseases of the eye or skin, and any other communicable disease, may not be employed ,in any capacity where they might come in contact with food, the further precau- tion is taken in debarring them from employ- ment in places where linens and table-ware are kept.</p>
        <p>If a Pennsylvania flailroad dining car or restaurant employe becomes disabled through any cause, he shall not be permitted to return to work until, he receives a "certificate of ability" from one of the company's doctors. In order to .surround the service with every pos- sible precaution, there are provided at lay-over points for dining car stewards,- waiters, and ,cooks, special lodgings with clean linen, clean beds, and every sanitary protection. These lodgings are inspected personally by the super- intendent of dining cars and restaurants, by service inspectors, and by medical examiners of !lie company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
