      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <p>;4.4.4.4.4.4..:..x.+4.++++++++,4.4.0++4.4.4.4.4.4.4.++4.++4.4.4.04.444.4.4 • V t + .:, CHILDREN'S CORNER • + ; + • • * Conducted by UNCLE JACK * * • * 4. .++•:••++..:.+.:..:..:..:.4..:..:.++.:. •:•.:.+.:..:.+. AN INTERESTING COMPETITION. (</p>
        <p>My dear Children,— As you will see from the poems pub- lished below, we have had a most interesting competition. It has been no easy matter to judge who should become our prize-winners, all being so equal in point of merit. sl.',would have liked to have awarded all a prize, but that would not do, would it? • If everyone knew they were getting a prize, all the interest and excitement would be taken out of the contest.</p>
        <p>, A Horrid Word. I'd like to do a lot of things, But all of them seem Wrong, And anything that isn't nice, I may do all day long.</p>
        <p>I'd like to wind up father's clock, And make the hands go round, But when I try, he calls out "Don't; It's only just been wound.".</p>
        <p>I have decided thiS time to give three prizes, namely, to the following:— BENNY HAMPSON, 195, Knutsford-road, War- rington; . _ _</p>
        <p>When I slide down the bannisters, Or bang the bedroom door, Mother gets so cross and says, "Now don't; I've told you so before."</p>
        <p>IfILDA PALMER, 3, Worsley-street, Warrington; LEONARD DICKENSON, 17, Stevenson-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>If I forget my lessons once, My teacher is hard on me, And says, "Now don't do that again," Then keeps me in till three.</p>
        <p>I am -very grateful to all of you who took part, and I hope that next time your, names will be in our prize list. I am going to tell you soon about a big competition we are to have for Christmas.</p>
        <p>But when I try to do my sums, And work with all my might, She never says, "Now don't do that r Or help to put them right.</p>
        <p>Books will be sent on to our prize-winners in the course of the next few days. Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>U'hen I have grown a bigger girl, I'll please myself all day, For then they can't say "Don't," to me In such a heartless way. VERA JONES, 12, St. Mary-street, Latchford.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>I am pleased to welcome the following new members this week :</p>
        <p>ETHEL Er.KERSLEY, 31, Ellesmere-street, War- rington. I{OBERT ECKERSLEY, 31, Elleknere-street, War- rington. JAMES ECKERSLEY, 31, Ellesmere-street, War- rington.,</p>
        <p>. Morning. Awake, little girl, it is tune to arise, Come, shake 'drowsy sleep from your eye; The lark is loud warbling his notes to the skies, And the sun is far mounted on high.</p>
        <p>Oh, come, for the fields with gay flow'rets o'er- flow,</p>
        <p>THEn COMPETITION POETRY. About Two Lads. TWO lads on their way to school, An orchard passing fair, Where every branch bowed down With apples they did bear.</p>
        <p>The dewdrop is trembling still; The lowing herds graze in the pastures below, And the sheep bell is heard from the hill.</p>
        <p>Oh- come, for the bee has flown out of his bed, To begin his employment anew; l'he spider is weaving her delicate thread, Which brilliantly glitters with dew.</p>
        <p>Two lads passed on their way, Two pairs of longing eyes Saw the host of golden apples, And drew two deep long sighs.</p>
        <p>Oh, come, for the ant has crept out of her cell, Again to her labour she goes; She knows the true value of moments too well, To waste them in idle repose.</p>
        <p>Two thoughts joined each other, . Two lads scaled the wall, But they didn't get the apples, Or what they like at all.</p>
        <p>Awake, little sleeper, and do not despise 01 insects instruction to ask; From your pillow with good resolutions arises And cheerfully go to your task. MINNIE DODD, 50, Steel-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Farmer Hollins saw them coming, And waited for them there, The farmer caught the culprits, And the* farmer birched the pair. BENNY I lAMPSON, 135, Knutsford-road, Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Cottager To Her Infant. The days are cold, the nights are long, The north wind sings a doleful song; , Then hush'again upon my breast, All merry things are now at rest, Save thee, my pretty love!</p>
        <p>The Shadow Girl. ..Vherever I go I alwttys see ridiculous girl following me, :oinetimes at my hack, sometimes at my side, he mounts a black pony whenever I ride. She does not speak and she does not laugh, But sometimes she hides a little while.</p>
        <p>The kitten sleeps upon the hearth, The crickets long have ceased their mirth; There's nothing stirring in the house Save one wee, hungry, nibbling mouse. -Then why so busy tbou?</p>
        <p>It's no use tz'ying to drive her away, . Or to go and leave her; I tried to-day, We got into a carriage and drove so fast, - • That I thought I was rid of the girl at last, But when we jumped out and walked up the hill, jumped out, too, and she follows me still.</p>
        <p>Nay! start not at that sparkling light, , 'Tis but the moon that shines so bright On the window pane bedropped with rain; There, little darling, sleep again, And wake when it is -day. STANUY PLEVIN, 46, New-road, Latchford.</p>
        <p>You needn't suppose that she looks like me, For she's just as different as she can be. She is short in the morning, broad at noon, and at night she is long, like a silver spoon. • I don't change like that, I grow and grow, But it hardly shows, it's so very slow.</p>
        <p>Daffodils.</p>
        <p>I wandered lonely as a cloud, That floats on high i o'er vales and hills, When all at once I saw a crowd, . . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>I have to Watch her quite closely, too, Or she'd do some things she ought not to do. She almost picked mamma's rose to-day, But when I saw her she ran away. Oh, dear! Oh, dear! my head's in a whirl With the pranks of that Inischievous shadow . _</p>
        <p>A host of golden daffodils, Beside the lake, beneath the trees, Fluttering and dancing in the breeze.,</p>
        <p>Continuous as the stars that shine;</p>
        <p>And twinkle on the milky way, They stretched in never-endiog line</p>
        <p>Along the margin of a bay ;- Ten thousand saw I at a glance, Tossing their heads in sprightly dance.</p>
        <p>HILnA PALMER, 3, Worsley-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>A Bit of Fun: I'm gi little white puppy, •. And I've had a lot of fun; I've eaten Daisy's supper And little Charlie's bun.</p>
        <p>The waves beside them danced, but they Outdid the sparkling waves in glee; A poet could not but be gay, In such a jocund company. I gazed, and, gazed, but little thought, What wealth the show to me had brought.</p>
        <p>I've torn up seven picture-books— They made a lovely noise— I've broken baby's dollies, And scattered all the toys.</p>
        <p>For oft when on my couch I lie, In vacant or in pensive mood; They flash upon that inward eye, • Which fs the bliss of solitude, And then my heart with pleasure fills, And dances with the daffodils. MINNIE BAYNE, 12, Warburton-street, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>I've made a dozen pieces • Of baby's little shoe, And worried nurse's slipper, Till it came in pieces, too.</p>
        <p>My Native Land.</p>
        <p>I got into the scuttle, And made mtrself so black, And now I'm rather frightened, For nurse is coming back.</p>
        <p>Breathes there a man, with soul so dead, Who never to himself tuith said, This is my own, my native land! Whose heart bath neer within him burned, As home his footsteps he hath turned, From wandering on a ,foreign strand! If such there breathe, go, mark him well, For him no minstrel raptures swell; High though his titles, proud his name, Boundless his wealth as wish can claim; Despite those. titles, power and pelf, The wretch concentred all in self, Living, shall forfeit fair renown, And, doubly dying, shall go own to the Vile dust, from whence he sprung, Unwept, unhonoured and unsung.</p>
        <p>And when she sees the litter, She'll be as cross as cross; But I really only meant it For a bit of fun, of course. LEONARD. DICKENSON, 17, Stevenson-street, Warrington</p>
        <p>"Too Late." Too late to rise, too late for school, Too late to keep by each good rule; The sluggard soon becomes a fool; Oh, never be "too late."</p>
        <p>Sent by EDWARD DICKENSON, 17: Stevenson- street, Warrington; and also by ,MAuntc.E MCKINLEY, 208, Orford-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Oh, use the precious hours to-day To gather knowledge while you may, For quickly hasteth time away; Then never .be "too late."</p>
        <p>And grateful to your parents be, For tenderly they've eared for thee, And soon on earth you may them see lklo more, and mourn "too late."</p>
        <p>And to thy suffering brother man Give aid and comfort while ye can, Aye, like the good Samaritan, Ere yet it be "to late."</p>
        <p>To all death hasteth on apace. Then seek thy Heavenly Father's grace, Ere yet it be "too late." ADA FBEETH, 24, Manchester-road, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>Day in Future.</head>
        <p>The Day in Future.</p>
        <p>Have courake, boys, .to. do the right.;</p>
        <p>Be bold, be brave, be'strong ; By doing right, you gain the might To overcome the wrong.</p>
        <p>Do the work that's nearest, Though it's dull at times, Helping, when you meet therni. Lame dogs over stiles. GasEnT COOKSON, 29, Folly-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Lite, I know Not What Thou Art. Life, I know not what thou art, But know that thou and I must part, And when, or how, or where we met,. I own to me's a secret yet.</p>
        <p>Life, we've been long together, Through pleasant and through cloudy weather; 'l' is hard to part when friends are dear; Perhaps 'twill cost a sigh, a tear;</p>
        <p>Then steal- away, give little warning ; Choose thine own time; Say not good-night, but 'in some brighter clime, Bid me good morning.</p>
        <p>GLADYS NOLAN, 93, Weg-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>LIVED IN FEAR OF AMPUTATION !</head>
        <p>LIVED IN FEAR OF AMPUTATION !</p>
        <p>Zamißuk Heals Liverpool Woman's Ulcer-Ridden Legs. ,</p>
        <p>Mrs. Jessie Blackburne's legs got into such'an awful state with ulcers that she lived in daily dread of amputation. Thanks to Zam-Buk, however, the disease-ridden limbs are now thor- oughly cleansed and healed with now healthy skin. Mrs. Blackburne, who lives at 258, Crown- street, Myrtle-street, Liverpool, said to a reporter_:— _</p>
        <p>Little red pimples came on my left leg about eighteen months ago. They grew as big as half- crowns, and then burst into ulcers. Similar sores also broke out on my right leg. I was so badly crippled and in such pain that I was com- pelled to he on the sofa and leave my husband and children to look after themselves. I got very little sleep, and had to lie with my legs out of bed, propped up on a chair, as I dare not let even the bedclothes touch the sore places. "There were ten discharging ulcers on my left leg and three on my right leg, and my daily dread was that the limbs would have to be am- putated. After being bedfast for weeks I had treatment at a skin hospital in Liverpool. " Then having heard a lot about Zam-Buk„ and as my legs didn't look like healing up, I got a box of this herbal balm from the chemist. I got decided relief from the very first dressings of Zam-Buk. In fact, each time I put the rich balm on the ulcers I felt its soothing and cool- ing influence. _ _</p>
        <p>Perseverance with this splendid Zam-Buk treatment resulted in every ulcer being gradu- ally soothed, cleansed and healed with new healthy skin. My legs got quite strong again, and do not trouble me at all now. Zam-Bnk has worked a grand cure in my severe case." You cannot safely do without Zam-Buk, the unique herbal balm, that cures eczema, ulcers, ringworm, scalp sores, poisoned wounds, and soothes and heals cuts. bruises, &amp;c. Sold in sealed boxes only, 1/11 and 2/9.</p>
        <p>Mr. 6yril Asquith, who underwent an opera- tion for appendicitis, is making very favourablco- progress, and the Prime Minister was able to go out of town for the week-end.</p>
      </div>
