      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>EXAMINER, S A TUE DA Y , OCTOBER 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S A TUE DA Y , OCTOBER 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Wu the Recent Conference a Political Meeting?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>GUARANTEE BOND QUESTION.</head>
        <p>GUARANTEE BOND QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly- meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. Linaker, jun., presiding.</p>
        <p>Appointments. On the recommendation of the House Com- mittee, Miss F. E. Partington was appointed assistant nurse,l and Miss Lilian Hall charge nurse at the Workhouse. The Local Government Board wrote stating that they offered no objection to the proposals of the Guardians of the Runcorn Union in re- gard to the provision of medicines, drugs, appli- ances, and dressings required for use in the treatment of the inmates of the Workhouse and Children's' Homes. The Board approved of the appointment of Dr. T. J. Selby, medical officer of the Workhouse, to act also as medical officer of the Children's Homes, with a salary of ~t 25 per annum for, his services,in that capacity.</p>
        <p>A Vaccination Question. The Guardians of the Blackburn Union asked the Board to support the following resolution: "That this Board regrets that recent legisla- tion has made the vaccination law practically inoperative and hopes the Government will ap- point a committee to inquire .into the carrying out of the clauses of the Act of 1906 relating to conscientious objections." It was decided to support the resolution. The Clerk to the Whitley Superior Parish Council wrote regretting the death of Mr. H. C. Wright, one of the Overseers. At a meeting of the Council held on the 9th inst...llr B. Brad- ford was appointed to fill the vacancy. Mr. Morris asked whether the resolution with regard to Assistant Overseers entering into a Guarantee Society had been put into force, and if it had been done ifl. every case. ; The Chairman : Every new one that is ap- pointed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>TRAFIALCIPif.,-..' DAY.</head>
        <p>TRAFIALCIPif.,-..' DAY.</p>
        <p>Children's Lack of Interest in the Shield</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>Competition.</head>
        <p>Competition.</p>
        <p>AN HISTORIC TROPHY.</p>
        <p>It would indeed be very regrettable, undigni- fied and ignominious if the patriotism of War- rington's school children was to be judged by the manner in which the competition inaugur- ated by Alderman Bennett eight years ago, has been taken up this year, for out of about 14,000 children there were but eight entrants. The shield, presented by the Alderman, is of 'copper, mounted on old oak, and it is of interest that the copper is from Lord NelAon's flagship the "Victory." The winning school this year was Fairfield girls, whilst the second prize was awarded to Arpley-street. The prizes were pre- sented on Trafalgar -Day, which fell upon Tues- day, and the presentations aroused much interest.</p>
        <p>response the children recited heartily the well- known words: "England expects every man will do his duty." He. asked Marie Bolesworth to ,accept the shield on behalf of the Fairfield Girls' School. In addition to the hooks which Accompanied the shield, the youthful winner was the recipient of two prizes from her schoolmates, congratu- lating her upon her success. Marie Bolesworth is the daughter of Mr. Albert Edward Bolesworth, of 21, King Edward- str, Pt, Warrington. - The Rev. J. &amp;MUSTER proposed a Vote of thangs to. Alderman Bennett, and this was, seconded by Councillor J. G. CnAnc, who said it must have grieved Alderman Bennett because so few had competed. It was certainly a great surprise to him. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, congratulated the scholars that they had such a head mistress, and the head mistress upon hexing such a splendid lot Of girls to be trained under her care. • It was a very excellent programme that was 1 provided, and it included the songs " Forty years on," "0 the oak and the ash," a 13,0,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>FAIRFIELD GIRLS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD GIRLS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A very pleasing programme had been arranged by Mrs. Stansfield and her staff, when songs and recitations appropriate to the day were rendered. Citnon Willis presided, and supporting him were the Rev. J. Buckmaster, Alderman A. Bennett, Councillor J. G. Craik, Mr. A. 3. Jolley -(vice- chairman of the Education Committee), and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <p>Photograph by Burrell</p>
        <p>HAROLD EMMERSON,</p>
        <p>:on of Mr. Henry'Enimerson, 44, Willis-street. 'Wilmer of the gmlior. (Boys') prize at the</p>
        <p>Secondary School</p>
        <p>f:iture they would have a more encouraging entry than on that occasion.. He suggested that they should adopt ,the method tried at the Secondary School and the Grammar School; where every child in the school wrote an essay. In the essays it was the ideals behind the sub- ject which counted. He did not look for liter- ary merit, although it was acceptable; he wanted to make good citizens.. The Alderman' askod: the children for Nelson's famous motto, and in</p>
        <p>-_,,,-----, - - , ..,'.....F.!:!..;',,A:;: ''".:3...,-.Z k.',.'"".1,?:',,9,:7.,,-..:;•,,, ; '-,,. :: --, "'': • '''" ' „ A4",A, ,I,' • ' ‘'‘ .:. • ' i,„,,,, t' -,' • ....... , . . H 1 ' ... ' --..,, .:•'. ' 1 ','. .. *-- '' '.. --• ''' :.• - , ' :,..,3,---,,,s''' t,,., ... .., ' ..,.',, • .. • V:, ' ......4 ; t t,,,, 1 , , .O ~,,-, ..-..., .... - -sii?4,•:- ' ...i. '.: . -;:,,e, ,• - •'''''' ',, ,-7 , ..--,,,,,,,,,,... • •-- 4t,,</p>
        <p>''.112.410 -4,N6 rj</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>I. 'a</p>
        <p>•1</p>
        <p>1.--..,.......-.- -....-...,....J</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. GORDON RIGBY, - -</p>
        <p>on of Mr. James Rigby, 140, ' Wellfield-street. Winner of the second,4lementary Schools prize. Attends Ariiley-street School.</p>
        <p>white and blue," "Death of Nelson," " Octo- 'ber," and the recitations, "All's right with the world," excellently rendered by Dorothy Wilson, as also was May Weston's contribution, " Admirals all." McSs Gandy presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>ARPLEY-STREET SCHOOL. , . The winner of the second prize in the essay shield competition was drawn from this school, and .for the presentation the large room was filled with both boys and girls. Mr. A. J. Jougy presided and said he was sorry that more interest had not been taken in the competition, but was glad to see that A.rpley- street was amongst the four which had entered for the competition had supplied one of the prize-winners. The Chairman, continuing, • said the competi- tion was for the object of stirring up patriotism amongst young people; and inciting into both boys and girls their responsibilities, responsi- bilities at -present whilst at school, and their responsibilities when they •got home, and to stimulate a ,full conception of their- duty both local and national. ,Alderman BENNETT said. that . besides being patriotic to the nation and to the State they skould have high ideals of duty to the school in which they spent a number of years, and they should try to live up tes the school's tradi- tions. . The traditions in that particular school were set up many years ago, and had been</p>
