      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>" It Was Not So."</head>
        <p>" It Was Not So."</p>
        <p>Mr. morris : The question arose a fortnight ago and they found that it was not so. The Chairman : That was an old appointment. Mr. Morris: Yes; but the resolution said "all" Assistant Overseers. The Chairman : All new ones. The Assistant Clerk: The resolution did em- body the old Assistant Overseers appointed some time ago. The Clerk had written to all the Assistant Overseers suggesting that they obtain a guarantee bond, and in most cases they had given, oa.nd in every case they had been 1n negotiated Mr. Morris thought that without fear or fav- our the resolution passed by the Board ought to be carried out. The Chairman said that the Clerk had carried out the resolution as far as he could. It was a question whether he could really compel them to be insured in a Bond Society. The Assistant Clerk: The Local Government Board had informed the Clerk that Guardians had no power to insist upon it, but it is always a good thing to get if possible. Mr. Lister Kaye.: I was once bondsman for. our parish and you have to sign an agreement The man went into a society, and ive were released from our bond. Otherwise we were bondsmen ufitil we sent our notice refusing to act further. The Chairman : There is no Assistant Over- seer acting to-day who is not covered by some- body, or some company'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>Guardian's Fix.</head>
        <p>A Guardian's Fix.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris: One of the Guardians was in an awkward position at one time, and that was the reason the question was brought to a 'head. The Assistant Cierk : I quite expect the Clerk will be in a position, at the next meeting, to say that guarantee bonds have been substituted for private bonds in all cases. Mr. 31orri5 : I am satisfied. The delegates to the North-Western Poor Law Conference held at Preston presented their report of the meeting, which took place under the presidency of Lord Richard Cavendish. Mr. Bygrave, Clerk to the Blackburn Union, read a paper on "The position of nurses and Salford dge improvements." under theonTeh ePo onßoery • osbrooke, of the , and suggested • Old-age Pensions, National Insurance, and the Poor Law, and their relation to one another." The Rev. J. P. Cort. chairman of the •Bucklow Union, read a Paper on "Education and Pauperism." • The Chairman said the discussion was a most interesting one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>Was It a Political Meeting?</head>
        <p>Was It a Political Meeting?</p>
        <p>Mr. Wood entered at some lengtl into the by li v.. e flosbrooke. details of the meeting, and in the course of the report Mr. Morris asked if pol t political in the pl meeting or otherwise. M Wy tlmt much wasdisappointed a The statements he made were most contra- dictory, and his conclusions were not logical. de Wood said readhe b wa had commenced by saying that "the granting of old-age pensions and the payments of sickness and maternity benefits would tend to raise 'the standard of living among that class Of people who were likely to come under the cognisance of the Poor Law Authorities.", He, Mr. Wood, maintained that those benefits were not being dispensed to a class of people who were likely to come under the Purview of the Guardians. They did not expect that the paupers of the future would be drawn from those who were Making provision for the future by becoming insured pEtrsons under the Act. On Page 5 of his paper he contradicted himself by saying: "It will be a very -long time before certain types of paupers vanished from our midst—the uninsured and the uninsurable Skulkers—they will always be with us!' Thei he went on to say that the Insurance Act wa, shortsighted and vindictive, because it did not allow its committees "to make arrangement, with Guardiang for the treatment of consunip- tires in Workhouse sanatoria." There were few very large Unions who might be fortunatv in possessing their own sanatoria and were abi, to deal with cases of 'phthisis, hut by far tjp• greater majority of Unions did not, and they had been faced with the difficulty of treating such cases in Infirmary ,wards, withotit any sat- isfactOry means of isolation. •</p>
        <p>Allowed to Make Arrangements. The Act clause provided sanatoria and allowed Guardians to make Arrangements with Insur- ance Committees for the treatment of Work- house patients. They would then. be relieve. of the cost of building and maintaining sana- toria out of the rates, and the bona-fide work- man would not be degraded by being placed in a Workhouse sanatorium, thus pauperising him. Another ridiculous statement which 'Mr. Fos- broOke Made was that "The Insurance • Act turning half the population into paupers for," said he, "what difference is there between, re- ceiving assistance from the Imperial Exchequer and the local rates?". That view of. thing would '.suit, the idle loafer who never did• holiest -day's work in his life. He thought he had just as much right to be kept out of the rates as the ex-Cabinet Minister, the retir(.( Army official, or any other retired! Governmen; official, but they could scarcely expect tho,-, crentlemen to look at the question from the sain standpoint. Mr. Fosbrooke had argued that flu great reduction in paupers was largely due tk the spell of good trade which .we were -enjoy- ing. - • Later on in his speech, however, he spoke of the reduction in the work of the officials from the Clerk- to the porter, and recommended a reduction in salaries, and also suggested that ! instead of closing blocks of the . Workhouses owing to the reduced number of inmates they might adapt the premises for a better classifi- cation of those with whom they had to deal. But what was the use of cutting down salaries und re-arranging the premises if the reduction in numbers was attributable to the good state of trade. According •to Mr. Fosbrooke., directly they had a season of bad trade they would have a large influx of paupers, and they would have to raise the -salaries of the officials and re- arrange the premises again. There were also other thoughts which, when analysed. showed how bit* thotight-out and illogical the paper WAS.</p>
        <p>(Fairfield , Girls' Sehool), daughter of Mr. Albert E. Bolesworth, 21, King Edward-street. Winner of the Elementary Schools Shield.</p>
        <p>• ' 4-1 • 4. • • 41° . , • • . , 55 • , , • • Nt0,.,'''7,17 21.9t52t, " 44, ' • • , . . . . I , ,</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. MARIE BOLESWORTIT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>Not What Was Wanted</head>
        <p>Not What Was Wanted</p>
        <p>Mr. Lister Kaye said he did riot think that was what they wanted from delegates. All they had heard was against Mr. Foshrooke, but what they wanted was a general outline of tli . meet- ing in the delegates' own woi'ds, The delegates were thanked for their report.</p>
        <p>Director of Education '(Mr. J. Moore Murray). Canon WILLIS congratulated the school upon their success. He thought they had a splendid record iii connection with that competition, for in 1911 they won the first prize, last year they had the second, and now, once again, they had the first prize. They lived in very interesting and important times. It Was only eight years ago since they were celebrating the centenary of the great battle of Trafalgar, and in two years' time they would be keeping up the cen- tenary of the battle of Waterloo, and they were pleased that they who were their bitterest foes at that time were now their closest friends. The great event of the battle of Trafalgar came home to him very closely, for 15 years ago he sailed through that bay, and some time ago one of his daughters passed it on her way to India. (,Cheers.) Alderman BENNETT said that he was disap- pointed with' Warrington over this prize, which had been offered now for eight years, and he was sorry to say that this year fewer scholarL had taken part than on any previous occasion. There were only four schools who competed— ' Fairfield, Arpley-street, St. Benedict's, and Bol- ton Schools—and only eight' children entered: He thought it was a little reflection on the schools of Warrington that only four thought it worth while going in for the shield. His aim in offering the prize was to bring out the point .of national duty, and, he hoped that in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>"RIDING THE HIGH hORSE."</head>
        <p>"RIDING THE HIGH hORSE."</p>
        <p>Parson and the Value of the Press</p>
        <p>to the Church.</p>
        <p>NONCONFORMIST EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>"H a. man comes across any church news in a paper he will pass it over and go on to the next murder."</p>
        <p>•'Such weir the -wcirdk of the 'Bev. Arthur Longden, who addressed a meeting of the War- rington and District Church of England Men's Society in the Patten Hall on Saturday even- ing. Mr. Longden is the -secretary of the ' society for the Northern Provinces, and his re- marks were very pointed and inspiring. The Bev. •G. C. Briggs presided over a fair • attend- ance ormen. The Rector (the Bev. Canon Willis) and other clergy were also present. The CHAIRMAN said he had just returned from the Federation Conference at Cardiff, and the layman who went with "him was Mr. Sinith, of Stockton, Heath. It was the finest and most inspiring thing of tile kind he had ever: been to. Ile thought,' however, that there were rather too • many clergy present at the Confer- ence. He would like More laymen to go, for they wanted to keep that society as muah as possible in the hands of the laity. The object , of that meeting was to make the C.E.M.S. more widely known. They wanted every man in Warrington to know all about it, for it was the finest movement in the Church of Eng- land to-day. . (Hear, hear, and applause.) ' There were in' the Warrington and District Federation eight branches, but only two, out of the six Warrington parishes had branches. That. waS 'not. as it should- be for every parish in Warrington' should have its 'branch.</p>
        <p>Neglected Church News.</p>
        <p>The Rev. ARTHUR LONGDEN gave an interest- ing address on the C.E:M.S. movement, and said that first of all he must deny what the Chairman had said with regard to the society being a layman's movement. It was the Church of England Men's Society. He was a man, and the society should be for clergy and laity.. If it was , not, he would have nothing more to do with it. If it was not binding clergy and laity together it was missing its object. (Hear, hear.) They had all kinds of tendencies to draw Men away from church, and they must organise if they were going to do any good, and if the Church was going to hold its own.</p>
        <p>The Church was holding its own, and the man who said the Church was going downhill was the man wild never looked at Church news in any newspapers. If he did happen to come across any Church items he would pass them ,wer and go on to the next murder or some- thing of that kind. They had got to conyince that man and they could not do that unless Lucy were convinced themselves, which meant !ricy had got to study. How many men in that room really took an interest in Church news, as it came out in the Press? • The reason why there was not more Church news in the weekly and other papers was largely due to Iheir own fault. • They would not give. the. information to the Press; they did not trouble themselves. Prominence was oiven to items of Nonconformist bodies because !hey gave the information sought. The Press was not averse to putting information in, but they in the Church rode the high horse, and thought they could get along by themselves, and they could not. It was not fair to the man in the -street, who read his paper, and was in- fluenced very greatly by what he read. The man who would not' read his Bible. would read his daily paper. They had been tied and bound with hard' and fast schemes 'too long. They wanted to get out of the- ruek, 'and each branch should, consider for itself and utilise that wondePful infinite power which God had given them, which was not given to the Church of England. They could do work amongst men in the workshops and on the football field. They should brace themselves up and go on With faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <p>TRAFALGAR DAY—Con iinued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <p>the old school to award_the_ prize</p>
        <p>They should try to do their duty to their town. It was hard to find 36 men out of a population of 70,000 who were prepared to allow themselves to stand for seats on the Council, but if all the children in that town—who would• be the men and women in a few years—had the right spirit, Warrington would be a worthy place in the future, and by improving Warring- ton they would improve Lancashire and Eng- land. ,</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett then presented the prize to Gordon Rigby.</p>
        <p>Mr. WALLINGTON, on behalf of the teachers and scholars, thanked Alderman Bennett, Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. Moore Murray for their presence. and said he had hoped for the first prize, and although they did not do badly, someone else did_ better. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. MOORE MURRAY further explained to the scholars that Alderman, Bennett's idea was not a high literary competition, but he desired that the boys and girls would tell him of their duty, and draw their ideal g from the lines that were chosen.</p>
        <p>The children rendered effectively the songs, Fairy elves" and "A toast—' The Navy.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>There was a large ilumber.of students present in the Gymnasium in the afternoon, when Mr.l E. S. A. Itobson the Principal, presided, alai supporting hiin were Alderman- Bennett, Mr. A. J. .Jolley and Mr. Moore Murray. The opening item was the song "Lest we forget"- -Alderman BEXNETT said that he was somewhat disappointed with the essays on that occasion, for he had fondly hoped, especially after they had a stable of Joseph Priestley presented to them by the Warrington Society - that they would have been good ,ones. The object of choosing Joseph Priestley was his connections and associations with the toWn, and because he was closely associated with science, which was taught in that school. In their essays they did not mention very much about the sfx most interesting years Priestley spent in the town, as he himself had admitted. Priestley's life was many-sided, and he wrote voluminously- on all subjects, including theology and philosophy. lie discovered oxygen in the ,air; first .. used indiarubber for erasing, and invented Soda water. Whilst in Warrington his salary was only £lOO a year, and he was living in the happiest time of his life. He made almost all his discoveries with the crudest appliances and had very little money behidd him. Whilst he was in Warring- ton he was engaged in a "Primer of Electricity." and which was printed in the town. He was a tutor' at the Warrington Academy. The pie- dominant feature of Priestley's life was his love of truth, which lie told without fear or favour. The prises wei'e then handed to the winners : Senior girls, Hilda Fitter; boys, Harold Emer- son ;- junior. .girls, M._Chidlow ; _boys, G. Morral. _</p>
        <p>M.r." A. J. Jolley, Mr. Moore- Murray, and the Principal also addressed the gathering, , the programme being interspersed with i 'patriotic songs,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>DOUBLE HONOURS.</head>
        <p>DOUBLE HONOURS.</p>
        <p>-11.crs. Bovril, Limited, were awarded the Grand Prix at the Ghent Exhibition, and also a (Pploma- for excellence ,at -the Congo Beige Exhibition, Elizabethville. This double dis- tinction, which heads a long list of previous awards, is certainly a high tribute to the excel- lence of the world-famous beef beverage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>NOTABLE 114E1410</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE 114E1410</p>
        <p>IN OPENING AND CLEANING TEXTI FIBRES.</p>
        <p>By WM. SCOTT TAGGART,</p>
        <p>M.l.Mech.E. (Manchester), Author of "Co Spinning."</p>
        <p>The textile industry is the chief combael factor of England (agriculture excepted), its influence on innumerable other indust that are bound up in it gives it an importi that no other industry can possibly attain. comparatively short and relatively weak fi of cotton come to Lancashire from every co field of the world, and emerge from the 1 factories as yarn, to be used for the finest most beautifully designed laces or the I highly finished products of the loom. whole process of transforming the slabs of pressed bale cotton into the highly finished ducts of the spindle and the loom is an exc ingly complicated mechanical prnblem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>From Bulky Fibre to Fine Yarn</head>
        <p>From Bulky Fibre to Fine Yarn</p>
        <p>Operations, successive steps, are necessar bring the cotton fibres gradually from masses down to fine thread or yarn. Two cesses are absolutely essential as prelimini! viz., the cotton must be opened up into as a condition as possible, and a quantity of ohiefly sand,. leaves, seed husks, stalks, &amp;c., be eliminated. Strong, heavy machinery quiring high power to drive, is necessar) this work, and considering the delicate ch, ter of the cotton fibres, the beating operat which both open the cotton and drive on' impurities, are unusually severe. Nothir any moment has been done to improve or 'beater blade or porcupine cylinders in action of dashing the cotton from the 1 nose or the nips of rollers. The pondo methods and high speed used are out of all portions to the requirements of the work hitherto nothing has been discovered that give better, or even'as satisfactory results. cotton starts on its career through the c, mill bruised and broken by the fearfully r treatment, and no amount of delicate atter which it afterwards receives, restores its v , The opening machines, moreover, require ' siderable attention -in adjusting their vs parts, in order to minimise the amount of I 'made. No matter how present opening cleaning machinery is set and regulated, discrimination of dirt and good fibres i tremely poor, so in order to lower the an of good fibres being driven away and beco waste, a quantity of impurities are left in this must be dealt with by the card, thus ting extra work and strain on this cla machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>Impurity Eliminated Without Waste.</head>
        <p>Impurity Eliminated Without Waste.</p>
        <p>A solution of how to open cotton fibres out the rough treatment at present in 'y and how to eliminate the impurity wi wasting fibres, would be inestimable val the spinning mill. This problem has now solved in an almost ideal manner by the p] of an English inventor, Mr. Win. Yol ,The present type of opener has been disc 2 yhe cotton is now fed into a cylindrical in the form of a slowly revolving perfc cage. A specially constructed beater occ the lower half of the/cage and runs just f any contact with the perforated covers c cage. The beater' is built up by thread'] a shaft a series of stamped out double ar fingers, the adjacent arms being set a lit advance of each other, thus forming a sei fingers that have the appearance of a 11 screw standing out from the boss. One h the beuter is 'given a right-hadd screw ,and the other half a. left-hand screw effect. • When cotton is fed into the cylindrical it comes into contact with the ends c quickly revolving, beater 'fingers, and jected to a regular beating ur buffeting 1.1 In its heavy and unopened condition it 1 stantly falling into 'contact with the 1 arms, but the beating it thus receives free, condition, abuot v gradually iad theu .which opens iteehr .permits When ih et n out into it to soof ipeesnt a ient artgetee:, cN,o, hmi ce hs wd ri hv ei ns th eo cooftt;nc nirsrerieitaco ut cylindrical space into an outlet pipe, and this point the opened and cleaned cotton ducted to the scutcher. The whole of the ing action on the cotton has 'been Perf whilst it is in the free condition in the al no other resistance than air is found nece by this method, to open thoroughly the it fibres. The opening. process by its mere tory action on the fibres, shakes out prac' every particle of dirt, which falls aw gravity ilito a suitable receptacle helo machine. ' No long fibres mix with this d a larger quantity of impurities are take and the waste of good fibres is consideral duced, and in most cases completely stor</p>
        <p>New Era For Cotton Mills. Two unusually valuable results are attained. First, the cotton is thor opened by a rapid vibratory beating given to the fibres whilst free in an air thus completely eliminating any p damage to the delicate fibres. Second! cotton is so completely opened that prac all the dirt is shaken out and falls away without any accompanying fibres . .</p>
        <p>Out of these two chief factors arise ant considerations. The waste from the ing room of a cotton mill consists of di fibres; the dirt is useless, the fibres an able. By preventing waste of fibres and ii ing the extraction of dirt, great financia will be. realised. One per cent of . waste saved will amount to an enormous amo Lancashire mills alone. If the dirt is oughly taken out in the blowing room, th ing machines .will work better, produce a product, and moreover the waste from th being free from dirt, will be onsideral hanced in value. The whole arrangeit the machine i compact, occupying les: than an owner, and requires very little It is devoid of setting or adjusting ( The production is over 30,0001 b. cleaned per week. In the opinion of the write has had an opportunity of seeing the n at work and examining the results, a n of improvement, in our cetton mills may pected.. The dust problem of the car( will be solved, and considerable econom undoubtedly be effected in operative cosi , mexhinejs in no sense in an experimenta] 1 this has long been passed. Numbers ' work, and giving a degree of satisfaction even than this brief sketch would lead expect.</p>
        <p>OVEIIWOIIK AND INSANITY</p>
        <p>Dr. !lobed Joues, Claybury, Essex, sa) too little is made of the spiritual part cation</p>
        <p>11 the asylum at Claybury scores of patients who ought not to be they had had proper moral and mental in early life. I have never seen a pure overwork in the,asylum,,but I have litit cases of overwbrk coupled with anxi anxious pupils prompted by more anxio cuts and very anxious teachers.</p>
        <p>Although the period allowed for sleer pubiic schools has been extended, still required. For children under five years _twelve hours should be allowed. and m teen years of ago the, period should be ten hours. Decayed teeth are sometin cause of sleeplessness, astigmatism al poids being additional causes. As a 11 I over, I believe that of late years ate(' been greatly underestimated. it ce:.hn Heves pain in doses far smaller than 1, intoxt al ion.</p>
