        <p>The play became very keen and fast, and lually the home team got the upper hand. mu 1 put the teams on equal terms. Excite- , Wate4, -I now. very high, and both teams played vmeerynt ht., 4,.. Qt Rigby making 'a poor clearance, .. en6ti on the ball and put the Isherwood iN. , ' j-h.us gaining the lead for the leather mot v'r; anosiikt i veni. 2 :C - ‘4511,t47c, k; ton Heath: Holmes; IsHeoartthimeners. pi Moores. Spencer and :a. .4467 i ff. oenziejsherwood and West; Lloyd, 15.1.TIolmes and Morris; Astall. - Excelsi,ei . ":: likfit O'Neil: Spencel. ,tiodi `Ahin.,..„; 'Walker, Leah, .Psitchard, Welsky owl Cim. \</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE. A DISAPPOINTING WEEK-END. After the rain we had had to flush 21C streams, and, the fine week-end, many anglers turned out fully expecting good sport, but the reports say that fish were off feed at all places. We only heard of three members doing any. thing worth mentioning. Norton, Mickle Tilt!. ford, Chester and Hartford all seemed to be off colour, and we are seriously thinking this will be a poor season to wind hp with... However, iglei.iotth uncertainty is the great draw for angling, and there is yet time and. room on the Set board for members to lift Many specials.</p>
        <p>. R Fairfield U. v. Sect Gm; e-es • odhouse; This aftetik match was played -ead. Fair- field-United: Bibby; Rlg owaidan h,d;:lti. Hamblett and Davi Glave; Bebbington, -Simcock and 5124 Bank e Quay Rem.: Chesters; Berry a.allayror;',l-44.7, Worrall and Booth; Eccleston, Einttry,. Brae. - shaw, Davies and Green. Referve, Gi H. liuslp, ton. The visitors were the first tot beconiu- dart-1 ggrous, and their forwards combinof very'.weltr, but the play of Woodhouse, at back, drove, the ball back to midfield, where the home forwai-ds. appeared to be unable to get the leather. undt,r proper control. The visitors again toot up ,the. attack, when Booth passed out to Gre*n, who made a good centre from the line. Bradshaw met the ball and drove it into the net, giving Bibby no chance. Half-time arrived with the score in favour of the visitors by one goal.to nil.</p>
        <p>I N.A; GRAND MATCH RESULTS. ilnquiries have been made as to the correct esult of the Northern Anglers' grand final rhal:-.lt. Although it has not been through the piress, it was placed on the notice board -at itgadqUarteri; for a week or more, -but for the tioneCt di those' erbq keep' records, we publish tlhi,- week' /he result ;---</p>
        <p>1-41-!dc in Carr Mil/ dam, near Si. Helens. 'Tinier ISO, to ,5.30 ; Weal. her bogutiful ; lista p:entstittfi,. mostly 30z. to lot. rorteft.</p>
        <p>I,FrdSmUlj, Salford. weight Sib. 50z., prize £3 10s. aid' gold medal £llos. 2, .L Dealt, Bolton, 41b. 6cri. £2.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the visitors laid siege to the home goal, and Eccleston, drawing Woodhouse away, put the ball across the goalmouth, where, owing to a misunderstanding between Rowlands and Bibby., Green had no difficulty in placing the ball between the posts, thus scoring the second goal for the Recs. The visitors continued to have the best of play, 'and.a round of good combination, in which all the forwards had a share, resulto in Davies scoring the third goal for the visitors. From the centre kick the home team showed up to better advantage, and a -corner was conceded by the visitors, which Collins placed behind. From the goal kick the home team again took up the attack, and after a fine individual effort by Bebbington, he put the ball past Chesters and scored the only point for the homesters. Time found Bank Quay Rees. victors by four goals against one. It was chiefly owing to the fine defensive play of Wood- house that the score was not greater against the home team.</p>
