      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>T ot 47 • lit- • • tet; 1,9 t, • ;""j „ ,tll. 4:ylp – • 1•4*„ A r ' ' f. -••• • %</head>
        <p>T ot 47 • lit- • • tet- ; 1,9 t, • ;""j „ ,tll. 4:ylp - • 1•4*„ A r ' ' f. -••• • %</p>
        <p>Warringtan Give a Hopeless Exhibition Against</p>
        <p>Barrow at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>from half-time,. when a good Passing bout by the visitors resulted in Carey scoring at the corner, the goal-kick failing. On resuming, Rylands' attacked strongly, and Barton scored a splendid try, but the same player failed with the goal-kick. A mistake by the home team's backs let in Faramond with Sherdley's second try, but again the goal-kick failed. Play at this stage was very fast, and Johnson, of Sherdley, got marching orders. Good work by Riley, who was Rylands' "star turn," gave Welsby a walk-over, Ogden's kick at goal hitting the upright. Near the close Riley kicked a goal for Sherdley from a free-kick. Final: Sherdley Athletic, eight points; Rylands' Recs. "A," six points. Rylands' Were greatly handicapped owing to the absence of Potter, their sporting captain and full-back.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' MATCH TO-DAY. The Rylands' Recs. v. Swinton Park match at Gorsey-lane to-day should be of interest. The home team will be: F. Horrocks; J. Griffiths, S. Carter, A. Horrocks and P. Morris; C. Floyd and J. Gallop; A. Stretford (captain), J. Brand- wood, J. Whittaker, H. •Morris, F. Mason and D. Slater. • .</p>
        <p>H. Morris returns to the team after an absence of six weeks, through injury in the final at Cadishead.</p>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM. Pithy Points Abaut N.U. Games.</p>
        <p>The Reserve team rested on Saturday. So did the Warrington first team., They seethed asleep in.'the Barrow match. What could the matter have been? Had they been " hoenssed"? Certainly they played disappointingly. Barrow simply danced round them. . Where was the art of passing, Warrington? Was it left in the dressing-room? "Lost, stolen; or strayed! Warrington's skill as football players." The finder will not be rewarded.</p>
        <p>There will have to he a big change.. If future matches are to be won. That is a dead snip of a certainty. The .11100 will not be available , Unless; there is an instant change. Who said that the A:100 was to beiiven To the players if they finished in the last four? Was it a prophecy, as to the future? Let us hope it won't be. correct. Get your backs into the game, Warrington. Give us a bit of the fire of a few weeks ago. You can do it if you ,choose to do.</p>
        <p>eeeeee Has anyone seen Jolley's boots? The pair he used to kick goals with. He seems to have lost that footgear. Some bad misses have thus been made. Is it true the committee arc going to Wales? What would their mission be there? Possibly to find four new threequarters. They might also have a lookout For some new half-backs. A few forwards might also be picked up. That is, if they are going cheap. Some change will have to be made..</p>
        <p>CCC C C Folk are now coming. in with Suggestions. Of course, they always do at such fillies. They know how to run a team Ever so much better than the committee. The latest suggestion is that - The half-backs should be these two Ruddick and Daintith should be the men. Others suggest A. Brown and Douglas. The threequarters, we are told, should be: Waywell, C. Brown, Renwick and Bradshaw. Another suggestion is still more startling: The , second team threequartors should be entirely annexed. •</p>
        <p>0 0 0.0 However, we can leave such a matter Safe in the hands of the committee. They have giveß the men a lecture, And things may again look up. Still Warrington badly needs strengthening. This more particularly at half-back and three- quarter. Just how to iclo it is the problem.. No amount of outside advice can Possibly help to solve the riddle. How shall we fare at Rochdale? Shall Warrington beat the stinging Hornets, Who beat the Huddersfield cracks?</p>
        <p>Interesting Games in the Warrington Le uriale</p>
        <p>and Some Surprises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>br HE Ho DEFENCE COMPLETELY COLLAPSE.</head>
        <p>br HE Ho DEFENCE COMPLETELY COLLAPSE.</p>
