        <p>BEATEN FROM THE START. 1.°41 the start it was evident that Barrow vr„ atittv qut to win. They played a ding-dong game i4.1(.°10 which completely 'upset all the; .surviv: 114,:1fe in the home side. The opening score of Ns'icrw was without seni4ation. Mitchell scored it() through the absolute weakness of the tdefence. - Lt should have been prevented. 4154„e1l matters were reversed, and we were 14,4711 red with a bit of attacking •work on the Warrington. This was of a most ragged :"IlitiPraPpy Character, but it was sufficient to '14,111° Clare to Score. Jolley failed with the 15( 'ick• The interval saw Barrow leading hy goals one try (seven points) to one try TIE* Points): It'a,lere were many who Cherished the hope that Vulgton would pull themselves together in eond portion. They did—occasionally. they did so their efforts were too weak 'Pasmodic to produce a score.</p>
        <p>OGyLE THE STRONG MAN. lifih,"3le himself was nearly equal to the four 14-a threequarters. Around each individual '4%.4;'ubet Of the quartet he made circles, and it 4111 v :at surpfiging that this young lad. who 'tbfe r eently emerged from a school team, was add two more tries. In the point ,of .ttlt!) Can show a clean pair of -heels to any -heoarrinoton sprinters. Dobson knew his lt7tWities a, fed' him most generously. /c il ards the end Warrington woke up,, and -Were' treated to a nice bout of passing 6‘'. however was far too seldom for it to be '`,ve• A kick for ,obstruction was awarded 4q(4P'llgton, but Jolley 'seemed to have left his Tit fling hoots at 'home. Of-course, he went wide !° iMark -when points were so urgently ATvt.ted- :""tt ti as left to Wayvv-ell to be the only scorer *4 h'l ome side. v a bit .of good footwork he gnie to dribble the' ball over the line'and q4Pun it in the nick of time. Jolley kicked' rst goal of the day, and Warrington retired Y beaten and disgraced by 3 goals 3 tries Doints) to 1 goal 2 tries (,ci points),</p>
        <p>,th NO COMPARISON. 'e was simply no comparison between the l 4 4 -,,•1 tarrow, largely made up of youths from tt4air\a°ol:o o eoriar and a veteran like Gifford, were seasoned side like Warring- have said, their superiority was not itti4Tl'"()lis in any one- department—it was so ' ‘ll;l;llll)e•k'evi4tieinti k gs.side•left nothing to chance. They 1L111:34 well and every man was aitrtier A U. 'lir- 1111.C' remark could not be applied Gctifford,s Some people have said that 41-.4114ts e' ) - A(3 "ere ov r but his exhibition at full-back that theory. 'reetiTl e 1.1,41f.b2,_1ted with machine-like precision. i iThe Barrow-. t I,tul --Z'S mono' iolised the scrums and made -tLW-rriagton Couple look small in com Intri- 1:111 ;,teL°lllY point in which Barrow had l not War- e a lead was in the line.. 4t,a411 the -er nearly 'l6 good in this department, sext 41,t of the 1 , et (lid wasnialededbbyytlti Nu , lome half-backs, tiaters.the etee homeble. oth HANCE FOR THE WORSE. i44 -he Plair of the home defence one is at a ';t-4tllicat th write. "Rotten" was the expres- -4,4 tli-s„ed I)3' a prominent member of the club, --ilgh the word is impolite, it about sums -j;43, - Oosition. It was, to put it more moiler- -111414 r'l t d' I,tigittilit.::: 4rnio.toctiosieeltviiilealliotiibiteiiw exhibition, and one a 'I )e repeated. ,to. het , mistakes galore,.but for. thiSn.he. toblame,as the weak three- -3' caused him to be rushed time after •t,lo,ile oYle also, with his dashes, flurried the ltise.ltstodian. All the same, Jolley has gene. 4k ""Ile• :Re is not the Jolley he was a feu •Isf 4go. • -tiv •es tiot g ball the way he for- ‘titi • ihd, i • gather the of its vim, k 4 he • s i 11:41s 4118 ili tackling has lost much &amp;f to be more reckless than 1015' e":41,,,,r1-• Why 'he should have so sud- l4-I°a'his gains .I cannot fathom, but 'lti.R 44rd fact remains that he ,is not uP to his standard. ,I,Vir P n o • :Qaltem. c-R THREEQUARTERS. a weak lot were the threequarters. e..iits;et