      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>RENWICK A FAILURE.</head>
        <p>RENWICK A FAILURE.</p>
        <p>Renwick was a failure as a half-back. He seems unable to do anything to open up the game. Nicholas was the smarter of the pair, but neither were so useful as Ruddick would have been. They lacked initiative, and when they got the ball seemed unable to realise what should be done with it. In this respect Ren- wick was the more conspicuous.' In tackling Renwick was more successful, and twice- his speed saved certain Barrow scores, but, unfortunately, that is not all which is re- quired of a half-back. It is evident, after Sat- urday's rout, that a great change will have to be made in the half-back and threequarter lines; and doubtless this will have been con- sidered carefully by the committee at their meeting to choose the side to meet Rochdale. Are there no reserve men who would justify a -trial in these positions?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCH AT ROCHDALE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCH AT ROCHDALE.</p>
        <p>4 I would' be a daring prophet to risk giving a forecast- of what will happen at Rochdale to-day. Still, if -Warrington play as the side seems capable of playing, I would venture to say that if there is a defeat it will not be a particularly heavy one. Last year's corresponding match resulted 12-6 against Warrington, and I do not. see why the match of to-day should be different from that. I would very much like to see a win, but after Saturday's performance I do not think we can expect that. Still one never knows what may happen, and with the team feeling the disgrace of last week very much we may see a marked improvement, and I hope at least they may be plucky enough to force a draw. The Warrington side has undergone a consid- erable change at the hands of the committee, who, rightly so, are anxious to see their. men win, and it is to be hoped the re-arranged team will justify expectations. C. Brown, the ex- Leigh man, has come into the threequarter line, from which. Dickenson has disappeared. Ren- wick is also an absentee. Daintith takes up this' old position at half-back, and Skelhorne is again in the pack. The side is :</p>
        <p>WpIZINGTOII. Jolley. Bradshaw, C. Brown, Davis, Waywell Nicholas, -Daintith.</p>
        <p>Cox, Chesters, Thomas, Fearnley, Skelhorne, Clare.'</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" AT HOME.*</p>
        <p>Warrington '` A" ought to give the Rochdale Reserve 'a good game at Wilderspool and the home' side should win. The team will be:—</p>
        <p>Branahall; Davies, Tranter, Dickenson and Baker; A. Brown and Ruddick; Naylor, Critch- ley, Blundell, Harmer, Boardman and Brad- shaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>DICKENSON'S BENEFIT MATCH. A Sum of £271 Paid into Bank.</head>
        <p>DICKENSON'S BENEFIT MATCH. A Sum of £271 Paid into Bank.</p>
        <p>• The accounts in connection with Dickenson's benefit maich have now been made up, Nand this shows that the veteran • has benefited tothe extent 'of £271. The gross receipts were £305 128., id., and the expenses .233 15s. 6d., leaving a balance of £271 16s. 7d. This, Mr. Bradshaw, the secre- tary, of the club, informs me has now been paid into the bank in Dickenson's name. I most heartily congratulate the Veteran on such a fine financial result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>SALFORD "A", v. RYLANDS' RECS.</head>
        <p>SALFORD "A", v. RYLANDS' RECS.</p>
        <p>At Salford. Bylaw's' losing the toss, Stret- ford kicked off. Salford attacked, but the "Wiremen" repelled the invasion and good work by Carter carried the ball to the other end, Harrison saving his charge with a dead ball. Salford attaCked again, and from a pass by Preston, Hennessey scored far out, Bevan failing at goal. Preston next scored a fine try, from which Bevan goaled. This roused Rylands', who bombarded the Salford citadel, and A. Horrocks scored, the goal-kick failing. At this point Griffiths was -carried off. Despite a man short, Rylands' again attacked, . and from a round of passing between the backs Warren scored, the same player goaling, thus making the scores level. Half-time arrived with the scores: Salford, one goal two tries (eight points); Rylands', one goal two tries - (eight points). :Salford comnienced the second half in grim earnest, but the visitors held them out. Rylands' next, through Carter. who was playing a fine 'game, attacked, and Salford were lucky to Pre-' vent a score. The game now began to get a bit rough, and Warren was injured, having to be carried off. From a .free-kick high ,up by Bevan Salford scored , far out through Cook, but --Dai" -Bevan landed a fine goal.. From a penalty Knott kicked a goal. Salford were victors by three goals three tries (15„.points) to Rylands' one goal two tries (eight points). Bevan, Harrison and Preston were the pick 'of Salford. For Rylands' Carter Horrocks, Warren and Gallop played well, Griffiths being handicapped through injury. The forwards were grandly matched.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. " A " v. SHERDLEY ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>This match, in the St. Itelens and District League, took place at Gorsey-lane, in ideal weather. Play was fairly even till five minutes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD. FIRST v. LIVERPOOL UNIVERSITY.</p>
        <p>This match . was played at Knotty Ash and provided Latchford with their third win this season. The game was by no means a good one, but the raison for this was that the ground was very bumpy.</p>
        <p>The score of 2-1 in favour of Latchford by no means represents the run of the play, for the winners were in the 'Varsity half for three- parts of the .game. On the face of it, then, it looks as though the shooting of the.visitors' for- wards was faulty. This, however, was not the case, for while this department was by no moans excellent, the small disparity in 'the scores was due principally to the fine work of the University. custodian, who was unfortunate in having-to finish On the losing side. • Latchford opened the scoring after 20 minutes' play, fine combination between Wilkinson and Minards ending in the latter scoring with a fast shot. This was the only scoring in the first half.</p>
        <p>In the second half Latchford nionopolised the play and Could do everything but finish up with the ball in the net. The ball played a lot of funny tricks and it was a great wonder the Players on both sides took it on the stick as often as they did. From a breakaway the Uni- versity gained a corner and scored ultimately from this, Bruhl 'having no chance with a fine, sharp shot. Following this, Latchford pressed continuously to the end, and about two minutes before time their, efforts were rewarded by a goal scored by Harry Fletcher., Circumstances did not permit the teams play- ing really good hockey, but on the 'Varsity. side White, the left half, and the goalkeeper ap- proached that high .standard, and Fletcher, Knight and Wilkinson, on the Latchford side, were nearest to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>Second Eleven v. Brooklands.</head>
        <p>Second Eleven v. Brooklands.</p>
        <p>The second eleven were beaten 6-3, and there was no gainsaying that Brooklands were the better team on the day's display. Latchford were unfortunate in having three play.ers off ill, but the play of Brooklands is deserving of all praise. Their forward line combined togethei perfectly and gave the Latchford backs a hot time, while they were ably supported by all their back division. They had a lead of 3-2 at the interval and this was increased by the time the whistle blew filially, to 6-3. W. Starkey. scored twice and Furness once for Latchford, while Bradshaw, in .thelf-back line, played a good game. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>Teams for To-day.</head>
        <p>Teams for To-day.</p>
        <p>The first eleven play Chester-to-day with the' following team: J. Bruhl ; W. Fletcher, C. Bil- lington ; F. W. Isherwood, J. B. Wilkinson, A. Knight; R. A. Dale, H. V. Fletcher, J. E. Pear- son, W. IT. Minards, H. R. Coltop. Train leaves Bank 'Quay Station 1.40. Umpire, Mr. F. Grounds. The second eleven play Bowdon, at Bowdon: T. F. Dixon; W. T. Eccles, C. Newbold; A. E. Bradshaw, F. Newbold, T. Banks; A. V. Star- key, J. J. McCawley, W. S. Furness, A. Marsden, D. Holt. . Traiu 'leaves - Arpley Station 1.14. Umpire, Mr. 11.-'Eccles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>PROTECTING THE – CROPS</head>
        <p>PROTECTING THE - CROPS</p>
        <p>Dr. Gordon Hewitt, the Dominion 'entc;mol6- gist, left Ottawa recently for •a visit to the field stations on the prairie and British Columbia. He•will extend his journey to Utah and Cali- fornia to study the Methods -employed there to destroy parasites. In Utah, a dangerous pest has made its appearance and threatens . the alfalfa crop. Experiments have been made for the destruction of this 'and other parasites. Dr. Hewitt will • study this new danger and the measures with whicly to combat it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH ROOT THE MEX.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH ROOT THE MEX.</p>
        <p>By 44 Linesman."</p>
        <p>centred finely, the Call going to Sidilb.lt„ YAP had not much difficulty in scoring, the thirsl, point .fori. the hoinesters. • The visitors' made'', a I brave rally, and \shots were rained upon • the home goal, when the home defenders, appealed: for "hands." The whistle did not sound, and Tipton, the visitors' inside, left, went,througli. With the ball and scored the only point for the visitors. • Taylor, Smith, Lees and Bardsley played•well for the home team.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>WARAINGTON AND DISTRICT FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>WARAINGTON AND DISTRICT FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>NOTES</p>
        <p>Latchford received a visit from Broadheath, in the North Cheshire League Competition. The home team were minus the services. of Fogg and llaspell, and had to call upon the reserves— Stubbs and Siddall--and it Speaks well .for the: club that they hate sui.th reliable players to fall back upon, as they have proved themselves to be utility men of good merit. They both played amongst the forwards in this match, and had the distinction of scoring a goal each,: and so had a big 'share in the victory obtained' for the dub.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's team paid, a visit to Heatley and had to acknowledge defeat- at the hands, of Oughtrington United. After their performance a week previous a better- result was, anticipated, and a division of points would have been seine consolation to their supporters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT 'LEAGUE. A Day of Surprises.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT 'LEAGUE. A Day of Surprises.</p>
        <p>' The Adult School had a visit from the Weston Club, and a very keen game was played in Vic- toria Park. On the form of 'recent performances of the School _team it was fully expected they would be able to extract the full points from the Weston lads, but such was not the case, as the visitors have improved considerably since their first visit to the town, and after their ! performance of last- week we shall hear ,of them doing better still. It was not`so much in their ! attack as it was in their defence that they have improved, and in Na.ncollis they have one of the best goalkeepers in the League, but Old- fields and B,urns, at back, kicked splendidly, while they received good assistance from Aspey at centre-half. Molyneux and' -Flanagan: were their best forwards. rn the Adult School team, Hunter continues- to show very good form, feed- ing his forwards with judgment and -making openings for them, which they failed to improve upon. The goal which Banner got was with a splendid shot, and enabled his side to lead at half-time. It was- during the second portion that the visitors showed up best, and had their share of the game, but it was owing to a display of bad temper on the part of Welsby that cost his team a point a few minutes before the finish, when, after making a- clearance in the goal area, he got hold of one of his opponents round the neck and threw him to the ground. The result was that a penalty kick had to be given against him, and Aspey equalised. The visitors thus gained a point which, on the run of the game, they would not have got but for this incident. . Crosfield's had Weaver. Refining Company as visitors to Fially-lane,‘, and. they sprang a sur prise upon their supporters by beating the Acton Bridge lads by :three goals to one. It may, however, be said the .visitors only- played. ten men the whole of the game, as, owing to bereavement, Aspey was not able to play, and, through , accidents, two other players _of their regular team were absent. ! The home team were best served by Jackson, Callagan, Wright, Bell- field and Kimberley. while for the visitors Lester, Evans and Woodward were the best 'players. - -</p>
        <p>The Divex Club had a visit from the Stockton Heath Club, when the game proved to be a very one-sided affair, the visitors, piling up ten goals against one by the home team. Bank Quay Recs. made the journey to Orford- lane to meet Fairfield United, and a very keen and interesting game was played. The first half was very fast, and some exciting exchanges took place, the visitors only leading by one goal to nil at half-time. It was in the sebond portion that the visitors showed up best, and, being better able to keep the pace, they put on three more goals to their credit,- while United only replied with one.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Reserve entertained the Excel- sior team, when; owing to the fine performance of the visitors in beating St. John's the previous week, the spectators were not disappointed at witnessing a very keen match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Latchford v. Broa_dheath.</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Latchford v. Broa_dheath.</p>
        <p>The above match was played at Thelwall-lane, when a good number of spectators witntssed a keen and interesting gable. The following plaTers lined up :—Latchford: Kite; Bardsley and Robinson:, Downs, Webb and. Carter; Smith. Siddall, Lees, Stubbs, and Taylor. Broadheath : Pearson; Richmond and Anderson; Constable, Ross and Quinn; P. Clegg, H. Clegg, Pearson, ,Tipton and -Hanson. The home team won .the toss 'and -Pearson set the-ball rolling, and the visitors at once made tracks for the. home goal, but the effort was nullified by the ball going out of play. The game was for a few minutes carried on without either, custodian being seriously called upon. The half-backs on both sides kept the forwards in check. A fine movement was initiated by Downs passing out to Smith on the right, who raced along the touch-line and made a grand centre, when Anderson, being pressed, miskicked the ball and turned it into the net,'Pearson being at the opposite end of his. goal. The home team thus, obtained the lead after 15 minutes' -play. From the centre-kick thee-visitore made an, advance towards the home-goal, but were driven, back by Bardsley. The visitors, smarting under the score against them, again forced their way into the home quarters, when Kite made a good save and got the ball away at the second attempt. The ball was carried to the other 'end, where Siddall was in a favourable position. He shot the ball past Pearson and notched the second , goal for the home team. Shortly after Pearson was penalised for carrying the ball, and from the free-kick Webb placed the ball into the net ! without it being played by a second player, and no goal was recorded. The home team were now having the .best of the game, and. Lees was ! very -unlucky with a grand Shot, which hit the upright, and Stubbs, meeting the rebound, shot over the bar. Pearson, in the visitors' goal,- was given plenty of work, but -was not very sure in ! his clearances. At half-time the home team were leading by two goals against none. After the interval the Latchford boys assumed the aggressive, only to be mei: with a sound defence, and the visiting forwards made a strong effort to break through ,the home defence, but could not ,get past Bardsley and Robinson, and Smith, getting possession on the right,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. For Agt. Pts. Great Sankey 4 3 0 1 22 2 7 Adult School 4 1 0 3 7 5 5 Stockton Heath 2 2 0 0 15 3 4 Weston (Runcorn) 5 1 2 2 5 12 4 Weaver RTg Co. 3 1 1 1 9 5 3 Orford Barracks .3 1 1 1 12 7 3 Crosfield's Daresbury 2 0 2 1 1 13 1 Di vex - 2 0 2 0 1 12 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Coals</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For At. Pis. Orford St.,jobn's 5 4 1 0 25 4 8 St. 13arnabas' 4 3 1 0 11 7 6 W'gton Excelsior 5;) 2016 9 6 Bank Quayltees. 3 2 1 0 8 3 1 Stockton Heath 1 1 0 2 7 2 Fairfield United 4 1 3 0 6 16 2 Fairfield 1 3 0 5 22 2 Emmanuel</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>Divex v. Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Divex v. Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Played in St. James' Field. Divex: Jones; Penfold and Pearson; Ashworth, Flute and Tymms; Roberts, Fireden, Bullock, Lightfoot and Savage. Stockton Heath Simcock ; ,Gos- ling and.Woodwd.rd; Magill, Denny and Marsh; Antrobus, Lockhart, Jones, Smith and Jack- son. Referee, Mr. C. S. Essenliigh. The visitors at once made a raid on the home goal and had hard lines in missing. Jones, in the centre, received the ball and opened the score for the visitors. The home players very rarely became dangerous, the visitors' half-back line being too good for them. Lockhart was, the most pro- minent 'of the visitors' forwards up. to the interval, being instrumental in adding three more goals with very good shots, and at half- time Stockton Heath were leading by four goals to none.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the game again was carried on chiefly in: the home haft Offside spoiled the efforts of the "Heathens," and 'gave temporary relief to the home defence. After a period of uninteresting play the "Heathens" settled down to their game, and Jones quickly added three more goals, while Smith scored twice and Magill put, one to. his account. 7. From slack play by the visitors' defence the home forwards made.' .a dashing " raid on he visitors' goal, when:Savage managed to put the ball into the net out of the reach of Siincock, and, scored the only point .for: the home team. A very one- sided game ended in favour of Stockton Heath by 10 goals to one.</p>
        <p>Adult School v. Weston, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park. Adult School : Welsby; Garnett and Spilsbury ; J. Leigh, W. Hunter and Clarke; Rhodes, Jackson, Dykes, Webb and Banner. Weston: Nancollis; Oldfields and Burns; Hazlehurst, Aspey and J. 'Smith; Moly- ne:ux, Hulse, Flanagan, Conway and Kirkham. Referee, Mr. J .Smith. Dykes started, and the visitors were compelled ,to defend. During the first 15 minutes Nancollis and his backs had a very busy time, but they kept their goal from downfall by some excellent play. After even exchanges in midfield, Molyneux sped away on the right and caused .Welsby. to handle with a long shot. At this juncture Hunter was play- ing with fine judgment for the School, feeding his forwards with good passes,. which they failed, to improve upon. • Jackson sent in a fine long shot, which hit the crossbar, but the ball was eventually, forced over the lines. Dykes and. Webb, by some good passing movements, placed to Banner when favourably situated, and he scored with a beautiful shot. The home team continued to have the best of play, but Nan- collis would not yield again, and at half-time the score was in favour of the School team by one goal to nil.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the visitors began to be aggres- sive, but failed to get past the home backs. Molyneux, on the visitors' right, was well fed by his partner, and the most danger came from this wing; while Conway and Kirkham were doing useful work.., Several attempts by Webb, Dykes and Jackson to break through individu- ally were of no avail,Aspey, at centre-half, doing some useful play in, breaking up the attack. Oldfield on several occasions kicked, rather erratic and conceded corners, but the home team. could not score. On one occasion Dykes drew the visitors' goalkeeper away from his charge, and then he kicked just wide of the post. As time was drawing near the Weston Lads played up and made several good attempts. Welsby, when holding the ball, was played by an oppon- ent, and 'foolishly threw the player down in the goal area, resulting in a penalty-kick being given against him, from which Aspey equalised. When time was called the score was one goal each.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Rec. Reserve v. Weaver Refining</p>
        <p>Company. At Folly-lane. Crosfield's Reserves: Ellison, Wright and Callagan; Scott, Kimberley and Jaundrell; Bellfield, Sparks, Beddall, Jackson and W. Pagett. Weaver Company: Mills; Woodward and Brooks; Newton,. Johnston .and Lester; Powell, Oram, A. Johnson and Bean. Referee, B. Howell. The visitors played ten men. By good passing movements the visitors worked the ball towards the home goal, when Wright, with .a huge kick, drove the players back, and , the home forwards got -possession. Kimberly tried a long shot, but was wide of the mark. The visitors, through Evans, made an advance, when Callagan conceded a corner, which proved futile. The Weaver forwards were playing splendidly, but received little sup- port from their half-backs. By good play Kim- berley set Bellfield going, and he centred finely, and Jackson, being well placed, intercepted the and shot past Mills, and opened the score for the homesters. No further ,scoring took place up to the interval.• On resuming the visitors' forwards were SOOP, prominent-, and 'Evans' shot was only, inches wide. Lester next made a,.grand effort, his shot hitting the upright.. The home team now made an attack on the visitors' goal, but Woodward was playingsa great game for Weaver at back. The ball going to Evans, he placed nicely to Lester, who this time 'made no mistake, and equalised. Nettled by this reverse, CrosfielO's made a raid to the visitors' end, where Wood- ward held them at bay for' a time. Kimberley beat Mills with a long shot, and nut his.,side ahead. 'Following some exchanges in midfield, Kimberley set Bellfield -going, and a good struggle was seen between the latter and Wood- ward. Bellfield got the ball, made for goal, and put the sphere past Mills in great style, scoring the third goal for Crosfield's. Time was called with the score: Crosfield's, three goals; Weaver, one. This was the home team's first victory in the competition, and there was much excite- ment in the home camp. s Stockton Heath v. Excelsior.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. The borne team was the first to make an attack, which Morris relieved by good tackling. Smart pass-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The above League has entered upon its ninth season and bids fair, to establish yet another record during the coming months. There is an increased membership and the majority of the teams have sot off their marks well, 'evidently intending to put up a keen struggle for the League trophy. It is pleasing to note that two of the old and successful teams have rejoined. It is, perhaps, rather early to judge, but sev- eral of the teams give evidence of possessing sound material. This is. particularly the case with Evelyn-street, who have won all their matches to date. Arpley-street and St. James' have also got together useful combinations, the defence of the latter being exceptionally strong. Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agt. Pts Evelyn Street 3 3 0 fl 14 9 6 Arpley Street 3 2 1 0 8 4 4 St. James' Heathside 4 1 4 1 4 9 3 Wycliffe.. 1 0 0 1 1 1 1 Parochial 2 0 1 1 2 3 1 St. Bamabas' 2 0 2 0 I 7 0 Penketh 1 0 1 0 0 8 0 Beamont St. Ann's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>SHOTS AT THE GOAL.</head>
        <p>SHOTS AT THE GOAL.</p>
        <p>Stubbs and Siddall are the reserve players for Latchford.</p>
        <p>They scored a goal each against'Broadheath. Very useful players for the club to fall back uPan. _ _ _</p>
        <p>'lt is goals that count and win matches. Lees on Saturday, for the first match, missed scoring. . .</p>
        <p>_ He was unfortunate; a rattling shot from him hit the upright. Kite was very safe in the Latchford goal. Smith and Taylor did some fine work on the extreme wings. Stockton Heath Club made the Divex team a present of ten goals to their debit account. Jones was top scorer with four as his share. Lockhart was runner-up with three, all good shots.</p>
        <p>Savage scored the only 'point for the Divex. Simcock only had one goal kick during the second half.</p>
        <p>Adult School team got a surprise from the Weston Club.</p>
        <p>The,Weston Lads have improved almost be- yond recognition. • Banner's goal was a beauty. Hunter played a good game,-but he is showing signs of the veteran stage. Welsby was to blame for the Adult School pnly adding one instead of two points to 'their record. Crosfield's scored their first victory in the W. and D.L. last Saturday. • - • The Weaver Refining Company team were their victims.</p>
        <p>The visitors only played ten men. Evans gave a fine exhibition at centre-for, ward.</p>
        <p>Dan Woodward is a fuie defender.</p>
        <p>Woodhouse, of Fairfield United, is one of the best backs in the Second Division of tht League. Bebbington played.a forceful game for United. Eccleston scored with a rattling shot, which beat Bibby all the way. . A few weeks ago Orford St. John's beat Stock- ton Heath 6-0.</p>
        <p>Two weeks ago Excelsior beat St. John's 4-3. Last Saturday, with a .weakened team, Stock- ton Heath beat Excelsior 2-1. How's this for form? . . .</p>
        <p>Holm(); in goal was a- treat to watch. - Boyd would not turn out for the, Heathens. He wishes to transfer his affections to the Ex- celsiors. .</p>
        <p>The Excelsfors were leading at half-time by 1-0. The Heathens scored twice in the second half. Watson's goal was a clinker. There are rumours of protest in the air. The Excelsior players took their beating very badly. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH'S CUP TIE.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH'S CUP TIE.</p>
        <p>- - - - The most important game in our area to-day will be the math at Stockton Heath with Northenden Amateurs in the second round of the Cheshire Amateur Cup ties. The Visitors • come with a• very good, reputation, and hold a good position in the Lancashire and Cheshire Amateur League, having played three matches and won them, scoring eleven goals against four. The following players will play for , Stockton Heath: Simcock; Gosling and. Woodward; Magill, Denny and Marsh; Parr, 'Lockhart, Jones, W. Taylor - and Jackson. Reserve, -T. Smith. Referee, Mr. Bell.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Warrington and District League. Weaver V. Barracks; R. Rule. Divex v. Crosfield's; U. IT. Rushton. Fairfield v. Bank Quay Rees; A. HOlt. St. Barnabas' V. _Orford St. John's; M. J. Kite,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>TO-DAY'S TEAMS.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TEAMS.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute (v. Latchford Athletic, at Victoria Park)..—T. Knight; J. Ireland and J. Ashton; H. Wainwright, J. Hatton (capt.) and J. O'Brien; J. Isherwood, T. Hayes, T. Pot-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>NORTON-</head>
        <p>NORTON-</p>
        <p>The canal here fished very badly, titer allow— ing" for the breeze that was blowing along the- canal. This made fibe fishing impossible. It. was .entirely off, aid members who took fish were very few. This, water seems to be very- puzzling this season. One day a wierriber•may go and get a fair catch, and when a few get to know of it, off they goy. and the result is almost nil. It is one of the angling mysteries, wherfi the fine Boz. roach he got to that the Run- corn club turned in: There are none Warring- ton .way that we have heard of. Some are of the opinion that they will do better in cold weather. We doubt, it, as Norkon has had its bet returns during July and .August. How- ever, it is worth a trial, especially for the angler who has baits to suit the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>MICKLE TB AFFORD.</head>
        <p>MICKLE TB AFFORD.</p>
        <p>The Gowy was visited by a fair crowd 4Di Warringtonians, but results for the number were very bad. Some anglers never saw a flat fish all day, and .those who visitea-tittle Bar- row end said the stream was in fine order, -find the colour just to their liking.' Everything ap- peared...right except the fish, and they were Conspicuous by their absence. Those a who fished the lower reaches fared very little better, with the exception of Messrs. Dryers; Hewitt and Clare. Dryers had a- mixed catch of dace- and 'roach, about a .dozen fish in all. His largest roach was 101 oz., and his largest dace soi. 13dr. Hewitt also had roach and' dace, about a dozen sizeable fish, but the weight we are unable to say, as they were not weighed. . _</p>
        <p>Will members 91eaSe note that when fishing the Gowy for daCe to compete for club specials they. Must .be caught in Northern Anglers' waters?</p>
        <p>Mr. Clare was the only other member who had anything. lie landed a pike of about 41b. Some members who were on the scene at the time say he went in the stream for him. Whether there is any truth in this or not, we are not quite prepared to say, but we heard of him asking-, the loan of a suit from our Mickle Trafford member. If this is so,. we would advise Mr. Clare to read his rules carefully, as we fail to and where any member is allowed to go in and hook them on, and then explain to other mem- bers it was an accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>OTHER PLACES.</head>
        <p>OTHER PLACES.</p>
        <p>Chester, both the Dee and canal, were off, and nothing of any note was done; the same applies ..to Hartford but several members are paying the 'latter place another visit tins week- end. Perhaps they are going to have a go .at the chub near the Viaduct?</p>
        <p>The set board practically remains the same. A list Will be 'published neNt week.</p>
      </div>
