      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <p>NEW STOCK OF DAINTY DOWN QUILTS, CUSHIONS, DRAPERIES OF EVERY DESCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>LACE AND CASEMENT CURTAINS A SPECIALITY.</p>
        <p>.-:..:.4.:•.:.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.4..:</p>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly me4ing of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning, Mr. E. L. Bent i presid- ing. •</p>
        <p>-NURSES, CERTIFICATED AND UNCERTI-</p>
        <p>FICATED.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the House Committee stated that two applications had be-en received for the position:of staff nurse, but in view of the fact that neither of the applicants was fully trained, it was recommended that the Services of the temporary nurse be retained for six months, for at the end of that time the probationary nurses would have gained the necessary experience to enable them to cope with the work. Mr. MATilkal asked • that the Minute be sent back, as the House Committee had not quite understood what the superintendent, nurse wanted, in regard to the stall nurses.' They could reconsider the matter. .11r. BROOIZES seCOllded. • The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: It would be desir- able to fully discuss the matter. Mr. 4ATHER : I understand that the Super- intendent does not want certificated nurses, but nurses trained in an institution who would take the position of probationary nurse. Mrs. SIMPSON: The House Committee would have done very well. . •</p>
        <p>The Rev. A.M. MITCHELL: I th,ought a certifi- cated nurse was always so desirable. (Hear, hear.). ,But instead Of that the matron or head nurse desires a, nurse who is not certifica;ed to assist her.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHEU : She wants a • nurse who will work.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: That is rather a slur on the certificated nurst,.s. Mr. MATHER : No no. The Rev. A. M. MiTcHiLL : I Would have a cer- tificated nurse.-</p>
        <p>Mr. MATkEIL : Ask these two nurses to appear. The Rev. A. M. :11rrcinir, : Has the -doctor: been ,consulted about this, or is there a doetor to consult?</p>
        <p>, Mr. MATHER : Refer the minute back to the house Committee; it is wasting °time. •</p>
        <p>The minute was referred back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>NIGHT PORTER'S DEATH</head>
        <p>NIGHT PORTER'S DEATH</p>
        <p>The Board regretted the death of Mr. Dyson, the night porter at the institution, who was for- 'tnerly in the .Police Force. 'A vote of sympathy was passed with the widow. Mr. BROOKES gave notice of motion that he would move the appointnlent of Mr. Edwards to the vacant position.</p>
        <p>It was also decided that the man should ap. pear before the Bard.</p>
        <p>FURNITURE FOR THE IMBECILE BLOCK.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote with re- gard to the new imbecile and epileptic block. They excluded from consideration the £750 for furniture, as that expenditure could not be paid out of a SO years' loan. The CLERK: They don't allow more than 10 to 15 years in regard to furniture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>ANONYMOUS LETTER FROM THE HAVEN."</head>
        <p>ANONYMOUS LETTER FROM THE HAVEN."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL produced a post-card, which, he said, was a complaint that three</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <p>IL Jachon-Moss,</p>
        <p>(LOND.), QUALIFIED OPHTHALMIC OPTICIAN.</p>
        <p>MONDAYS, TUESDAYS an'd FRIDAYS, at 25, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON BEATH. 10 a.m. .to 8 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <p>PLATE,</p>
        <p>/ CUTLERY, LAMPS, COAL VASES, FIRESIDE</p>
        <p>BRASSES AND CURBS, CHINA, GLASS AND EVERY HOUSEHOLD REQUISITE</p>
        <p>children who were chargeable to the rates oughi not to be in the Padgate Homes. SEVERAL MEMBERS: IS it signed? The Rev. A. M. :N.lrrcnem : I don't know the; it is. It is headed "The Haven," Warrington. Mr. MATHER : It is for the officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>PAY FOR EXTRA WORK.</head>
        <p>PAY FOR EXTRA WORK.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the Finance Com- mittee it was decided that Mr. Gidman (reliev- ing officer) be granted t 2(), in recogniHon of the, extra work he had done for the past six months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>NEW RELIEVING OFFICER.</head>
        <p>NEW RELIEVING OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Mr. iNIATif ER gave notice that he would propose that Mr. Jones, the schoolmaster of the Padgate Homes, be appointed relieving officer in place of Mr. Peacock (resigned). .He was of the opinion that servants of the Board ought to be given a.chance_Of improvement.</p>
        <p>The CLERK: The Local Government Board laid down that a man had to be a qualified person, having • had Poor Law experience, or a man having held a similar position to. that of reliev- ing officer. Many local people .whom they had appointed had. made excellent officers. He hoped, however, that Mr. Jones would be ap- pointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>MR. COOKE'S ILLNESS.</head>
        <p>MR. COOKE'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN explained that Mr. Cooke had been absent from the meetings for six months, owing to illness. After six months' absenoe a member ceased to be a -Guardian, but the Guar- dians could extend the time.</p>
        <p>'The time was extended, and the Board hoped Mr. Cooke would speedily recover. , WORTH THE MONEY. •</p>
        <p>The CLERK explained that the larton Union to which they granted .1:20 at the last meeting, had won their case. It was worth the money expended, for. they would also .get the. benefit of the decision if occasion arose.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. JOLLEY the following resolution was, unanimously carried : `` That owing to Mrs. Constable having been compelled to relinquish the office of porteress at the Workhouse by reason of her ,serious illness, and having in mind the consciendous manner in which she has at all times performed her duties, the Local Government Bo'ard he asked to sanc- tion a proposal of the Guardians to add ten years to her actual period of service for the purpose of computing her superannuation allow- anee." . • -</p>
        <p>More Than He Could Do.</p>
        <p>"1)0 yOl.l iiiiikpal Call support lay ilaughte in the style to which she is iteensloined?" aslcei the cautious father.</p>
        <p>I know _I rail," said Sapleigh</p>
        <p>"Thank heaven, I've found you," cried the cautious father, embracing hint, "you can do more than I can."</p>
        <p>A - Fearful Outlook. "My old barber has left the city." "You seem .very regretful." " Yes ; he had been tilling to sell me .a bottle of hair- tonic for the past 15 years. Now I shall have to start the battle all over with a new man."</p>
        <p>PEVERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <head>WHEN THE BULLETS ARE---</head>
        <p>WHEN THE BULLETS ARE---</p>
        <p>The Billets for Ulster Refugees in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S SCHEME.</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S SCHEME.</p>
        <p>It st't2llL. ~iyonge that‘ Mr. Hareld Smith, who is to dump ou a large scale Ulster women an,l children into Warrington, when the bullets begin to whistle round the patriots of the North, goes everywhere else than Warrington to tell of his scheme. Possibly the Member thinks</p>
        <p>that Warringtonians have got to have thein, And need not be told what they should do. Although in the borough a week, he never said a word about his scheme, but he had something to say about it at a Conservative meetirig at Accrington. Ile then moved : "That in the event of the loyalists of North-East Ulster being compelled to resist by force of arms the authority of the proposed Irish Parliament, it is desirable that steps be taken to make provi- sion for the women and children of those who are engaged in actively defending their liberties. and that for the furtherance of this object it is desirable to seek the co-operation and assist- ance of the Women's-Unionist Associations and Primrose League Habitations." Mr. Harold Smith said he was quite prepared, if the resolution was carried, to receive the criticism and the attacks of opponen4. They (the Unionists) would be told that they were revolutionaries and treason mongers. But he was satisfied that the quarrel 'was a just one. The proposals of the Government were unjust and wicked, and they could afford to ignore and to treat with contempt the criticism Of Liberal opporients. Mr.'- Smith described what has</p>
        <p>already been done in Warrington to house hun- dreds of :women and children from Ulster in case there was trouble there, though he hoped there would not be. lie suggested that through- out Lancashire they should start a great organ- isation whereby they could make an offer to the men of Ulster who were fighting to defend their liberties, that they in England would protect their women and children when that critical time arrived. IL was impossible to carry such a scheme through without some sort of organ- isation.</p>
        <p>"SERIOUSNESS OF THE SITUATION." Colonel Rutherford, M.P. (Darwen), sec- onded, though he hoped there would be no occasion for such provision to be made. The resolution adopted, Mr. Harold Smith thanked the delegates, and said an immediate atteinpt would be made to get into touch with the secretaries of the various women's organisa- tions. He suggested that it was the duty of the delegates to inform their branches of what had taken place at that meeting, and to impress upon their members the seriousness of the situation. Ile expected that a general meeting for the county would shortly be called, and an Executive Council appointed to make the neces- sary arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>AMBULANCE WORK.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE WORK.</p>
        <p>Keen Competition for the Crosfield</p>
        <p>Challenge Shield.</p>
        <p>411 CZ:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>WARRINGTON VICTORY.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON VICTORY.</p>
        <p>The fifteenth annual competition for the Crostield Challenge Cup, open to No. 4 District of the ,St. John Ambulance Brig Tide, took place at CroSfleld's Works on Saturday.. -.There- were eight entrips from florwich (holders). Great Cen- tral Railway' (Manchester), Damen, Walkden, ilindley, Warrington (Headquarters Division), Preston and Nelson. The judges were Assist- ant-Commissioner A. L. Garnett, Burnley, who gave the practical test, and Dr. Clegg, Accring- ton, who took the questions.</p>
        <p>Test: "The patient, who has been in a motor smash, is suffering from a simple fracture of the right clavicle, and his right metacarpal bones and the ,plialanges of his left foot are crushed. Treat and convey to ,hospital." Result: • 1, Warrington (Corporal Warriner and Privates Kenning,. Walker, Willmore and Ambrose); 2, Horwich ; 3, Preston, 156, after tying several times with 155 against Nelson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>DeNolion to Duty.</head>
        <p>DeNolion to Duty.</p>
        <p>Colonel TRIMBLE, Deputy Commissioner, No. 4 District, in calling upon Capt. Crosfield, said that formerly that gentleman held a position on the staff of the district, which, to the general regret, he had to relinquish because of his busi- ness engagements and his dutieS as an ener- getic Territorial officer. Although they . had lost his services they had not lost his 'friend- ship, or that of the firm he represented. (Hear. hear.) There was no trophy which created greater interest in No. 4 District than the Cros- field trophy, which did much to maintain inter- est in ambulance work. This maintained.inter- est prevented ambulance men forgetting most of what they had ,learned, and so becoming the most dangerous members of. society. They thanked Capt. Crostield and his firm for their continued interest and kindness, which was very much appreciated. • (Applause.) He wished' to thank tile members of the Division— officers, men and nursing sisters—for their de- votion to duty last .Iply, which enabled him to cover with lirst-aiders the whole route ,of 300 miles traversed by file King and Queen on their visit to Lancashire. That work was done by 3,000 members at. their own cost, and without being a charge to the extent of a single six- pence upon the public furls. This redounded their- credit as an organisation, and to 'their unselfishness and Christianity as individuals. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Value of Brigade Work.</p>
        <p>Capt. CROSFIELD, who said he was pleased</p>
        <p>think, that the Warrington Corps of the St. qui Ambulance Brigade was in such a healthy state, thanks to Capt. Waring, the chief super- intendent, and Mr. Bose, the superintendent of the Crosfleld Division, congratulated the com- peting teams upon their general appearance. Ile wished the Territorials .were present to see their turn-out and their smart salute. The frightful chapter of accidents they had bad during the week should make everybody realis,. more !han ever the great value of the work done by the St. John Ambulance Brigade. The value of, their work as a brigade was that ii kept the men together as a real asset tp thc country, and prevented them, as Colonel Trimble had said, from becoming dangerous by getting out Of practice. •(Applause.) On the motion of ..Capt. WAnING, seconded by Supt. IlosE, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Capt. Croslield and the judges.</p>
        <p>Among others present were First affi. eer Wil- loins and Second Officer Spann, Headquarters Division; and First Officer Cawloy, Crostield Division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Headquarters Division was held recently. Chief Superintend- ent Waring presiding. Privates Wilson and Broadhurst were appointed on.. the committee. it was decided to change the drill night from Wednesday to Friday. A vote of condolence was passed/ to the family of the late Private John Kirby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0323" n="art0323">
        <head>PRIZE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>As will be seen by an advertisement in an- other column, a prize .competition on a rather large scale is being conducted by the proprietors of Dr. Sands' Liver Seeds. There is no en- trance fee, and the competition is one of skill— the writing of catch phrases or testimonials to advertise these well-known pills. The condi- tions should be carefully read.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <p>Furnishing Problem -Solved-</p>
        <p>h</p>
        <p>U</p>
        <p>TESTERRY IN THE DISTRICTS.</p>
        <p>ORFORD SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The second day's sale at Orford Schools was opened yesterday by Mrs. Cyril Gough. The Rev. G. Vale-Owen presided, and Mrs. Gough wished the sale 'every success. A vote of thanks was accorded Mrs. Gough, on the pro- position of Mr. Brandreth. Mr. F. White Iva?' the sevetary for the social in the evening. The takings for the two days realised about £4O.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>• • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>_ The Foreign Missionary meeting took place on Thursday evening at the above church. speaker was the Rev. .Griffin, of West Afritl4" The 4v. J. Gunnell reported that .1:62 had heel' contributed, an increase of ..{3. 104.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH RETRIEVER PRIZE-</p>
        <p>WINNER. Mr. V. V. Davies, Birehdale, Stockton Heath, won first 'prize for the best team of retrievers and three thirds with retrievers at the Kennel Club • Show, Crystal Palace, this week. Mr. Davies has been very successful this season with kis dogs. SERIOUS, LYMM CHARGE DISMISSED" At the Chester Assizes, John Gibbon, House, Lymm, was charged with a 01'1°1 Hence against Emma Davies, aged 13 years an" 10 months, who resided with her parents Itt Higher-lane, Lymm. Mr. Trevor F. Lloyd, i!1. structed by Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington. represented the defendant. After a long hear- ing the jury returned a verdict of "Not guilty, and the defendant was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON MUTUAL IMPROVE-</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON MUTUAL IMPROVE-</p>
        <p>MENT SOCIETY. On Thursday the Oughtrington Mutual Im- provement Society held their second meeting, when a lecture was given by Mr. Parrott uu "Lovely Lucerne," illustrated by numerous slides from excellent photographs which had been taken byP himself on various visits to Switzerland. '</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL MAN DROWNED. An inquest was held last evening at the Dog and Dart Hotel, Grappenhall, on John Antro- bus, Bradshaw-lane, found in the Ship Canal /4 Latchford. The evidence pointed to the sole position that he fell into the water while cros: ing the Locks during the fog on Wednesdo3 morning. Verdict: "Accidentally drowned."</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. W. CRAMPTOK. Impressive Service at Hill Cliffe. The funeral of Mr. W. Crampton, Whitefield- road, Stockton Heath, whose death is recorded .under the Stockton Heath news, took place yes- terday afternoon at the Hill Chile Churchyard- There was a large assembly in the church, where" a short and impressive service was held, in which the Revs. W. Cooper, H. 11. Williiias' Buglass, Chessum. and Waddell took part, the Rev. Mr. Buglass pronouncing the cOntinittsl sentences at the graveside. . The chief mourners. were: Mrs. W. Crampton (widow), •Mrs. T. R. Dootson, Mrs. H. R. Wil" limns, Mr_ T. Crampton, Mr. I'. P. Crampto Mrs. P. P. Crampton, Mr. T. .I.lnsworth, Mis't Ann Crampton, Mr.. T. R. Dootson, Mrs. John Williams, Mrs. Davenport, Mr. John Evans, --Ntr' and Mrs. Whitley, Mr. J. Griffiths, Mr. Dawson. Williams, Mr. P. Crampton, Mr. Calvert, Fey' W. Cooper (Leigh), rev. 11. B. Williams (Cob')n Bay), Rev. Buglass (Manchester), Rev. Chessato:. (Warrington), and Rev. J. W. Waddell (Liver" pool). r , The 'following were amongst those who- set, floral tributes: The Widow, Mr. and Mrs. flthS, Mr. J. and Mrs. Wilkinson and Miss 1:04,-, worth, ." Helen and Tom," "Mabel and Eric,i Mrs. E. Williams and family, Friends t,'ll,ti 'tenants, Sankey Bridges; "Paul, Annie nil` family," Mr. and Mrs. T. Elwell, Mr. and 31q; T. L. Miliett, Mr. and Mrs. A. E. Hoyle, "Louisl and Mrs. Parker Stevens (Stretford), Rev. ion Mrs. H. E. Williams, J. A. Williams, " Cousin Alice" uhd -Crampton (Stretford).</p>
