        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH .OF WARRINGTON. EDUCATION COMMITTEE.• . • Applications are invited for the position of STOItES CLERK. Commencing salary at the rate of .2120 Per annum. Candidates must have had a. suitable training in an Educational Publishing Firm, or in the Stock and Stores work of a local education authority, or have some equivalent qualifica- tion for the position, and be fully competent in this branch of the work of an Education Office. Canvassing members of the Committee, either personally or otherwise, will be considered a disqualification. Applications, which must be made in candi- dates' own handwriting, on the official form, to be obtained from the undersigned, must be ,sent, accompanied by copies of not more than three recent testimonials, to the Director of Education, so as to be received by him not later than noon on Saturday, Ist November, 1913. J. MOORE AIURRAY, Secretary and Director. Education Office, Sankey-street, Warrington, • 20th October, 1913. OUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. SHOPS- ACT, 1912. NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN that the Town Council propose to make a Closing. Order for BOOT and SHOE SHOPS in the following area, viz. :—All Streets and other public places in. the centre of the Borough being within the area bounded by and including the following Streets: Crosfield-street, Parker-street, Wilson Patten- street, Arpley-road, Bridge-street, Mersey-street, Howard-place, Buttermarket-street, Scotland road, Horsemarket-street, Winwick-street to Winwick-road, Tanners'-lane, Bewsey-street, Froghall-lane to'Crosfield-street, fixing the hours at which such shops shall be closed as follows: Monday and Tuesday and Thursday? p.m. Wednesday 8 p.m. Friday 9 p.m. Saturday 10 p.m. provided that on the Market Day iMmediately preceding Christmas Day and on the Thursday immediately preceding Good Friday and the Warrington " Walking " Day, the hour at which such shops shall be closed for serving customers shall be 10 p.m. Any person wishing to make objection to the Order or suggestions as to the provisions of the Order should address a statement in writ- ing to the undersigned before the first day of December next. - NOTICE IS FURTHER GIVEN that the occupier of any shop to be affected by the Order may, on presenting himself at the Health Office, Garven House. Sankey-street, . during office .hours on any day' before the aforesaid date, verify that his name, address and trade are duly entered in the Register of Shops. Dated this 18th day of October. 1913.. J. LYON WHITTLE, • Town Clerk. Town Hall. Warrington.</p>
        <p>HENsH,A,LL FOR Id PP fr''') GrvA or% FVE LATCHFORD t. Councillor Henshall's programme includes an enlarged Victoria Park, Lake for Boating and Swimming and Golf Links for the Working Classes.</p>
        <p>Of my native town, and particularly for the benefit of Whitecross Ward. As a member of the Health, the Electricity and Tramways, the Sanitary Works, and the Paving and Sewerage Committees, the work of these im- portant departments has claimed my special atten- tion. The question of Ptiblic Health is one of vital importance to both rich and.poor, and, if re-elected, I shall continue to support all measures that tend to improve the health of the community. I have given whole-hearted support to the efforts of the Health Committee to diminish the death-rate. With the object of more effectively dealing with tuberculosis patients undet the Insurance Act, an extension of the Sankey Sanatorium hgs been decided upon. The inauguration of the Motor 'Bus Service, which I have advocated for some years, supplies a long-felt want in the district, and the success which has attended it justifies the provision of additional At the meeting of the Town Council in June last, I brought forward a resolution urging the com- mencement of laying out the Recreation Ground in Lovely-lane for adults and children, and the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee has since agreed to establish two bowling greens, tennis courts, and a recreation ground for children on this site. The remaining portion of the land has been approved by the Board of Education for the proposed Ele- mentary School, and as school accommodation in the Ward is strictly limited, the time has now arrived when an additional one should be erected. This is the fourth time I have had the privilege of soliciting your support, and I venture to hone that my experience and practical training in the al life may claim a renewal of dyouutire sc6onf fidMucnei.cipl have endeavoured to represent all, irrespective of any creed or party, and, if re-elected, that same spirit will always animate me in. my Municipal en work. I am, yours sineerelY, JOSEPH HAWTHORN. 3, Folly-lane. Warrington, October 7th. ]913.</p>
